window.allQuestionsData=window.allQuestionsData||{};window.allQuestionsData["terraform"]=[{"id":"lq_48","level":"junior","question":"What is Terraform state and why is it important?","tags":["TERRAFORM","State","Fundamentals"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\"><strong>Terraform state</strong> (<code>terraform.tfstate</code>) is the authoritative single source of truth mapping your declarative HCL configuration to real-world cloud resources. Stored as a JSON document, it records resource metadata, private IDs, and dependency relationships so Terraform knows what exists, what needs updating, and what to destroy.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Resource Mapping:</strong> Maps high-level configuration identifiers (e.g. <code>aws_instance.web</code>) to cloud-assigned physical attributes (e.g. <code>i-0a1b2c3d4e5f6g7h8</code>).</li>\n  <li><strong>Metadata & Performance Cache:</strong> Stores resource dependencies and attribute caches so Terraform can calculate execution dependency graphs without querying hundreds of cloud API endpoints on every plan.</li>\n  <li><strong>Drift Detection Baseline:</strong> Serves as the comparison baseline between your local code, the prior applied state, and live cloud infrastructure.</li>\n  <li><strong>Locking & Concurrency:</strong> Remote backends like AWS DynamoDB or Terraform Cloud lock the state during writes to prevent race conditions.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Inspect the local or remote state file safely\nterraform state list\n\n# View exact attributes stored for a specific resource\nterraform state show aws_instance.web\n\n# Pull remote state locally for emergency inspection\nterraform state pull &gt; state_backup.json</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Storing state in Git: Storing state files in version control is a catastrophic security vulnerability. State files contain unencrypted plaintext secrets (database passwords, TLS private keys, IAM tokens) returned by cloud provider APIs.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Interview delivery: 'Terraform state is the bridge between declared intent and cloud reality. Without state, Terraform cannot know if a resource already exists or needs creation, leading to duplicate resource provisioning or orphaned infrastructure.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"lq_49","level":"mid","question":"How do you manage Terraform state in a team environment?","tags":["TERRAFORM","State","RemoteBackend","Collaboration"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">In a collaborative engineering team, sharing local state files via Git or email leads to state divergence, concurrent overwrite corruption, and credential leaks. Teams manage state using a <strong>Remote Backend</strong> (e.g., AWS S3 + DynamoDB, HashiCorp Terraform Cloud, or Google Cloud Storage) with automated <strong>distributed state locking</strong>, encryption-at-rest, and strict IAM access controls.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Centralized Remote Storage:</strong> S3 or GCS bucket stores the authoritative <code>.tfstate</code> file with Server-Side Encryption (AES-256 or AWS KMS) and S3 Object Versioning enabled.</li>\n  <li><strong>Distributed State Locking:</strong> DynamoDB table (holding a primary key <code>LockID</code>) grants an exclusive lock during <code>terraform plan</code> and <code>apply</code>. Simultaneous runs by teammates receive an immediate lock error.</li>\n  <li><strong>State File Versioning:</strong> S3 Bucket Versioning enables instant point-in-time recovery if an apply corrupts state.</li>\n  <li><strong>Access Segmentation:</strong> IAM policies restrict access to state buckets; CI/CD pipelines assume least-privilege IAM roles.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>terraform {\n  required_version = \"&gt;= 1.5.0\"\n  backend \"s3\" {\n    bucket         = \"corp-terraform-state-prod\"\n    key            = \"networking/vpc/terraform.tfstate\"\n    region         = \"us-east-1\"\n    encrypt        = true\n    dynamodb_table = \"terraform-state-locks\"\n  }\n}</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Deleting the DynamoDB Lock Table: Without a configured locking mechanism, two engineers or CI pipelines applying simultaneously will overwrite each other's state file, permanently corrupting the state and leaving cloud resources orphaned.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Production rule of thumb: 'Structure state files by blast radius: separate state files for foundation networking, database clusters, and application services. Never run an entire company out of a single monolithic state file.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"lq_50","level":"junior","question":"What is the difference between terraform plan and terraform apply?","tags":["TERRAFORM","CLI","Workflow"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\"><code>terraform plan</code> is a dry-run preview command that compares desired code with live cloud state, while <code>terraform apply</code> is the execution command that issues API calls to provision, modify, or destroy real infrastructure.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>terraform plan:</strong> Refreshes live state by querying cloud APIs, compares live attributes with your local <code>.tf</code> files, computes a deterministic Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG) of necessary modifications (+ create, ~ in-place update, - destroy, -/+ replace), and writes an optional binary execution plan file (<code>-out=tfplan</code>).</li>\n  <li><strong>terraform apply:</strong> Acquires state lock, executes the proposed graph by sending authenticated HTTP REST requests to cloud APIs, and updates the state file immediately upon each successful resource modification.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Production Best Practice: Generate deterministic plan file and apply it\nterraform plan -out=tfplan -input=false\n\n# Apply strictly the pre-approved plan file in CI/CD pipeline\nterraform apply -input=false tfplan</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Running unconstrained 'terraform apply -auto-approve' in production: If live cloud drift occurred between code review and deployment, an unconstrained apply may unexpectedly recreate databases or load balancers with downtime. Always apply a saved <code>tfplan</code> file.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Staff SRE workflow: 'In CI/CD, always separate plan and apply into two stages: the PR step runs <code>terraform plan -out=tfplan</code>, and upon manual approval or merge, the CD runner executes <code>terraform apply tfplan</code> to guarantee zero drift between preview and execution.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"lq_51","level":"mid","question":"Explain Terraform modules and why you'd use them.","tags":["TERRAFORM","Modules","Architecture","DRY"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">A <strong>Terraform Module</strong> is a container for multiple resources that are used together. Modules are the foundational building block for writing DRY (Don't Repeat Yourself), reusable, testable, and standardized infrastructure components across environments and teams.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Root Module vs. Child Module:</strong> The root module consists of the resources defined in the current working directory. A child module is invoked from a root or another child module using the <code>module</code> block.</li>\n  <li><strong>Module Interface Contract:</strong> Inputs are defined via <code>variable</code> blocks (with types, descriptions, and validations); outputs are defined via <code>output</code> blocks (exposing IDs, endpoints, and ARNs). Resources inside a module are private; callers can only consume declared outputs.</li>\n  <li><strong>Source Versioning:</strong> Modules can be sourced from Git tags, Terraform Registry, S3, or local directories. Using Git tags (e.g. <code>?ref=v2.1.0</code>) ensures predictable, immutable version upgrades.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Invoking a versioned VPC module from a Git repository\nmodule \"production_vpc\" {\n  source  = \"git::https://github.com/terraform-aws-modules/terraform-aws-vpc.git?ref=v5.1.0\"\n\n  name = \"prod-vpc\"\n  cidr = \"10.0.0.0/16\"\n\n  azs             = [\"us-east-1a\", \"us-east-1b\", \"us-east-1c\"]\n  private_subnets = [\"10.0.1.0/24\", \"10.0.2.0/24\", \"10.0.3.0/24\"]\n  public_subnets  = [\"10.0.101.0/24\", \"10.0.102.0/24\", \"10.0.103.0/24\"]\n\n  enable_nat_gateway = true\n  single_nat_gateway = false\n}</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Sourcing modules from Git branches instead of tags: Using <code>?ref=main</code> means any commit pushed to the module repository will immediately break or alter future plans in consumer projects. Always pin modules to semantic version tags (e.g. <code>?ref=v1.4.2</code>).</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Design principle: 'A great Terraform module behaves like a well-designed microservice: it has sensible defaults, exposes clear validation guards on inputs, documents all outputs, and abstracts infrastructure complexity from application developers.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"lq_52","level":"senior","question":"What is state drift and how do you handle it?","tags":["TERRAFORM","Drift","Troubleshooting","Operations"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\"><strong>State Drift</strong> occurs when the real-world configuration of cloud infrastructure diverges from what is recorded in the Terraform state file or declared in HCL code. Drift is typically caused by out-of-band manual changes made in cloud web consoles, emergency hotfixes, automated auto-scaling actions, or rogue external scripts.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Detection Mechanics:</strong> During <code>terraform plan</code>, Terraform refreshes live state by issuing read API calls to cloud providers. It computes a diff between live cloud reality, current state, and desired HCL code.</li>\n  <li><strong>Reconciliation Paths:</strong></li>\n  <li>1. <em>Revert Drift (Enforce Code):</em> Running <code>terraform apply</code> will overwrite the out-of-band change, bringing cloud infrastructure back in line with declared HCL.</li>\n  <li>2. <em>Adopt Drift (Update Code):</em> Update your HCL files to match the newly desired cloud configuration, then run <code>terraform apply</code> (resulting in zero changes).</li>\n  <li>3. <em>Ignore Benign Drift:</em> Use the <code>lifecycle { ignore_changes = [...] }</code> block for attributes intentionally managed dynamically (e.g., ASG desired capacity, EC2 tags, image tags).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Run a plan strictly to detect drift without refreshing state writes\nterraform plan -refresh-only\n\n# Apply refresh-only to safely update state file to match cloud reality\nterraform apply -refresh-only\n\n# Continuous drift detection pipeline command (exits with code 2 if drift found)\nterraform plan -detailed-exitcode -no-color</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Overwriting emergency manual hotfixes: If an engineer manually resized an RDS instance at 2 AM to survive a DDoS attack, a naive morning CI/CD pipeline running <code>terraform apply</code> without drift inspection will shrink the database back down, triggering a secondary outage.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Enterprise posture: 'Prevent drift architecturally: strip IAM write permissions to the AWS Console in production, enforce all changes through GitOps pipelines, and run scheduled daily <code>terraform plan -detailed-exitcode</code> cron jobs that page oncall when unauthorized drift is detected.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"lq_53","level":"senior","question":"How do you handle secrets in Terraform code?","tags":["TERRAFORM","Security","Secrets","Vault"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Managing secrets in Terraform requires protecting credentials across three distinct vectors: in <strong>version control (code)</strong>, in <strong>execution memory (variables)</strong>, and in the <strong>state file (.tfstate)</strong>. Plaintext passwords or API tokens must never appear in <code>.tf</code> files.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>1. In Code (Eliminating Hardcoded Secrets):</strong> Mark variables as <code>sensitive = true</code> to prevent them from being printed to CLI stdout, log files, or CI output. Inject values dynamically via environment variables (<code>TF_VAR_db_password</code>) or external secret managers (HashiCorp Vault, AWS Secrets Manager).</li>\n  <li><strong>2. Dynamic Data Sources:</strong> Retrieve secrets at runtime using provider data blocks rather than passing them through input variables.</li>\n  <li><strong>3. In State Files:</strong> Even with <code>sensitive = true</code>, Terraform records all resource attributes in plaintext inside <code>terraform.tfstate</code>. Store state in encrypted remote S3/GCS buckets with KMS CMK encryption and strict IAM boundary policies. Terraform 1.7+ also added native state encryption features.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>variable \"db_password\" {\n  type        = string\n  sensitive   = true\n  description = \"Master database password\"\n}\n\n# Fetch secrets dynamically from AWS Secrets Manager\ndata \"aws_secretsmanager_secret_version\" \"db_secret\" {\n  secret_id = \"prod/app/db\"\n}\n\nlocals {\n  db_creds = jsondecode(data.aws_secretsmanager_secret_version.db_secret.secret_string)\n}\n\nresource \"aws_db_instance\" \"database\" {\n  allocated_storage = 20\n  engine            = \"postgres\"\n  username          = local.db_creds.username\n  password          = local.db_creds.password\n  instance_class    = \"db.t4g.medium\"\n}</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Assuming 'sensitive = true' encrypts the state file: Marking a variable sensitive only redacts it from terminal logs (<code>(sensitive value)</code>). It does NOT encrypt the value in <code>terraform.tfstate</code>. Anyone with read access to the state bucket can view the secret in plain text.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Modern pattern: 'Avoid generating static passwords in Terraform altogether. Use IAM Database Authentication (AWS RDS IAM auth), AWS KMS envelope encryption, or Vault dynamic short-lived credentials that expire automatically in 15 minutes.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"lq_54","level":"junior","question":"What's the difference between Terraform and Ansible?","tags":["TERRAFORM","Ansible","Architecture","IaC"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">While both are Infrastructure as Code (IaC) tools, <strong>Terraform</strong> is an <strong>Infrastructure Provisioning</strong> tool (creating VPCs, subnets, VMs, databases, load balancers), whereas <strong>Ansible</strong> is a <strong>Configuration Management</strong> tool (installing packages, configuring OS users, updating systemd services, patching software).</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Paradigm:</strong> Terraform is strictly <strong>Declarative</strong> (you declare desired end state; Terraform calculates graph diffs). Ansible is primarily <strong>Procedural / Task-based</strong> (tasks execute sequentially from top to bottom, though modules are idempotent).</li>\n  <li><strong>State Tracking:</strong> Terraform maintains a state file (<code>.tfstate</code>) to track resource lifecycles and detect drift. Ansible is <strong>stateless</strong> (discovers system state dynamically via SSH and Python facts).</li>\n  <li><strong>Lifecycle Control:</strong> Terraform excels at resource deletion and replacement. Ansible does not natively track what it previously created, making lifecycle destruction complex.</li>\n  <li><strong>Connectivity:</strong> Terraform speaks to cloud vendor REST APIs. Ansible connects to live operating systems via SSH or WinRM.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Complementary workflow in DevOps pipelines:\n# Step 1: Terraform provisions AWS EC2 instances &amp; Security Groups\nterraform apply -auto-approve\n\n# Step 2: Ansible configures the provisioned OS via dynamic inventory\nansible-playbook -i aws_ec2.yaml configure_webserver.yml</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Using Ansible to provision complex cloud topologies: While Ansible has cloud modules, managing VPCs and subnets without state tracking makes rollbacks, dependency graphs, and drift detection fragile and error-prone.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Interview soundbite: 'Terraform builds the house (servers, network, storage); Ansible furnishes the house (installs Nginx, tunes sysctl, configures users). Combine them: use Packer and Terraform to bake immutable AMIs, or hand off Terraform outputs to Ansible dynamic inventories.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"lq_55","level":"mid","question":"Explain the purpose of terraform.tfstate locking.","tags":["TERRAFORM","State","Locking","Concurrency"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\"><strong>State Locking</strong> is a concurrency protection mechanism that prevents multiple users or CI/CD pipelines from executing operations on the same Terraform state file simultaneously. Without state locking, concurrent applies cause race conditions that corrupt the state file or result in orphaned, unmanaged cloud resources.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Lock Acquisition:</strong> When an engineer or pipeline runs <code>terraform plan</code> or <code>apply</code>, Terraform contacts the backend.</li>\n  <li><strong>Lock Registration:</strong> The backend generates a lock record (e.g. creating an item in AWS DynamoDB with a <code>LockID</code>, timestamp, hostname, and operation type).</li>\n  <li><strong>Lock Rejection:</strong> Any subsequent concurrent <code>terraform</code> invocation attempting to access that state checks the lock table, sees the active lock, and halts immediately with an error displaying who holds the lock.</li>\n  <li><strong>Automatic Release:</strong> Upon plan/apply completion (success or graceful failure), Terraform releases the lock automatically.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Emergency force-unlock command (use ONLY if process is confirmed dead)\n# Get LockID from error message:\nterraform force-unlock 12345678-abcd-ef01-2345-6789abcdef01</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Force-unlocking while a job is actively running: If a Jenkins job hangs and you run <code>terraform force-unlock</code> while the process is actually still writing to the cloud API, the next apply will corrupt the state file beyond repair. Always verify the PID or build job is terminated before force-unlocking.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Architecture requirement: 'When configuring an AWS S3 backend, always specify <code>dynamodb_table = \"terraform-locks\"</code>. S3 alone does NOT support distributed locking; DynamoDB is mandatory to prevent concurrent state corruption.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"lq_56","level":"mid","question":"What is a Terraform workspace and when would you use one?","tags":["TERRAFORM","Workspaces","State","Environments"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">A <strong>Terraform Workspace</strong> allows a single HCL configuration directory to manage multiple separate state files. Originally called 'environments', workspaces isolate state while reusing the same code files, making it easy to test branch deployments or ephemeral developer sandbox environments.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>State Isolation:</strong> Every backend creates a dedicated state sub-path for each workspace (e.g. <code>env:/dev/terraform.tfstate</code> and <code>env:/stage/terraform.tfstate</code>). The default workspace is named <code>default</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Dynamic HCL Interpolation:</strong> The current workspace name is accessible inside HCL code via the <code>terraform.workspace</code> variable.</li>\n  <li><strong>Best Use Cases:</strong> Ephemeral feature-branch preview environments, testing multi-region variants with identical configurations.</li>\n  <li><strong>When NOT to Use Workspaces:</strong> Separating long-lived Dev vs. Prod environments. Workspaces share identical credentials, provider configurations, and backend buckets, creating an unacceptable blast radius between production and staging.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Create and switch between workspaces\nterraform workspace new dev\nterraform workspace new prod\nterraform workspace select dev\n\n# List active workspaces\nterraform workspace list</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Using workspaces for production isolation: In production enterprise architectures, dev and prod should reside in completely separate AWS accounts with distinct IAM boundaries and KMS keys. Workspaces share backend configurations, meaning a typo in the dev workspace could destroy prod resources.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Staff recommendation: 'Use workspaces for ephemeral developer testing and short-lived PR preview environments. For permanent long-lived environments (Dev, Staging, Prod), use directory separation or Terragrunt with dedicated AWS accounts.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"lq_57","level":"senior","question":"How do you safely destroy or recreate a specific resource without affecting others?","tags":["TERRAFORM","Operations","Targeting","Replace"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">In production, you often need to forcefully recreate a degraded virtual machine or destroy a single misconfigured resource without tearing down adjacent databases, VPCs, or networking. Modern Terraform provides the <code>-replace</code> flag and targeted destruction options to safely operate on individual resource addresses.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Recreating Resources (-replace):</strong> Introduced in Terraform 0.15.2+ (replacing legacy <code>terraform taint</code>). Instructs Terraform during planning to treat the specified resource as scheduled for destruction and immediate recreation. Safe because it does NOT mutate state on disk until you approve the plan.</li>\n  <li><strong>Targeted Destruction (-target):</strong> Scopes planning and destruction to a specific resource address (and its upstream dependencies). <code>terraform destroy -target=aws_instance.test</code> destroys only that single resource.</li>\n  <li><strong>State Removal (terraform state rm):</strong> Removes a resource from the state file entirely without deleting it from the cloud, allowing it to become unmanaged or adopted elsewhere.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Modern safe recreation of a single EC2 instance in next plan/apply\nterraform plan -replace=\"aws_instance.web\" -out=tfplan\nterraform apply tfplan\n\n# Safely destroy strictly a single test cache instance\nterraform destroy -target=\"aws_elasticache_cluster.redis_test\"\n\n# Remove resource from state without deleting physical infrastructure\nterraform state rm aws_s3_bucket.legacy_logs</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Over-reliance on -target in CI/CD: The <code>-target</code> flag bypasses normal dependency graph calculations. If you target a resource that has downstream dependencies, you risk leaving dependent resources in an inconsistent or broken state. Never use <code>-target</code> in automated CI pipelines.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Interview distinction: 'Never use <code>terraform taint</code> in modern environments—taint immediately mutates state before planning. Always use <code>terraform apply -replace=\"resource_address\"</code> to preview the exact replacement diff before committing changes.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf200_1","level":"junior","question":"What is Terraform, how does its declarative engine work, and why is Infrastructure as Code (IaC) essential?","tags":["TERRAFORM","IaC","Fundamentals"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\"><strong>Terraform</strong> by HashiCorp is the industry-standard, cloud-agnostic <strong>Infrastructure as Code (IaC)</strong> tool. It enables engineers to define, provision, version, and manage physical and cloud infrastructure using a human-readable, declarative language called <strong>HashiCorp Configuration Language (HCL)</strong>.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Declarative Engine:</strong> You write code describing the desired target state (e.g., 'I want a VPC with 3 subnets'). Terraform automatically computes the execution steps required to achieve that state, unlike imperative scripting where you must code every step.</li>\n  <li><strong>Graph Engine:</strong> Constructs a Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG) of all resources, automatically ordering operations and provisioning independent resources in parallel.</li>\n  <li><strong>Provider Plugin Architecture:</strong> Terraform Core communicates with cloud plugins (AWS, Azure, GCP, Kubernetes) over local gRPC RPCs.</li>\n  <li><strong>Why IaC is Essential:</strong> Eliminates manual click-ops, prevents configuration drift, provides automated audit trails in Git, enables automated peer reviews, and allows entire datacenters to be stamped out in minutes.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>terraform {\n  required_providers {\n    aws = {\n      source  = \"hashicorp/aws\"\n      version = \"~&gt; 5.0\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nprovider \"aws\" {\n  region = \"us-east-1\"\n}\n\nresource \"aws_s3_bucket\" \"audit_logs\" {\n  bucket = \"enterprise-audit-logs-2026\"\n\n  lifecycle {\n    prevent_destroy = true\n  }\n}</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Treating Terraform like a general-purpose programming language: Terraform is an IaC tool, not an application framework. Avoid building overly clever, deeply nested dynamic logic that makes infrastructure impossible to read or debug during production outages.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Staff SRE principle: 'Infrastructure as Code is not just about automation—it is about immutability, auditability, and reproducibility. If it is not defined in Git, it does not exist in production.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf200_2","level":"junior","question":"What are the main features of Terraform?","tags":["TERRAFORM","Features","Architecture"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Terraform's dominance in modern cloud engineering stems from core architectural features designed for enterprise-scale infrastructure management: declarative configuration, execution plans, resource dependency graphs, change automation, state management, and provider extensibility.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Infrastructure as Code (HCL):</strong> Declarative, readable syntax with support for variables, locals, expressions, and functions.</li>\n  <li><strong>Execution Plans (Dry-Run):</strong> Separates the 'plan' phase from the 'apply' phase, giving operators full visibility into additions, deletions, and modifications before touching production.</li>\n  <li><strong>Resource Dependency Graph:</strong> Builds an internal DAG to execute independent actions concurrently while strictly respecting explicit (<code>depends_on</code>) and implicit dependencies.</li>\n  <li><strong>State Management:</strong> Maps declared resources to cloud provider IDs and tracks configuration drift.</li>\n  <li><strong>Modular Architecture:</strong> Enables component reuse through versioned modules.</li>\n  <li><strong>Provider Ecosystem:</strong> Over 3,000 community and official providers managing clouds, SaaS tools, DNS, databases, and monitoring.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Visualize the resource dependency graph in DOT format\nterraform graph | dot -Tpng &gt; graph.png\n\n# Format all code recursively to HashiCorp standard\nterraform fmt -recursive\n\n# Validate syntax and configuration integrity\nterraform validate</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Ignoring provider updates: Not locking provider versions with <code>required_providers</code> allows breaking provider upgrades to run automatically during CI/CD, causing unexpected plan diffs and API deprecation failures.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Key distinction: 'Terraform is cloud-agnostic in syntax, but NOT cloud-agnostic in code. You use HCL for all clouds, but an <code>aws_instance</code> is fundamentally distinct from an <code>azurerm_virtual_machine</code>.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf200_3","level":"junior","question":"What is the difference between Terraform and other IaC tools like Ansible, Puppet, Chef, and CloudFormation?","tags":["TERRAFORM","Comparison","IaC"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">IaC tools fall into two distinct domains: <strong>Infrastructure Provisioning</strong> (bootstrapping clouds and services) and <strong>Configuration Management</strong> (configuring OS internals and packages). While tools like AWS CloudFormation are proprietary and cloud-locked, Terraform provides a unified, cross-cloud declarative provisioning framework.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Terraform vs. CloudFormation:</strong> CloudFormation is native to AWS and uses JSON/YAML; Terraform is multi-cloud (AWS, GCP, Azure, Datadog, GitHub) and uses modern HCL with cleaner syntax, faster planning, and a massive provider ecosystem.</li>\n  <li><strong>Terraform vs. Ansible/Puppet/Chef:</strong> Terraform provisions foundational infrastructure (VPCs, RDS, EKS). Ansible/Puppet/Chef manage software inside virtual machines. Terraform uses state files to detect drift and manage deletion; Ansible is stateless. Terraform is declarative (HCL); Ansible executes sequential tasks in YAML.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Terraform Multi-Cloud Harmony: AWS VPC + Cloudflare DNS in one configuration\nresource \"aws_lb\" \"web\" {\n  name               = \"web-alb\"\n  internal           = false\n  load_balancer_type = \"application\"\n  subnets            = [aws_subnet.pub_a.id, aws_subnet.pub_b.id]\n}\n\nresource \"cloudflare_record\" \"web\" {\n  zone_id = var.cloudflare_zone_id\n  name    = \"api\"\n  value   = aws_lb.web.dns_name\n  type    = \"CNAME\"\n  proxied = true\n}</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Using CloudFormation in multi-cloud or hybrid environments: Relying solely on cloud-native tools locks teams into vendor silos, requiring separate tools for AWS, Kubernetes, Cloudflare, and Datadog.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Executive summary: 'Use Terraform to provision all infrastructure and SaaS services. Use Ansible or cloud-init for VM OS bootstrapping. Use Kubernetes/Helm for container orchestration.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf200_4","level":"junior","question":"What is a provider in Terraform?","tags":["TERRAFORM","Providers","Architecture"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">A <strong>Provider</strong> is a plugin that Terraform uses to translate HCL resource declarations into real API calls against external platforms (e.g. AWS, Azure, Google Cloud, GitHub, Kubernetes, Datadog). Written in Go, providers run as standalone gRPC binaries managed by Terraform Core.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>gRPC Plugin Architecture:</strong> Terraform Core handles language parsing, state management, and dependency graphs. It delegates all resource CRUD operations to the provider plugin over local RPC.</li>\n  <li><strong>Provider Declaration:</strong> Configured in the <code>terraform { required_providers { ... } }</code> block, specifying source registry and version constraints.</li>\n  <li><strong>Provider Configuration:</strong> Passes credentials, regions, and default tags to the plugin.</li>\n  <li><strong>Aliases:</strong> Multiple provider blocks can be declared with aliases to deploy resources across multiple AWS regions or accounts simultaneously.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>terraform {\n  required_providers {\n    aws = {\n      source  = \"hashicorp/aws\"\n      version = \"~&gt; 5.20.0\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n\n# Primary Region Provider\nprovider \"aws\" {\n  region = \"us-east-1\"\n  default_tags {\n    tags = { Environment = \"Production\", ManagedBy = \"Terraform\" }\n  }\n}\n\n# Secondary Region Provider via Alias\nprovider \"aws\" {\n  alias  = \"west\"\n  region = \"us-west-2\"\n}</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Missing required_providers version locks: If you omit provider version constraints, running <code>terraform init</code> on a new machine or CI runner downloads the latest major version, which may introduce breaking changes and break deployments.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Security best practice: 'Never hardcode AWS access keys or secret keys in provider blocks. Always leverage ambient credentials: IAM instance roles, environment variables (AWS_REGION), or AWS Vault.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf200_5","level":"mid","question":"How does Terraform manage dependencies?","tags":["TERRAFORM","Dependencies","Graph","Architecture"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Terraform uses a <strong>Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG)</strong> to map, order, and execute all infrastructure operations. Terraform distinguishes between <strong>Implicit Dependencies</strong> (inferred automatically from attribute references) and <strong>Explicit Dependencies</strong> (declared via <code>depends_on</code>).</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Implicit Dependencies (Recommended):</strong> Occurs naturally when an attribute of one resource references an attribute or output of another (e.g. <code>subnet_id = aws_subnet.public.id</code>). Enables maximum concurrency: independent branches of the graph are created in parallel.</li>\n  <li><strong>Explicit Dependencies (depends_on):</strong> Declared when a dependency exists in real life but is not visible in attribute references (e.g. an EKS node group requires an attached IAM role or VPC Internet Gateway).</li>\n  <li><strong>Cycle Detection:</strong> If Resource A depends on B, and B depends on A, Terraform immediately halts with a <code>Cycle error</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>resource \"aws_iam_role_policy_attachment\" \"eks_worker_policy\" {\n  role       = aws_iam_role.worker.name\n  policy_arn = \"arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/AmazonEKSWorkerNodePolicy\"\n}\n\nresource \"aws_eks_node_group\" \"nodes\" {\n  cluster_name    = aws_eks_cluster.main.name\n  node_group_name = \"standard-nodes\"\n  node_role_arn   = aws_iam_role.worker.arn\n  subnet_ids      = [aws_subnet.private_a.id, aws_subnet.private_b.id]\n\n  # Explicit dependency: Ensure IAM policies are attached BEFORE building nodes\n  depends_on = [\n    aws_iam_role_policy_attachment.eks_worker_policy\n  ]\n}</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Overusing 'depends_on': Placing <code>depends_on</code> indiscriminately across modules or resources serializes execution, stripping Terraform of its ability to provision resources concurrently and ballooning deploy times from 5 minutes to 45 minutes.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Rule of thumb: 'Always rely on implicit dependencies via direct attribute references. Reserve <code>depends_on</code> exclusively for hidden dependencies like IAM policy attachments, VPC Internet Gateways, and S3 bucket policies.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf200_6","level":"junior","question":"What is a state file in Terraform and what data does it store?","tags":["TERRAFORM","State","JSON"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">A <strong>Terraform State File</strong> (<code>terraform.tfstate</code>) is a structured JSON file that records the exact mapping between resources declared in your HCL code and the real physical or virtual objects created in the cloud. It is the database of record for Terraform.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>State Schema Format:</strong> Formatted as standard JSON containing a format version, serial counter, lineage UUID, and a <code>resources</code> array.</li>\n  <li><strong>Resource Attributes Stored:</strong></li>\n  <li>• <em>Type & Name:</em> e.g. <code>aws_instance</code> and <code>web_server</code>.</li>\n  <li>• <em>Provider:</em> e.g. <code>provider[\"registry.terraform.io/hashicorp/aws\"]</code>.</li>\n  <li>• <em>Instances Array:</em> Contains the unique cloud ID (e.g. <code>i-0123456789abcdef0</code>), private IP, public IP, ARN, security group IDs, attached EBS block devices, and all attributes returned by the API.</li>\n  <li>• <em>Dependencies:</em> List of upstream resource IDs this instance depends on.</li>\n  <li>• <em>Sensitive Values:</em> Plaintext values for passwords, private keys, and tokens.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> JSON</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>{\n  \"version\": 4,\n  \"terraform_version\": \"1.6.0\",\n  \"serial\": 12,\n  \"lineage\": \"9c12a34b-56cd-78ef-90ab-cdef12345678\",\n  \"resources\": [\n    {\n      \"mode\": \"managed\",\n      \"type\": \"aws_vpc\",\n      \"name\": \"main\",\n      \"provider\": \"provider[\\\"registry.terraform.io/hashicorp/aws\\\"]\",\n      \"instances\": [\n        {\n          \"schema_version\": 1,\n          \"attributes\": {\n            \"arn\": \"arn:aws:ec2:us-east-1:123456789012:vpc/vpc-0123456789abcdef0\",\n            \"cidr_block\": \"10.0.0.0/16\",\n            \"id\": \"vpc-0123456789abcdef0\"\n          }\n        }\n      ]\n    }\n  ]\n}</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Manually editing the state JSON: Never open <code>terraform.tfstate</code> in a text editor to hand-edit values. Modifying the JSON directly often invalidates the serial number or schema, permanently corrupting the state file. Always use <code>terraform state</code> CLI subcommands.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Architecture note: 'The state file serial number increments with every state-mutating operation. If Terraform finds a state file with a lower serial number than expected, it aborts immediately to protect against stale state overwrites.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf200_7","level":"mid","question":"Why is it important to manage and backup the state file in Terraform?","tags":["TERRAFORM","DisasterRecovery","State","Operations"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">If you lose your Terraform state file, Terraform completely loses its memory of all previously provisioned infrastructure. While the physical cloud resources continue running, Terraform now believes nothing exists—running <code>terraform apply</code> will either fail due to resource naming collisions or recreate duplicate resources, causing catastrophic production outages.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>The Cost of Lost State:</strong> Without state, updating or destroying resources requires painstakingly importing hundreds of individual resources back into state via <code>terraform import</code>, which can take days of manual toil.</li>\n  <li><strong>Automated State Backups:</strong> Local runs automatically create a <code>terraform.tfstate.backup</code> file prior to state writes.</li>\n  <li><strong>Cloud S3 Versioning:</strong> Enabling S3 Object Versioning on remote state buckets retains every historical iteration of the state file, allowing instant rollback in seconds.</li>\n  <li><strong>MFA Delete & Object Lock:</strong> Enforce S3 MFA Delete and S3 Object Lock to prevent accidental deletion or ransomware destruction of state files.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># AWS CLI: Enable S3 Versioning on the Terraform State Bucket\naws s3api put-bucket-versioning \\\n    --bucket corp-terraform-state-prod \\\n    --versioning-configuration Status=Enabled\n\n# Emergency recovery: Restore previous state version using AWS CLI\naws s3api get-object \\\n    --bucket corp-terraform-state-prod \\\n    --key networking/vpc/terraform.tfstate \\\n    --version-id \"abc123VersionIdXYZ\" \\\n    recovered_terraform.tfstate\n\n# Push recovered state back to the remote backend\nterraform state push recovered_terraform.tfstate</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Forgetting S3 Lifecycle noncurrent version expiration: Storing thousands of historical state files without lifecycle policies can result in tens of thousands of versions accumulating, creating unnecessary storage costs. Retain 90 days of noncurrent versions.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Disaster Recovery drill: 'Include Terraform State Recovery in your team's quarterly DR game days: practice restoring a corrupted state file from S3 object versioning and running <code>terraform plan</code> to verify zero drift.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf200_8","level":"senior","question":"How can you secure the state file in Terraform with encryption and IAM?","tags":["TERRAFORM","Security","Encryption","IAM","Compliance"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Because Terraform state files contain sensitive unencrypted outputs (database passwords, private keys, IAM tokens, TLS certs), securing state is one of the highest-priority DevSecOps responsibilities. Protection requires a multi-layered defense: <strong>encryption at rest</strong>, <strong>encryption in transit</strong>, <strong>least-privilege IAM policies</strong>, and <strong>access logging</strong>.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>1. Encryption at Rest (KMS CMK):</strong> Encrypt the S3 state bucket with a dedicated AWS KMS Customer Managed Key (CMK). Access to the state file requires dual permissions: both S3 read/write access AND KMS <code>kms:Decrypt</code> / <code>kms:GenerateDataKey</code> permissions.</li>\n  <li><strong>2. Encryption in Transit:</strong> Enforce TLS 1.2+ using an S3 Bucket Policy that denies <code>aws:SecureTransport = false</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>3. Least-Privilege IAM Boundary:</strong> Block all human developer access to state buckets; grant access exclusively to automated CI/CD IAM execution roles (e.g. GitHub Actions OIDC role).</li>\n  <li><strong>4. S3 Block Public Access:</strong> Enforce S3 Account-level Block Public Access to prevent state leakage.</li>\n  <li><strong>5. Native State Encryption (Terraform 1.7+):</strong> Use native client-side encryption blocks to encrypt state before it leaves the runner.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># S3 Bucket Policy enforcing TLS encryption and blocking unencrypted uploads\nresource \"aws_s3_bucket_policy\" \"state_security\" {\n  bucket = aws_s3_bucket.state.id\n\n  policy = jsonencode({\n    Version = \"2012-10-17\"\n    Statement = [\n      {\n        Sid       = \"EnforceTLSRequestsOnly\"\n        Effect    = \"Deny\"\n        Principal = \"*\"\n        Action    = \"s3:*\"\n        Resource = [\n          aws_s3_bucket.state.arn,\n          \"\\${aws_s3_bucket.state.arn}/*\"\n        ]\n        Condition = {\n          Bool = { \"aws:SecureTransport\" = \"false\" }\n        }\n      }\n    ]\n  })\n}</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Granting AmazonS3FullAccess to developers: Developers with broad S3 read access can download the state file and inspect production database master passwords and TLS private keys in plaintext. Always isolate state buckets in a dedicated, locked-down AWS Security/Core account.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Staff SRE defense: 'Adopt GitHub Actions OIDC federated credentials combined with AWS KMS key policies. Only the CI runner executing on the main branch can request KMS Decrypt permissions for the production state file.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf200_9","level":"mid","question":"What are root and child modules in Terraform, and how do they communicate?","tags":["TERRAFORM","Modules","Architecture","DesignPatterns"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Every Terraform configuration has at least one module known as the <strong>Root Module</strong>, which consists of the <code>.tf</code> files in the working directory where <code>terraform</code> commands are run. A <strong>Child Module</strong> is any module invoked by the root or another module using a <code>module</code> block.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Encapsulation Boundary:</strong> Child modules are black boxes. Resources inside a child module cannot directly read variables or resources from the parent, and the parent cannot reach into the child to read private attributes.</li>\n  <li><strong>Communication Protocol:</strong></li>\n  <li>• <em>Downstream Input (Parent -> Child):</em> The parent passes values into the child module by declaring arguments that match the child's <code>variable</code> definitions.</li>\n  <li>• <em>Upstream Output (Child -> Parent):</em> The child exposes computed attributes via <code>output</code> blocks. The parent accesses them using the syntax: <code>module.<MODULE_NAME>.<OUTPUT_NAME></code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Meta-Arguments on Modules:</strong> Modern Terraform supports <code>for_each</code>, <code>count</code>, <code>depends_on</code>, and <code>providers</code> directly on <code>module</code> blocks.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># In Root Module (main.tf): Invoking Child Module and consuming its output\nmodule \"vpc\" {\n  source     = \"./modules/vpc\"\n  cidr_block = \"10.0.0.0/16\"\n}\n\nmodule \"app_cluster\" {\n  source    = \"./modules/ecs_cluster\"\n  # Consuming output exposed by the VPC child module\n  subnet_ids = module.vpc.private_subnet_ids\n  vpc_id     = module.vpc.vpc_id\n}\n\n# In Child Module (./modules/vpc/outputs.tf):\n# output \"private_subnet_ids\" {\n#   value       = aws_subnet.private[*].id\n#   description = \"List of private subnet IDs\"\n# }</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Circular module dependencies: If Module A takes an output from Module B, and Module B attempts to take an output from Module A, Terraform halts with a cyclic dependency error. Decouple them by passing shared resources at the root module level.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Architecture tip: 'Keep child modules thin, opinionated, and focused on a single logical domain (e.g. <code>terraform-aws-eks-cluster</code>). Never build monolithic 'god modules' that provision VPCs, databases, and DNS all in a single child module.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf200_10","level":"junior","question":"What is the purpose of the terraform init command and what operations does it perform?","tags":["TERRAFORM","CLI","Init","Plugins"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\"><code>terraform init</code> is the first command that must be executed in any new or cloned Terraform working directory. It initializes the working directory, downloads required provider plugins, configures backend storage, and downloads child modules.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Four Core Operations of terraform init:</strong></li>\n  <li>1. <strong>Backend Initialization:</strong> Connects to the remote backend (e.g. S3, GCS, Terraform Cloud), creates backend state structures, and migrates state if backend settings changed (<code>-migrate-state</code>).</li>\n  <li>2. <strong>Child Module Installation:</strong> Downloads remote modules (from Git, Registry, S3) into the local hidden <code>.terraform/modules/</code> directory.</li>\n  <li>3. <strong>Provider Plugin Installation:</strong> Reads <code>required_providers</code>, consults the Dependency Lock File (<code>.terraform.lock.hcl</code>), downloads provider binaries from registries, verifies checksums, and caches them in <code>.terraform/providers/</code>.</li>\n  <li>4. <strong>Lock File Management:</strong> Generates or updates <code>.terraform.lock.hcl</code> to ensure cryptographic reproducibility across machines.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Standard initialization in fresh working directory\nterraform init\n\n# Reconfigure backend settings (e.g. switching S3 bucket or key)\nterraform init -reconfigure\n\n# Upgrade provider plugins to the latest matching version\nterraform init -upgrade\n\n# CI/CD optimization: Use shared provider cache directory\nexport TF_PLUGIN_CACHE_DIR=\"$HOME/.terraform.d/plugin-cache\"\nterraform init -input=false</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Forgetting to re-run 'terraform init' after adding a new provider or module: Adding a new child module or provider to your code and immediately running <code>terraform plan</code> will crash with: <code>Error: Module not installed</code> or <code>Error: Could not load plugin</code>. Always run init after changing dependencies.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">CI/CD speed optimization: 'In GitHub Actions or GitLab CI, set <code>TF_PLUGIN_CACHE_DIR</code> and cache the directory between runs. This prevents runners from downloading 250MB AWS provider binaries on every build, slashing CI pipeline runtimes by up to 80%.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf200_11","level":"junior","question":"What does the terraform plan command do and how does it calculate execution diffs?","tags":["TERRAFORM","CLI","Plan","Workflow"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\"><code>terraform plan</code> creates an execution plan, letting you preview the exact set of changes Terraform will make to your cloud infrastructure before committing them. It computes a 3-way reconciliation between <strong>your HCL code</strong>, the <strong>state file</strong>, and the <strong>live cloud reality</strong>.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>The 3-Way Diff Calculation:</strong></li>\n  <li>1. <em>Refresh Phase:</em> Terraform queries provider APIs to read current live attributes of all resources in state (unless <code>-refresh=false</code> is passed).</li>\n  <li>2. <em>Diff Calculation:</em> Terraform compares the live attributes against the desired HCL declarations.</li>\n  <li>3. <em>Action Symbols:</em></li>\n  <li>  • <code>+ create</code>: Resource exists in code but not in cloud.</li>\n  <li>  • <code>- destroy</code>: Resource exists in state/cloud but removed from code.</li>\n  <li>  • <code>~ update in-place</code>: Resource exists, but non-destructive attributes changed (e.g. tags).</li>\n  <li>  • <code>-/+ replace</code>: Destructive attribute changed that cannot be updated in-place (e.g. changing EC2 AMI or VPC CIDR), requiring destruction followed by recreation.</li>\n  <li>4. <em>Exit Codes (<code>-detailed-exitcode</code>):</em> 0 = No changes, 1 = Error, 2 = Changes present (ideal for CI/CD drift detection).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Save execution plan to file for safe deterministic application\nterraform plan -out=tfplan\n\n# Inspect human-readable plan from a binary plan file\nterraform show tfplan\n\n# Automated CI drift detection check\nterraform plan -detailed-exitcode -no-color\nEXIT_CODE=$?\nif [ $EXIT_CODE -eq 2 ]; then\n  echo \"Infrastructure drift detected! Paging on-call...\"\nfi</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Plan is NOT a 100% guarantee of apply success: <code>terraform plan</code> validates HCL syntax and checks API diffs, but it cannot predict runtime cloud API errors (e.g., AWS service quota limits exceeded, insufficient EC2 instance capacity in an AZ, or invalid IAM role trusts).</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Production security rule: 'Plan files (<code>tfplan</code>) contain unencrypted secrets in binary JSON format. If you upload <code>tfplan</code> as a CI/CD build artifact, encrypt it or ensure artifact storage has strict access controls.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf200_12","level":"junior","question":"What is the terraform apply command used for and how does it guarantee atomicity?","tags":["TERRAFORM","CLI","Apply","Operations"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\"><code>terraform apply</code> is the operational command that executes the actions proposed in a Terraform plan. It makes authenticated API calls to cloud providers to create, update, or destroy physical and virtual resources, updating the state file after every operation.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Execution Phases:</strong></li>\n  <li>1. If no plan file is supplied, it automatically generates a fresh plan and prompts the operator with <code>Do you want to perform these actions? (yes/no)</code>.</li>\n  <li>2. Acquires the backend state lock to ensure exclusive execution.</li>\n  <li>3. Traverses the dependency graph, executing independent resource operations concurrently (governed by <code>-parallelism=10</code> default).</li>\n  <li>4. Updates state continuously: as each individual resource API call returns success, Terraform writes the updated state immediately to prevent state desync.</li>\n  <li><strong>Atomicity Limitations:</strong> Terraform is NOT a transactional database. If an apply provisioning 10 resources fails on resource 7 (e.g. quota limit), resources 1 through 6 remain running in the cloud and are recorded in state. Terraform does NOT automatically roll back.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Standard interactive apply with prompt\nterraform apply\n\n# Non-interactive apply with pre-approved plan file (Standard CI/CD)\nterraform apply -input=false tfplan\n\n# Tuning concurrency parallelism (reduce to prevent API rate-limiting)\nterraform apply -parallelism=5 tfplan</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Assuming Terraform automatically rolls back on failure: If resource creation fails halfway through, Terraform leaves previously created resources running in the cloud. You must either fix the root cause and re-run apply, or run <code>terraform destroy</code> to clean up partial states.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Staff SRE workflow: 'Never run naked <code>terraform apply</code> in production. Always run <code>terraform plan -out=tfplan</code> in a review stage, review the exact diff, and run <code>terraform apply tfplan</code> in the deployment stage.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf200_13","level":"junior","question":"What is the purpose of the terraform destroy command and how do you prevent accidental teardown?","tags":["TERRAFORM","CLI","Destroy","DisasterPrevention"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\"><code>terraform destroy</code> terminates and deletes all infrastructure resources managed by the current Terraform configuration. It traverses the dependency graph in <strong>reverse order</strong>, safely tearing down downstream dependencies before deleting foundation components.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Reverse Dependency Graph:</strong> To avoid dependency violations (e.g. deleting a VPC before deleting EC2 instances inside it), Terraform deletes leaves first, then intermediate components, and finally root infrastructure.</li>\n  <li><strong>Disaster Prevention Safeguard 1 - prevent_destroy:</strong> Declared inside a resource's <code>lifecycle</code> block. Terraform will immediately halt with an error if any plan or destroy command attempts to delete that resource.</li>\n  <li><strong>Disaster Prevention Safeguard 2 - State Segmentation:</strong> Isolating production state files from dev/test states.</li>\n  <li><strong>Disaster Prevention Safeguard 3 - CI/CD Role Stripping:</strong> Strip <code>ec2:Delete*</code> or <code>rds:DeleteDBInstance</code> permissions from standard developer IAM roles.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Enforce prevent_destroy on mission-critical production database\nresource \"aws_db_instance\" \"prod_database\" {\n  allocated_storage = 100\n  engine            = \"aurora-postgresql\"\n  instance_class    = \"db.r6g.xlarge\"\n\n  # Absolute safeguard against accidental destruction\n  lifecycle {\n    prevent_destroy = true\n  }\n}\n\n# CLI command to destroy ephemeral test environment\n# terraform destroy -auto-approve</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">prevent_destroy does not protect against manual cloud console deletion: <code>lifecycle { prevent_destroy = true }</code> is strictly a client-side Terraform guard. It does NOT prevent someone with AWS Console permissions from clicking 'Delete' in the AWS Console. Combine with AWS Resource Termination Protection and SCPs.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Production safeguard: 'Always set <code>prevent_destroy = true</code> on production databases, VPCs, KMS keys, and S3 buckets. If you genuinely intend to delete the resource, you must explicitly remove the lifecycle block in a separate, peer-reviewed pull request first.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf200_14","level":"junior","question":"How do you define, validate, and use input variables in Terraform?","tags":["TERRAFORM","Variables","Validation","HCL"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\"><strong>Input Variables</strong> serve as parameters for Terraform modules and configurations, allowing infrastructure code to be customized dynamically across environments without altering source code. Modern Terraform supports type constraints, default values, sensitivity masking, and custom validation rules.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Type Constraints:</strong> Supports primitive types (<code>string</code>, <code>number</code>, <code>bool</code>) and complex structural types (<code>list</code>, <code>set</code>, <code>map</code>, <code>object</code>, <code>tuple</code>).</li>\n  <li><strong>Custom Validation Blocks:</strong> Evaluates boolean expressions during planning. If an expression evaluates to false, Terraform aborts planning with a custom error message.</li>\n  <li><strong>Precedence Hierarchy (Lowest to Highest):</strong></li>\n  <li>1. Default value in variable declaration.</li>\n  <li>2. Environment variables (<code>TF_VAR_variable_name</code>).</li>\n  <li>3. <code>terraform.tfvars</code> file.</li>\n  <li>4. <code>terraform.tfvars.json</code> file.</li>\n  <li>5. <code>*.auto.tfvars</code> files (alphabetical).</li>\n  <li>6. Command line flags (<code>-var</code> or <code>-var-file</code>).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>variable \"environment\" {\n  type        = string\n  description = \"Target deployment environment\"\n  default     = \"dev\"\n\n  # Custom validation rule to restrict allowed environments\n  validation {\n    condition     = contains([\"dev\", \"staging\", \"prod\"], var.environment)\n    error_message = \"The environment variable must be one of: dev, staging, prod.\"\n  }\n}\n\nvariable \"db_port\" {\n  type        = number\n  description = \"Database listening port\"\n  default     = 5432\n\n  validation {\n    condition     = var.db_port &gt; 1024 &amp;&amp; var.db_port &lt; 65535\n    error_message = \"Port must be in valid unprivileged range (1025-65534).\"\n  }\n}</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Omitting type constraints: Leaving a variable type undeclared (<code>any</code>) allows unexpected data structures to be passed, resulting in cryptic runtime errors deep within child modules during apply rather than early validation during plan.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Staff best practice: 'Use custom <code>validation</code> blocks extensively on module variables. Catching invalid IP CIDRs, naming convention violations, or invalid port ranges during the plan phase prevents broken apply operations.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf200_15","level":"junior","question":"What are output values in Terraform and how are they used in CI/CD and cross-module referencing?","tags":["TERRAFORM","Outputs","CI/CD","Modules"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\"><strong>Output Values</strong> are return values from Terraform configurations. Similar to function return values in general programming languages, outputs expose resource attributes to the terminal CLI, pass data from child modules to parent modules, and provide integration endpoints for downstream CI/CD deployment pipelines.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Three Core Use Cases:</strong></li>\n  <li>1. <em>Child-to-Parent Communication:</em> Exposes internal child module resource attributes (e.g. subnet IDs) so the parent root module can consume them.</li>\n  <li>2. <em>CLI Display:</em> Prints key connection strings, public IPs, and load balancer DNS hostnames to stdout upon apply completion.</li>\n  <li>3. <em>Downstream CI/CD Integration:</em> Exported as JSON via <code>terraform output -json</code> for automated consumption by Ansible, Helm, or smoke testing scripts.</li>\n  <li><strong>Sensitive Outputs:</strong> Outputs containing secrets must be declared with <code>sensitive = true</code>, redacting them from console output unless queried specifically via <code>terraform output -raw</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Expose VPC ID to parent modules and CLI\noutput \"vpc_id\" {\n  value       = aws_vpc.main.id\n  description = \"The ID of the primary VPC\"\n}\n\n# Expose sensitive database connection string\noutput \"db_connection_string\" {\n  value       = \"postgresql://\\${aws_db_instance.db.username}:\\${aws_db_instance.db.password}@\\${aws_db_instance.db.endpoint}/\\${aws_db_instance.db.db_name}\"\n  description = \"Database connection URI\"\n  sensitive   = true\n}\n\n# CLI command: Extract output directly in shell scripts\n# DB_HOST=$(terraform output -raw rds_endpoint)</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Referencing sensitive outputs without marking parent output sensitive: If Child Module A marks an output sensitive, Parent Module B must also declare <code>sensitive = true</code> on any output that references it; otherwise, Terraform halts with an unhandled sensitive attribute error.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">DevOps automation tip: 'In deployment pipelines, use <code>terraform output -json > tf_outputs.json</code>. Downstream jobs can parse the JSON with <code>jq</code> to dynamically configure Kubernetes ingress hostnames or database connection pools.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf200_16","level":"senior","question":"How do you manage different environments (e.g., dev, staging, prod) in Terraform using workspaces or directory structures?","tags":["TERRAFORM","Environments","Architecture","Terragrunt"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Managing multiple environments (Dev, Staging, Production) requires striking a balance between code reusability (DRY) and blast radius isolation. The two dominant architectural patterns are <strong>Workspace-Based Isolation</strong> and <strong>Directory-Based (or Terragrunt) Isolation</strong>.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Pattern 1: Terraform Workspaces (Single Codebase, Multiple States):</strong></li>\n  <li>• <em>Pros:</em> Minimal code duplication; switching environments is a simple <code>terraform workspace select</code>.</li>\n  <li>• <em>Cons:</em> High blast radius. All environments share the same backend, same provider credentials, and same code version. Promoting changes to prod requires riskily updating the shared code.</li>\n  <li><strong>Pattern 2: Directory Separation / Terragrunt (Multi-Repo/Multi-Dir - Industry Gold Standard):</strong></li>\n  <li>• Structure:</li>\n  <li>  <code>environments/dev/</code> (isolated AWS dev account, isolated S3 state bucket)</li>\n  <li>  <code>environments/prod/</code> (isolated AWS prod account, locked S3 state bucket)</li>\n  <li>• Environments call shared versioned modules: <code>source = \"git::...?ref=v1.2.0\"</code>.</li>\n  <li>• <em>Pros:</em> Complete blast radius isolation. A disaster in dev cannot touch prod credentials. Environments can run different module versions simultaneously.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Production Directory Structure:\n# repo-root/\n# ├── modules/\n# │   ├── vpc/\n# │   └── eks/\n# └── live/\n#     ├── dev/\n#     │   ├── backend.tf       # Dedicated S3 bucket in Dev Account\n#     │   └── main.tf          # Calls modules with ?ref=v1.3.0\n#     └── prod/\n#         ├── backend.tf       # Dedicated S3 bucket in Prod Account\n#         └── main.tf          # Calls modules with ?ref=v1.2.0 (stable)</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Using workspaces to isolate AWS accounts: Workspaces cannot easily switch AWS provider credentials or assume distinct IAM roles across disparate cloud accounts within the same directory without fragile wrapper scripts. Never use workspaces across multiple cloud accounts.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Staff SRE recommendation: 'For enterprise multi-account architectures, use Directory Separation backed by Terragrunt or Atmos. It enforces strict physical state isolation, dedicated IAM boundaries, and independent module version pinning across environments.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf200_17","level":"senior","question":"What is remote state and how do you configure it with S3, GCS, or Terraform Cloud?","tags":["TERRAFORM","RemoteState","Backends","Architecture"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">By default, Terraform stores state locally in <code>terraform.tfstate</code>. In production, teams configure a <strong>Remote Backend</strong> to store the state file in a remote, shared, and secure data store (such as Amazon S3, Google Cloud Storage, Azure Blob, or HashiCorp HCP Terraform) to enable team collaboration, automated state locking, and secure CI/CD execution.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>S3 + DynamoDB Backend Architecture:</strong></li>\n  <li>• S3 stores the state file with encryption and versioning.</li>\n  <li>• DynamoDB manages distributed mutex locking to prevent concurrent writes.</li>\n  <li><strong>Backend Migration Workflow:</strong></li>\n  <li>1. Add the <code>backend \"s3\"</code> block to your HCL code.</li>\n  <li>2. Run <code>terraform init -migrate-state</code>.</li>\n  <li>3. Terraform detects existing local state, prompts to copy it to the remote backend, and removes or archives the local state file.</li>\n  <li><strong>Enhanced Backends (HCP Terraform / Spacelift):</strong> Provide remote planning and applying on dedicated cloud runners directly from webhooks.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Complete Production AWS S3 Remote Backend Configuration\nterraform {\n  required_version = \"&gt;= 1.5.0\"\n\n  backend \"s3\" {\n    bucket         = \"enterprise-tf-state-us-east-1\"\n    key            = \"platform/kubernetes/terraform.tfstate\"\n    region         = \"us-east-1\"\n    encrypt        = true\n    dynamodb_table = \"terraform-lock-table\"\n  }\n}</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Hardcoding backend variables in HCL: Terraform <code>backend</code> blocks do NOT support variable interpolation (you cannot write <code>bucket = var.state_bucket</code>). You must use static values or pass backend configuration dynamically via <code>-backend-config=file.tfbackend</code>.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">DRY backend configuration: 'Maintain a shared <code>backend.hcl</code> file with common bucket, region, and lock table parameters. Initialize with <code>terraform init -backend-config=backend.hcl -backend-config=\"key=service/terraform.tfstate\"</code> to eliminate copy-paste duplication.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf200_18","level":"senior","question":"How do you import existing cloud resources into Terraform state using terraform import and import blocks?","tags":["TERRAFORM","Import","Refactoring","IaC"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">When adopting Terraform in an organization with existing manually provisioned cloud resources, you must bring those resources under Terraform management without recreating them. Modern Terraform supports two import workflows: the legacy <strong>imperative CLI import</strong> (<code>terraform import</code>) and the modern <strong>declarative import block</strong> (introduced in Terraform 1.5+).</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>1. Declarative Import Block (Terraform 1.5+ Recommended):</strong></li>\n  <li>• Declared directly in HCL code via an <code>import</code> block.</li>\n  <li>• Specifies the target resource address (<code>to</code>) and the cloud resource ID (<code>id</code>).</li>\n  <li>• Supports automated HCL generation: running <code>terraform plan -generate-config-out=generated.tf</code> automatically writes the corresponding HCL code for you!</li>\n  <li>• Can be committed to version control and applied deterministically in CI/CD.</li>\n  <li><strong>2. Legacy Imperative CLI (terraform import):</strong></li>\n  <li>• Requires pre-writing the HCL resource block manually.</li>\n  <li>• Mutates the state file immediately from the terminal without review.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Modern Declarative Import Block in HCL (Terraform 1.5+)\nimport {\n  to = aws_security_group.legacy_sg\n  id = \"sg-0123456789abcdef0\"\n}\n\n# Run plan to automatically generate matching HCL code:\n# terraform plan -generate-config-out=generated_sg.tf\n\n# Apply the import deterministically\n# terraform apply</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Import does NOT generate code in legacy CLI: In older versions, running <code>terraform import aws_s3_bucket.mybucket mybucket</code> imports state but does NOT generate any HCL code. If you run plan immediately afterward, Terraform attempts to destroy the resource because no HCL was declared. Always use modern 1.5+ import blocks.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Staff SRE practice: 'Declarative import blocks revolutionize IaC adoption. By committing <code>import { to = ... id = ... }</code> to Git, the import becomes a peer-reviewed, audited pull request that executes safely in CI/CD without requiring risky local engineer CLI access.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf200_19","level":"junior","question":"What are data sources in Terraform and how do they fetch read-only attributes from external systems?","tags":["TERRAFORM","DataSources","Architecture"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">A <strong>Data Source</strong> allows Terraform to query read-only data from external cloud APIs, other Terraform state files, or local operating system utilities. Unlike managed resources (<code>resource</code> blocks) that create and destroy infrastructure, data sources (<code>data</code> blocks) strictly fetch existing information to inform configuration decisions.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Read-Only Query Engine:</strong> Executed during the plan phase to fetch current real-time metadata (e.g. latest Ubuntu AMI ID, list of available Availability Zones, current AWS Account ID).</li>\n  <li><strong>Dynamic Discovery:</strong> Eliminates hardcoding AMI IDs or subnet IDs across regions and accounts.</li>\n  <li><strong>terraform_remote_state:</strong> A special data source that reads outputs from an entirely separate Terraform state file, enabling clean separation of concerns between networking and application teams.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Fetch the latest official Ubuntu 22.04 LTS AMI dynamically\ndata \"aws_ami\" \"ubuntu\" {\n  most_recent = true\n  owners      = [\"099720109477\"] # Canonical official owner ID\n\n  filter {\n    name   = \"name\"\n    values = [\"ubuntu/images/hvm-ssd/ubuntu-jammy-22.04-amd64-server-*\"]\n  }\n\n  filter {\n    name   = \"virtualization-type\"\n    values = [\"hvm\"]\n  }\n}\n\n# Fetch the current AWS Account ID and Region\ndata \"aws_caller_identity\" \"current\" {}\ndata \"aws_region\" \"current\" {}</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Overly broad data source filters: If an AMI filter matches multiple images and <code>most_recent = true</code> is omitted, Terraform halts with: <code>Error: your query returned more than one result</code>. Ensure filters are sufficiently specific.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Production decoupling: 'Use data sources to decouple teams: the Platform team provisions the VPC and tags subnets (e.g. <code>Type=Private</code>); Application teams use <code>data \"aws_subnets\"</code> with tag filters to discover subnets dynamically without needing direct state access.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf200_20","level":"mid","question":"What are provisioners (local-exec, remote-exec) in Terraform and why are they considered a last resort?","tags":["TERRAFORM","Provisioners","AntiPatterns","BestPractices"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\"><strong>Provisioners</strong> (<code>local-exec</code>, <code>remote-exec</code>, <code>file</code>) are escape hatches in Terraform that execute arbitrary shell commands or copy files onto a newly provisioned virtual machine or local runner. HashiCorp explicitly classifies provisioners as a <strong>measure of last resort</strong> because they break declarative idempotency, complicate state management, and introduce external execution dependencies.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>1. local-exec:</strong> Executes a command locally on the machine running Terraform (e.g. running an <code>aws cli</code> command or triggering a webhook).</li>\n  <li><strong>2. remote-exec:</strong> Connects to the newly created VM over SSH or WinRM and executes shell commands.</li>\n  <li><strong>3. Creation-time vs. Destroy-time:</strong> Creation-time provisioners run only when a resource is created; if they fail, the resource is marked <em>tainted</em>. Destroy-time provisioners run before a resource is destroyed.</li>\n  <li><strong>Why Provisioners are Anti-Patterns:</strong></li>\n  <li>• They bypass Terraform's declarative model and cannot be planned or previewed.</li>\n  <li>• They require SSH credentials and network connectivity from the runner to the target host.</li>\n  <li>• Superior alternatives exist: cloud-init, Packer pre-baked AMIs, Kubernetes controllers, or SSM Agent.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Last-Resort Example: local-exec to invoke a local script\nresource \"aws_instance\" \"web\" {\n  ami           = \"ami-0c55b159cbfafe1f0\"\n  instance_type = \"t3.micro\"\n\n  # Anti-pattern: Better handled by cloud-init user_data\n  provisioner \"local-exec\" {\n    command = \"echo Server IP: \\${self.public_ip} &gt;&gt; inventory.txt\"\n  }\n}\n\n# RECOMMENDED Modern Replacement: Native user_data cloud-init\nresource \"aws_instance\" \"web_modern\" {\n  ami           = \"ami-0c55b159cbfafe1f0\"\n  instance_type = \"t3.micro\"\n  user_data     = &lt;&lt;-EOF\n                  #!/bin/bash\n                  yum install -y nginx\n                  systemctl enable --now nginx\n                  EOF\n}</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Failure taints resource: If a <code>remote-exec</code> provisioner fails (e.g. transient network timeout or apt lock conflict), Terraform marks the resource as <strong>tainted</strong>, forcing it to be destroyed and recreated on the next apply even if the VM was 99% configured.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Staff SRE doctrine: 'Bake, don't fry. Use HashiCorp Packer to bake software, security agents, and configurations directly into immutable AMIs or container images. Use Terraform strictly to deploy the baked image.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf200_21","level":"senior","question":"How do you handle sensitive credentials and secrets in Terraform without committing them to Git?","tags":["TERRAFORM","Security","Secrets","BestPractices"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Committing plaintext cloud access keys or database passwords to version control is a leading cause of enterprise cloud security breaches. Production Terraform architectures enforce a <strong>Zero-Hardcoded-Secrets policy</strong> using dynamic environment variables, cloud IAM role federation (OIDC), and secrets management integrations.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>1. Ambient Authentication (No Static Keys):</strong> Run Terraform inside CI/CD using <strong>OIDC (OpenID Connect)</strong> to assume short-lived AWS IAM roles dynamically without storing static <code>AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID</code> credentials anywhere.</li>\n  <li><strong>2. Sensitive Variable Flags:</strong> Mark all credential variables with <code>sensitive = true</code> to prevent values from appearing in logs or terminal output.</li>\n  <li><strong>3. Environment Variable Injection:</strong> Pass secrets via <code>TF_VAR_<variable_name></code> injected from enterprise CI/CD secret vaults (e.g. GitHub Secrets, GitLab CI Variables).</li>\n  <li><strong>4. External Secrets Managers:</strong> Fetch passwords dynamically at runtime using HashiCorp Vault or AWS Secrets Manager data sources.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Inject secrets via environment variables in CI/CD\nexport TF_VAR_db_password=\"$CI_SECRET_DB_PASSWORD\"\nexport AWS_ROLE_ARN=\"arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/GitHubActionsTerraformRole\"\nexport AWS_WEB_IDENTITY_TOKEN_FILE=\"/var/run/secrets/token\"\n\n# Run plan without prompting for secrets\nterraform plan -out=tfplan</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Git-ignoring .tfvars files is NOT enough: Relying on <code>.gitignore</code> for <code>terraform.tfvars</code> is fragile; one accidental <code>git add -f</code> permanently commits secrets into Git history. Prefer environment variable injection or dynamic secret retrieval.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Defense-in-depth: 'Enforce pre-commit hooks using tools like <code>gitleaks</code> or <code>trivy</code> to scan every commit for secrets locally before code can be pushed to remote Git repositories.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf200_22","level":"mid","question":"What is a backend in Terraform, and what is the difference between enhanced and standard backends?","tags":["TERRAFORM","Backends","Architecture"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">A <strong>Backend</strong> in Terraform defines two fundamental things: <strong>where state is stored</strong> and <strong>how operations are executed</strong>. Backends are classified into two categories: <strong>Standard Backends</strong> and <strong>Enhanced Backends</strong>.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>1. Standard Backends (Storage Only):</strong></li>\n  <li>• Examples: <code>s3</code>, <code>gcs</code>, <code>azurerm</code>, <code>consul</code>, <code>http</code>.</li>\n  <li>• Function: Provides shared remote state storage and distributed state locking.</li>\n  <li>• Execution: All operations (plan, apply, graph calculation) execute locally on the operator's laptop or CI runner CPU.</li>\n  <li><strong>2. Enhanced Backends (Storage + Remote Execution):</strong></li>\n  <li>• Examples: <code>remote</code> (HCP Terraform / Terraform Enterprise), <code>cloud</code> block.</li>\n  <li>• Function: Not only stores state, but can offload the entire execution of <code>terraform plan</code> and <code>terraform apply</code> to isolated, managed cloud runners.</li>\n  <li>• Enables VCS-driven GitOps workflows and speculative plans directly on pull requests.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Modern Terraform Cloud / HCP Terraform Enhanced Backend ('cloud' block)\nterraform {\n  required_version = \"&gt;= 1.5.0\"\n\n  cloud {\n    organization = \"enterprise-corp\"\n\n    workspaces {\n      name = \"prod-infrastructure\"\n    }\n  }\n}</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Switching backends without migration: Changing the backend block and running <code>terraform init</code> without understanding state migration prompts can cause state loss if you overwrite an existing remote state with an empty local state.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Interview clarity: 'A standard backend like S3 is just a smart file server for state JSON. An enhanced backend like Terraform Cloud or Spacelift is a full CI/CD orchestration platform that runs Terraform in ephemeral containers with policy gates and audit logging.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf200_23","level":"mid","question":"How do you use conditional expressions (ternary operators) and null values in Terraform?","tags":["TERRAFORM","HCL","Expressions","Conditionals"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Terraform supports declarative conditional logic using ternary expressions: <code>condition ? true_val : false_val</code>. When combined with the <strong>null</strong> value, conditionals allow resources, dynamic blocks, and optional attributes to be selectively enabled or omitted without duplicating code.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Ternary Operator Syntax:</strong> <code>condition ? true_val : false_val</code>. Both branches must resolve to the same type (or types that can automatically convert).</li>\n  <li><strong>The Magic of 'null':</strong> Assigning <code>null</code> to an argument in a resource block behaves exactly as if that argument had been completely omitted from the configuration, falling back to the provider's default behavior.</li>\n  <li><strong>Conditional Resource Creation:</strong></li>\n  <li>• Using <code>count = var.enable_bastion ? 1 : 0</code>.</li>\n  <li>• Using <code>for_each = var.enable_bastion ? { \"bastion\" = var.bastion_config } : {}</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>variable \"create_eip\" {\n  type    = bool\n  default = false\n}\n\nvariable \"custom_kms_key_arn\" {\n  type    = string\n  default = null\n}\n\nresource \"aws_eip\" \"nat\" {\n  count = var.create_eip ? 1 : 0\n  domain = \"vpc\"\n}\n\nresource \"aws_ebs_volume\" \"data\" {\n  availability_zone = \"us-east-1a\"\n  size              = 50\n\n  # If custom_kms_key_arn is null, AWS uses the default AWS-managed KMS key\n  kms_key_id = var.custom_kms_key_arn != null ? var.custom_kms_key_arn : null\n  encrypted  = true\n}</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Type mismatches in ternary branches: Writing <code>var.is_prod ? [\"subnet-1\"] : \"subnet-1\"</code> will fail with a compile error because the true branch is a list and the false branch is a string. Both branches must return identical types.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Clean code pattern: 'Prefer <code>null</code> over dummy placeholder strings like \"none\" or \"N/A\". Passing <code>null</code> instructs the cloud provider API to completely ignore the parameter, preventing API schema validation errors.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf200_24","level":"junior","question":"What is the purpose of the terraform validate command and how does it differ from terraform plan?","tags":["TERRAFORM","CLI","Validation","Linting"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\"><code>terraform validate</code> is a fast, offline verification command that checks whether your Terraform configuration is syntactically valid and internally consistent according to provider schemas. Unlike <code>terraform plan</code>, it runs entirely locally without contacting remote backends or querying cloud APIs.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>What terraform validate Checks:</strong></li>\n  <li>• HCL syntax errors (missing brackets, malformed strings).</li>\n  <li>• Attribute name typos (e.g. writing <code>instanc_type</code> instead of <code>instance_type</code>).</li>\n  <li>• Type correctness (e.g. passing a string to an argument expecting a list).</li>\n  <li>• Undeclared variable references.</li>\n  <li><strong>Differences from terraform plan:</strong></li>\n  <li>• <em>Speed:</em> Validate completes in ~1-2 seconds; plan can take 1-5 minutes for large state files.</li>\n  <li>• <em>Network:</em> Validate is 100% offline (no cloud API access or credentials required). Plan requires valid cloud IAM credentials and remote backend access.</li>\n  <li>• <em>State:</em> Validate ignores state and drift. Plan performs 3-way state diffing.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Fast pre-commit validation command in Git hooks\nterraform validate\n\n# JSON output mode for programmatic CI reporting\nterraform validate -json | jq .valid</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Running validate before init: <code>terraform validate</code> requires provider schemas to be present in the <code>.terraform</code> directory. Running validate in a fresh directory before running <code>terraform init</code> will fail with an uninitialized provider error.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">CI pipeline stage ordering: 'Place <code>terraform fmt -check</code> and <code>terraform validate</code> in the earliest lint stage of your PR pipeline. It catches 80% of developer typos in 5 seconds before launching expensive cloud plan runners.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf200_25","level":"junior","question":"How can you format and lint Terraform configuration files using terraform fmt, tflint, and tfsec?","tags":["TERRAFORM","Tooling","Linting","Security","CI/CD"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Maintaining enterprise-grade IaC codebases requires automated formatting, static analysis, and security scanning. The industry standard triad consists of <strong>terraform fmt</strong> (style alignment), <strong>tflint</strong> (deep cloud provider static analysis), and <strong>tfsec / trivy</strong> (security vulnerability scanning).</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>1. terraform fmt:</strong> Built-in canonical formatter. Standardizes indentation (2 spaces), aligns assignment operators (<code>=</code>), and organizes arguments.</li>\n  <li><strong>2. TFLint:</strong> A Pluggable linter for Terraform. Warns about deprecated syntax, invalid EC2 instance types (e.g. catching <code>t2.nanoo</code> typo before apply), and cloud-specific anti-patterns.</li>\n  <li><strong>3. tfsec / Trivy:</strong> Security scanners that detect security flaws (unencrypted S3 buckets, open <code>0.0.0.0/0</code> SSH security groups, overly permissive IAM wildcards) directly in HCL code prior to deployment.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># 1. Format all code recursively\nterraform fmt -recursive\n\n# 2. Check formatting in CI (returns exit code 3 if unformatted files exist)\nterraform fmt -check -recursive\n\n# 3. Run TFLint to catch cloud provider attribute errors\ntflint --init\ntflint\n\n# 4. Scan code for security misconfigurations with Trivy / tfsec\ntrivy config . --severity HIGH,CRITICAL</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Skipping fmt checks in CI: Inconsistent formatting causes messy Git diffs where whitespace changes obscure real infrastructure modifications during pull request reviews. Enforce <code>terraform fmt -check</code> as a blocking PR check.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">DevOps tooling integration: 'Implement <code>pre-commit</code> hooks (using the <code>antonbabenko/pre-commit-terraform</code> framework). It automatically formats HCL, updates README docs with <code>terraform-docs</code>, and runs <code>tflint</code> on every git commit before code reaches the repo.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf200_26","level":"mid","question":"What is the difference between count and for_each in Terraform, and why does for_each prevent unwanted recreations?","tags":["TERRAFORM","HCL","Iteration","BestPractices"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Both <code>count</code> and <code>for_each</code> are meta-arguments used to create multiple instances of a resource or module. However, <code>count</code> identifies resources by an <strong>integer index array</strong>, whereas <code>for_each</code> identifies resources by a <strong>string map or set key</strong>. This difference makes <code>for_each</code> vastly superior for preventing accidental resource recreation.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>The 'Count Index Shifting' Disaster:</strong></li>\n  <li>• Given: <code>subnets = [\"subnet-a\", \"subnet-b\", \"subnet-c\"]</code> with <code>count = length(var.subnets)</code>.</li>\n  <li>• Resources in state: <code>aws_subnet.net[0]</code>, <code>aws_subnet.net[1]</code>, <code>aws_subnet.net[2]</code>.</li>\n  <li>• If someone deletes <code>\"subnet-a\"</code> from the middle of the list: <code>\"subnet-b\"</code> shifts to index <code>[0]</code>, and <code>\"subnet-c\"</code> shifts to index <code>[1]</code>.</li>\n  <li>• Terraform sees index <code>[0]</code> changed attributes and <strong>destroys and recreates subnet-b and subnet-c</strong>, taking down production databases and nodes!</li>\n  <li><strong>How for_each Solves This:</strong></li>\n  <li>• Resources in state are keyed by immutable string names: <code>aws_subnet.net[\"subnet-a\"]</code>.</li>\n  <li>• Removing <code>\"subnet-a\"</code> destroys strictly <code>aws_subnet.net[\"subnet-a\"]</code>; <code>[\"subnet-b\"]</code> and <code>[\"subnet-c\"]</code> remain completely untouched.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># BEST PRACTICE: Using for_each with a set of strings\nvariable \"subnets\" {\n  type    = set(string)\n  default = [\"subnet-a\", \"subnet-b\", \"subnet-c\"]\n}\n\nresource \"aws_subnet\" \"example\" {\n  for_each          = var.subnets\n  vpc_id            = aws_vpc.main.id\n  cidr_block        = \"10.0.\\${index(tolist(var.subnets), each.value)}.0/24\"\n  availability_zone = each.value\n\n  tags = {\n    Name = each.key\n  }\n}</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Using count for collections of distinct resources: Use <code>count</code> strictly for boolean toggles (<code>count = var.enabled ? 1 : 0</code>) or identical worker pools. For any named or parameterized resources, always use <code>for_each</code>.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Staff SRE rule of thumb: '<code>count</code> is for numbers (e.g. 5 identical stateless web servers); <code>for_each</code> is for collections with identity (subnets, IAM users, security group rules, disk volumes).'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf200_27","level":"mid","question":"How do you use loops (for expressions, dynamic blocks) in Terraform to transform data structures?","tags":["TERRAFORM","HCL","Loops","DynamicBlocks"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Terraform provides powerful functional iteration constructs to transform data structures and dynamically construct repeated nested configuration blocks: <strong>for expressions</strong> (list/map comprehensions) and <strong>dynamic blocks</strong>.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>1. For Expressions (List Comprehensions):</strong></li>\n  <li>• <code>[for s in var.list : upper(s)]</code>: Transforms a list into a new list.</li>\n  <li>• <code>{for k, v in var.map : k => v.id if v.active}</code>: Filters and constructs a new map.</li>\n  <li><strong>2. Dynamic Blocks:</strong></li>\n  <li>• Generates repeated nested blocks inside a resource (e.g. multiple <code>ingress</code> rules inside a Security Group or multiple <code>setting</code> blocks in Elastic Beanstalk).</li>\n  <li>• Iterates over a collection using <code>for_each</code> inside the <code>dynamic \"block_name\"</code> construct.</li>\n  <li>• The current item is accessed via the block name iterator: <code>ingress.value</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Dynamic blocks: Generating Security Group Ingress rules dynamically\nvariable \"ingress_rules\" {\n  type = list(object({\n    port        = number\n    description = string\n  }))\n  default = [\n    { port = 80, description = \"HTTP Web\" },\n    { port = 443, description = \"HTTPS Secure\" },\n    { port = 8080, description = \"Internal App\" }\n  ]\n}\n\nresource \"aws_security_group\" \"web_sg\" {\n  name   = \"dynamic-web-sg\"\n  vpc_id = aws_vpc.main.id\n\n  dynamic \"ingress\" {\n    for_each = var.ingress_rules\n    content {\n      from_port   = ingress.value.port\n      to_port     = ingress.value.port\n      protocol    = \"tcp\"\n      cidr_blocks = [\"0.0.0.0/0\"]\n      description = ingress.value.description\n    }\n  }\n}</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Overusing dynamic blocks makes code unreadable: Overly clever dynamic block logic can obscure the intended security posture of resources. Use standalone resources (like <code>aws_security_group_rule</code>) when rules need independent lifecycle management.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Code pattern: 'Combine <code>for</code> expressions with <code>flatten()</code> when iterating over nested data structures (e.g. looping over an array of subnets inside an array of VPCs) to generate clean input maps for <code>for_each</code>.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf200_28","level":"junior","question":"What are locals in Terraform and how do you use them to simplify complex expressions?","tags":["TERRAFORM","Locals","HCL","CleanCode"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">A <strong>Local Value</strong> (<code>locals</code> block) assigns a name to an expression, allowing that expression to be reused multiple times within a module without repeating the calculation. Similar to standard local variables in programming languages, locals help keep HCL code DRY, readable, and maintainable.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Variables vs. Locals:</strong></li>\n  <li>• <em>Input Variables (variable):</em> External inputs provided by the user/caller (cannot reference other variables in their default values).</li>\n  <li>• <em>Local Values (locals):</em> Internal intermediate computations defined within the module (can freely interpolate other variables, resources, and data sources).</li>\n  <li><strong>Common Use Cases:</strong></li>\n  <li>• Standardizing consistent resource naming conventions: <code>name = \"${local.prefix}-${var.env}-${var.app_name}\"</code>.</li>\n  <li>• Common tag merging: <code>tags = merge(var.custom_tags, local.mandatory_tags)</code>.</li>\n  <li>• Pre-computing complex conditionals or data structure flattening.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>locals {\n  # Standardized naming convention\n  name_prefix = \"\\${var.company}-\\${var.environment}-\\${var.app_name}\"\n\n  # Mandatory enterprise tags\n  common_tags = {\n    Owner       = \"Platform-Team\"\n    ManagedBy   = \"Terraform\"\n    Environment = var.environment\n    CostCenter  = \"Engineering-104\"\n  }\n}\n\nresource \"aws_s3_bucket\" \"app_storage\" {\n  bucket = \"\\${local.name_prefix}-storage\"\n  tags   = local.common_tags\n}</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Creating monolithic locals that compute everything: Avoid defining huge, tangled local blocks with 50 interdependent expressions. Keep locals scoped and close to where they are used to preserve code readability.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Tag management standard: 'Always use <code>locals</code> to define base tags, and merge them with resource-specific tags using <code>tags = merge(local.common_tags, { Role = \"Database\" })</code>. This guarantees 100% cloud cost allocation compliance across all resources.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf200_29","level":"mid","question":"What is the purpose of the terraform taint command and why is terraform apply -replace preferred in modern Terraform?","tags":["TERRAFORM","Operations","Taint","Replace"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Historically, <code>terraform taint</code> was used to mark a specific managed resource as degraded or corrupted, forcing Terraform to destroy and recreate it during the next execution. In modern Terraform (v0.15.2+), <code>terraform taint</code> is deprecated in favor of <code>terraform apply -replace</code>, which is safe, atomic, and previewable.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>The Flaw of terraform taint:</strong> Running taint immediately mutates the state file on disk before you ever run plan. If your intent changes or another teammate plans concurrently, the resource is forcefully destroyed.</li>\n  <li><strong>Why -replace is Superior:</strong> <code>terraform plan -replace=\"aws_instance.web\"</code> does NOT modify the state file. It simply plans a replacement, allowing full code review and dry-run inspection in pull requests.</li>\n  <li><strong>Untainting:</strong> If a resource was tainted, <code>terraform untaint</code> can clear the taint flag if you decide not to recreate it.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Modern safe replacement preview in plan\nterraform plan -replace=\"aws_instance.app_server\" -out=tfplan\n\n# Apply strictly the planned replacement\nterraform apply tfplan\n\n# Legacy untaint command (if previously tainted)\nterraform untaint aws_instance.app_server</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Tainting Stateful Databases: Accidentally tainting an RDS instance or EBS volume will destroy and recreate the database, causing complete data loss unless final snapshots or delete protection are enabled.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Staff SRE guidance: 'Never use <code>terraform taint</code> in CI/CD pipelines or local terminal sessions. Always use <code>terraform plan -replace=...</code> to guarantee that resource replacement is an explicit, reviewed action.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf200_30","level":"mid","question":"How do you manage module versioning in Terraform using Semantic Versioning and Git tags?","tags":["TERRAFORM","Modules","Versioning","SemVer"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Module versioning allows teams to release updates, bug fixes, and breaking refactors to shared infrastructure modules without unexpectedly breaking downstream consumers. Terraform supports Semantic Versioning (SemVer <code>MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH</code>) natively when sourcing modules from the Terraform Registry or Git repositories.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Git Repository Sourcing:</strong> Use the <code>?ref=</code> URL query parameter pointing to an immutable Git tag (e.g. <code>?ref=v2.3.1</code>).</li>\n  <li><strong>Registry Sourcing:</strong> When using Terraform Registry or HCP Terraform private registry, use the <code>version</code> argument with version constraint operators (e.g. <code>~> 2.0</code>).</li>\n  <li><strong>Pessimistic Constraint (~>):</strong> <code>~> 2.1.0</code> permits upgrades to <code>2.1.1</code> and <code>2.1.9</code>, but blocks <code>2.2.0</code>. <code>~> 2.1</code> permits upgrades up to <code>2.9</code>, but blocks <code>3.0</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Immutable Git Tags:</strong> Never point <code>?ref=</code> to mutable branches like <code>main</code> or <code>master</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Sourcing versioned module from private Git repository via HTTPS\nmodule \"eks_cluster\" {\n  source = \"git::https://github.com/my-org/terraform-aws-eks.git?ref=v3.4.1\"\n\n  cluster_name    = \"prod-eks\"\n  cluster_version = \"1.29\"\n}\n\n# Sourcing versioned module from Terraform Registry with version constraint\nmodule \"vpc\" {\n  source  = \"terraform-aws-modules/vpc/aws\"\n  version = \"~&gt; 5.1.0\"\n\n  name = \"primary-vpc\"\n  cidr = \"10.0.0.0/16\"\n}</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Mutating Git Tags: Overwriting an existing Git tag (e.g. force-pushing <code>v1.0.0</code>) breaks reproducible builds across CI/CD runners that cached the old tag. Always increment patch versions (<code>v1.0.1</code>).</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Enterprise policy: 'Mandate that all internal modules follow Semantic Versioning: increment MAJOR for breaking attribute changes, MINOR for new optional inputs, and PATCH for bug fixes.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf200_31","level":"junior","question":"What is the Terraform Registry and how do you publish and consume public and private modules?","tags":["TERRAFORM","Registry","Modules","Collaboration"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">The <strong>Terraform Registry</strong> (registry.terraform.io) is HashiCorp's centralized directory for discovering, sharing, and publishing publicly available providers and community-vetted infrastructure modules. Organizations also utilize <strong>Private Module Registries</strong> (PMR) in HCP Terraform or GitLab to share proprietary enterprise modules securely.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Public Registry Sourcing:</strong> Compact three-part address: <code><NAMESPACE>/<NAME>/<PROVIDER></code> (e.g. <code>terraform-aws-modules/vpc/aws</code>).</li>\n  <li><strong>Private Registry Sourcing:</strong> Prefixed with the hostname: <code>app.terraform.io/<ORG>/<NAME>/<PROVIDER></code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Publishing Requirements:</strong></li>\n  <li>• Repository must be named <code>terraform-<PROVIDER>-<NAME></code> on GitHub/GitLab.</li>\n  <li>• Must follow standard module structure: <code>main.tf</code>, <code>variables.tf</code>, <code>outputs.tf</code>, <code>README.md</code>.</li>\n  <li>• Releases must be published as SemVer Git tags (e.g. <code>v1.0.0</code>).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Consuming an officially verified module from the Terraform Registry\nmodule \"security_group\" {\n  source  = \"terraform-aws-modules/security-group/aws\"\n  version = \"5.1.0\"\n\n  name        = \"web-server-sg\"\n  description = \"Security group for web-servers with HTTP/HTTPS ports\"\n  vpc_id      = \"vpc-01234567\"\n\n  ingress_cidr_blocks = [\"10.100.0.0/16\"]\n  ingress_rules       = [\"http-80-tcp\", \"https-443-tcp\"]\n}</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Consuming unverified public community modules without code auditing: Public modules can contain cryptominers, malicious local-exec provisioners, or security anti-patterns. Always audit third-party module source code before adopting.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Enterprise governance: 'In enterprise environments, fork popular public community modules into your internal Git organization and publish them through your Private Module Registry after passing security scans.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf200_32","level":"junior","question":"How do you perform a dry run in Terraform and save plan files for audit compliance?","tags":["TERRAFORM","CLI","DryRun","Compliance"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">In Terraform, a <strong>Dry Run</strong> is achieved using <code>terraform plan</code>. To satisfy regulatory audit requirements and eliminate race conditions between review and execution, the output plan must be serialized into an immutable binary plan file using the <code>-out</code> parameter.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Deterministic Execution Pipeline:</strong></li>\n  <li>1. The CI runner executes <code>terraform plan -out=tfplan</code>. Terraform serializes the exact execution graph, refreshed state, and variable inputs into a binary file.</li>\n  <li>2. The binary plan can be converted into human-readable JSON via <code>terraform show -json tfplan</code>.</li>\n  <li>3. Automated policy engines (Open Policy Agent, Sentinel) scan the JSON for compliance violations.</li>\n  <li>4. The human approver reviews the diff.</li>\n  <li>5. The deployment step runs <code>terraform apply tfplan</code>. Terraform guarantees that <em>only</em> the changes calculated in the dry run are executed.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Step 1: Perform dry run and serialize to binary plan file\nterraform plan -out=production.tfplan\n\n# Step 2: Convert to JSON for automated audit scanning\nterraform show -json production.tfplan &gt; plan.json\n\n# Step 3: Scan with OPA / Conftest for security compliance\nconftest test plan.json --policy ./policies\n\n# Step 4: Apply the audited dry-run plan\nterraform apply production.tfplan</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Applying a stale plan file: If cloud reality changes significantly between when the dry run was captured and when apply is executed (e.g. hours later), applying the stale plan file may fail with an API conflict. Keep plan-to-apply windows under 15 minutes.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Security reminder: 'Binary plan files contain all variables and resource attributes in plaintext. If plan files are archived in Jenkins or GitHub Actions artifacts, restrict artifact download permissions strictly to release managers.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf200_33","level":"mid","question":"What is the terraform state command family used for and how do you inspect state safely?","tags":["TERRAFORM","State","CLI","Troubleshooting"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">The <code>terraform state</code> command family provides advanced administrative subcommands for safely inspecting, querying, modifying, and refactoring the Terraform state file without opening or editing the raw JSON directly.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Core Subcommands:</strong></li>\n  <li>• <code>terraform state list</code>: Lists all resource addresses currently tracked in the state file.</li>\n  <li>• <code>terraform state show <address></code>: Displays all recorded attributes, IDs, and metadata for a specific resource.</li>\n  <li>• <code>terraform state mv <source> <dest></code>: Renames or moves a resource in state without destroying/recreating physical infrastructure.</li>\n  <li>• <code>terraform state rm <address></code>: Removes a resource from state management without deleting it from the cloud.</li>\n  <li>• <code>terraform state pull</code>: Outputs the entire remote state JSON to stdout.</li>\n  <li>• <code>terraform state push</code>: Overwrites remote state with a local file (disaster recovery only).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># List all resources in the state\nterraform state list\n\n# Show detailed attributes of the RDS database\nterraform state show aws_db_instance.primary\n\n# Filter state list for specific resource types\nterraform state list | grep aws_s3_bucket\n\n# Download remote state to local file for offline inspection\nterraform state pull &gt; state_dump.json</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Running state-mutating commands (mv, rm) without a backup: <code>terraform state mv</code> and <code>rm</code> immediately alter the remote state backend. Always run <code>terraform state pull > backup.json</code> before performing any state modification operations.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Refactoring rule: 'Whenever you reorganize files or split monoliths into child modules, use <code>terraform state mv</code> (or modern <code>moved</code> blocks) to move the resource addresses in state. This prevents Terraform from destroying and recreating active production resources.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf200_34","level":"senior","question":"How do you rename a resource in the state file using terraform state mv vs moved blocks?","tags":["TERRAFORM","Refactoring","State","MovedBlocks"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">When you rename a resource in HCL (e.g. from <code>aws_instance.server</code> to <code>aws_instance.web</code>) or move it inside a child module, Terraform sees the old address as deleted and the new address as created. This triggers a destructive <strong>destroy and recreate</strong> cycle. Operators resolve this using either the imperative <code>terraform state mv</code> CLI or the declarative <strong>moved block</strong> (Terraform 1.1+).</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Method 1: Declarative moved Block (Terraform 1.1+ Recommended):</strong></li>\n  <li>• Declared directly in HCL code: <code>moved { from = aws_instance.server to = aws_instance.web }</code>.</li>\n  <li>• Committed to Git as part of the pull request.</li>\n  <li>• Evaluated automatically during plan and apply: Terraform renames the resource in state seamlessly across all developer machines and CI pipelines.</li>\n  <li><strong>Method 2: Imperative CLI (terraform state mv):</strong></li>\n  <li>• Executed manually: <code>terraform state mv aws_instance.server aws_instance.web</code>.</li>\n  <li>• Fragile: Must be coordinated manually across team members and CI runners.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Modern Declarative Refactoring in HCL (Terraform 1.1+)\n# 1. Old resource declaration was: resource \"aws_instance\" \"server\"\n# 2. Renamed to: resource \"aws_instance\" \"web\"\nresource \"aws_instance\" \"web\" {\n  ami           = \"ami-0c55b159cbfafe1f0\"\n  instance_type = \"t3.micro\"\n}\n\n# 3. Add moved block to tell Terraform this is a rename, NOT a recreation!\nmoved {\n  from = aws_instance.server\n  to   = aws_instance.web\n}\n\n# Moving a resource into a child module:\n# moved {\n#   from = aws_security_group.app\n#   to   = module.networking.aws_security_group.app\n# }</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Deleting moved blocks too quickly: If you merge a moved block and delete it the next day, any branch, staging environment, or teammate who hasn't applied yet will suffer resource destruction. Keep moved blocks in your codebase for at least 1-2 release cycles.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Staff SRE doctrine: 'Never run <code>terraform state mv</code> manually in team environments. Always use <code>moved</code> blocks in your code. They are declarative, peer-reviewed in pull requests, and execute atomically in CI/CD.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf200_35","level":"mid","question":"What is the purpose of the terraform workspace command and how does it partition state?","tags":["TERRAFORM","Workspaces","CLI","State"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">The <code>terraform workspace</code> command family manages multiple distinct state files associated with a single HCL configuration directory. It allows developers to spin up isolated sandbox environments without modifying code or creating duplicate directories.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>State Storage Paths:</strong></li>\n  <li>• In local backends: stored in <code>terraform.tfstate.d/<WORKSPACE_NAME>/terraform.tfstate</code>.</li>\n  <li>• In AWS S3 backend: stored under the prefix <code>env:/<WORKSPACE_NAME>/<KEY></code>.</li>\n  <li>• The default workspace always writes to the root key without prefix.</li>\n  <li><strong>Key Subcommands:</strong></li>\n  <li>• <code>terraform workspace list</code>: Shows all active workspaces.</li>\n  <li>• <code>terraform workspace new <name></code>: Creates and switches to a new workspace.</li>\n  <li>• <code>terraform workspace select <name></code>: Switches context to an existing workspace.</li>\n  <li>• <code>terraform workspace delete <name></code>: Deletes an empty workspace.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Create an ephemeral testing workspace for feature branch JIRA-104\nterraform workspace new feature-jira-104\n\n# Apply infrastructure for the feature branch\nterraform apply -auto-approve\n\n# Switch back to default workspace when finished\nterraform workspace select default\n\n# Destroy the feature branch infrastructure and remove workspace\nterraform workspace select feature-jira-104\nterraform destroy -auto-approve\nterraform workspace select default\nterraform workspace delete feature-jira-104</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Accidentally applying in the wrong workspace: It is remarkably easy to forget which workspace is active in your terminal shell. Running <code>terraform apply</code> in the default (production) workspace while intending to deploy to dev will cause immediate production drift or outages. Display active workspace in your shell prompt (PS1).</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Production safeguard: 'In HCL code, add a precondition or assertion checking the active workspace: <code>lifecycle { precondition { condition = terraform.workspace != \"default\" error_message = \"Deployments to default workspace are prohibited!\" } }</code>.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf200_36","level":"mid","question":"How do you debug Terraform configurations using TF_LOG and TF_LOG_PATH?","tags":["TERRAFORM","Debugging","Troubleshooting","Logging"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">When Terraform commands fail with cryptic errors, hang indefinitely during resource provisioning, or produce unexpected plan diffs, operators enable detailed logging using the <strong>TF_LOG</strong> and <strong>TF_LOG_PATH</strong> environment variables to inspect raw HTTP requests, gRPC RPC messages, and internal graph calculations.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Log Levels (in increasing verbosity):</strong></li>\n  <li>• <code>ERROR</code>: Fatal errors only.</li>\n  <li>• <code>WARN</code>: Warnings and non-fatal anomalies.</li>\n  <li>• <code>INFO</code>: High-level operational events.</li>\n  <li>• <code>DEBUG</code>: Internal algorithmic state and dependency graph traversal.</li>\n  <li>• <code>TRACE</code>: Raw HTTP request headers, REST payloads, gRPC messages, and full API responses (highest verbosity).</li>\n  <li><strong>Targeted Provider Logging:</strong> Modern Terraform allows logging specific providers: <code>TF_LOG_PROVIDER_AWS=DEBUG</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>File Redirection:</strong> <code>TF_LOG_PATH</code> writes log output directly to a file, preventing terminal buffer overflow.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Enable full trace debugging and redirect output to a log file\nexport TF_LOG=TRACE\nexport TF_LOG_PATH=\"terraform_debug.log\"\n\n# Run command to capture debug trace\nterraform apply -auto-approve\n\n# Search log for raw HTTP error status codes (e.g. 403 Forbidden, 400 Bad Request)\ngrep -E \"HTTP/|status code|Response\" terraform_debug.log\n\n# Disable debugging after troubleshooting\nunset TF_LOG\nunset TF_LOG_PATH</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Leaving TF_LOG=TRACE enabled in CI/CD logs: TRACE level dumps all raw HTTP request bodies into log files, which may include database master passwords, private TLS keys, and cloud API tokens. Always sanitize or delete debug log files immediately after troubleshooting.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">SRE debugging workflow: 'When Terraform hangs for 15+ minutes waiting for a resource (e.g. RDS cluster or AWS ALB), run with <code>TF_LOG_PROVIDER_AWS=DEBUG</code>. You will see the exact polling loop and status checks (e.g. <code>Status: creating, polling in 10s</code>), confirming whether the cloud provider is still working or stuck.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf200_37","level":"junior","question":"What is the difference between local and remote backends in Terraform, and why is remote state mandatory for production?","tags":["TERRAFORM","Backends","State","Enterprise"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">A <strong>Local Backend</strong> stores the state file on the local filesystem (<code>terraform.tfstate</code>), whereas a <strong>Remote Backend</strong> stores the state file in a centralized cloud storage system (S3, GCS, HCP Terraform). Remote state is mandatory for enterprise production to enable team collaboration, prevent state loss, and enforce distributed concurrency locks.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Local Backend Weaknesses:</strong></li>\n  <li>• Single Point of Failure: If the developer's laptop hard drive fails, all infrastructure state is permanently lost.</li>\n  <li>• Concurrency Blindness: No distributed locking. Multiple engineers applying concurrently will corrupt state.</li>\n  <li>• Security Exposure: Plaintext secrets reside unencrypted on local disks.</li>\n  <li><strong>Remote Backend Strengths:</strong></li>\n  <li>• Centralized Team Access: CI/CD runners and engineers access the single authoritative source of truth.</li>\n  <li>• State Locking: Mutex locking via DynamoDB or native cloud APIs prevents simultaneous writes.</li>\n  <li>• Encryption & Versioning: KMS encryption at rest and S3 object versioning enable instant point-in-time recovery.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Production Remote Backend Configuration (S3 + DynamoDB)\nterraform {\n  backend \"s3\" {\n    bucket         = \"corp-iac-state-prod\"\n    key            = \"core/network/terraform.tfstate\"\n    region         = \"us-east-1\"\n    encrypt        = true\n    dynamodb_table = \"terraform-locks\"\n  }\n}</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Committing local terraform.tfstate to Git: When using a local backend, developers often commit <code>terraform.tfstate</code> to GitHub. This leaks AWS secrets to the entire organization and creates Git merge conflicts on every commit that destroy state formatting.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Governance check: 'Add a mandatory pre-commit hook or CI check that fails builds if <code>backend \"local\"</code> is detected in any production repository. Local backends should be restricted to isolated scratch learning sandboxes.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf200_38","level":"mid","question":"How do you handle provider versioning in Terraform using pessimistic constraint operators (~>)?","tags":["TERRAFORM","Providers","Versioning","SemVer"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Cloud providers (like AWS, Azure, GCP) release new provider versions weekly, often deprecating resource arguments or changing default behaviors. To guarantee deterministic deployments while still receiving security patches, Terraform uses <strong>Version Constraints</strong> governed by the <strong>Pessimistic Constraint Operator (<code>~></code>)</strong>.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Pessimistic Constraint Operator (~>):</strong> Allows only the <em>rightmost</em> version component to increment.</li>\n  <li>• <code>~> 5.2.0</code>: Allows <code>>= 5.2.0</code> and <code>< 5.3.0</code> (strictly bug fix patch releases).</li>\n  <li>• <code>~> 5.0</code>: Allows <code>>= 5.0.0</code> and <code>< 6.0.0</code> (allows new features, blocks major breaking changes).</li>\n  <li><strong>Other Constraint Operators:</strong></li>\n  <li>• <code>=</code> (Exact match): <code>version = \"5.20.0\"</code> (Strict locking).</li>\n  <li>• <code>>=</code> (Greater than or equal): <code>version = \">= 5.0.0\"</code>.</li>\n  <li>• Compound: <code>version = \">= 5.10.0, < 6.0.0\"</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Dependency Lock File:</strong> <code>.terraform.lock.hcl</code> records the exact cryptographic SHA checksum of the installed provider binary.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>terraform {\n  required_version = \"&gt;= 1.5.0\"\n\n  required_providers {\n    aws = {\n      source  = \"hashicorp/aws\"\n      # Allows 5.20.1, 5.20.2 ... but BLOCKS 5.21.0\n      version = \"~&gt; 5.20.0\"\n    }\n    kubernetes = {\n      source  = \"hashicorp/kubernetes\"\n      # Allows minor feature releases up to 2.99, but BLOCKS 3.0.0\n      version = \"~&gt; 2.25\"\n    }\n  }\n}</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Using unconstrained version requirements ('>= 1.0'): An unconstrained requirement means running <code>terraform init</code> next month could download a major breaking upgrade (e.g. AWS provider v6.0), breaking syntax across hundreds of resources.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Best practice: 'In root modules (production deployment repos), pin tightly with <code>~> 5.20.0</code> or exact versions. In reusable shared child modules, use broader constraints like <code>~> 5.0</code> to give consumers flexibility.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf200_39","level":"mid","question":"What is the purpose of the terraform refresh command and why is standalone refresh deprecated in favor of -refresh-only?","tags":["TERRAFORM","State","Drift","Deprecated"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Historically, <code>terraform refresh</code> was used to update the local state file with the real-world attributes of cloud resources. However, standalone <code>terraform refresh</code> modified state immediately without giving operators a chance to preview changes. In modern Terraform, standalone refresh is deprecated in favor of <strong><code>terraform plan -refresh-only</code></strong> and <strong><code>terraform apply -refresh-only</code></strong>.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>The Danger of Legacy terraform refresh:</strong> If an external resource was accidentally deleted or unlinked in the cloud console, running <code>terraform refresh</code> immediately wiped it from state with zero confirmation. If you had dependent resources, subsequent applies suffered cascading failures.</li>\n  <li><strong>Modern Refresh-Only Workflow:</strong></li>\n  <li>1. <code>terraform plan -refresh-only</code>: Queries cloud APIs and prints an interactive diff showing exactly what drift occurred in the cloud.</li>\n  <li>2. <code>terraform apply -refresh-only</code>: Interactively prompts the operator: <em>'Do you want to update the state to match these changes?'</em></li>\n  <li>3. Only updates the state file to reflect external cloud changes without touching live infrastructure.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Modern safe workflow: Inspect drift between state and cloud reality\nterraform plan -refresh-only\n\n# Safely update state file to adopt out-of-band changes with human approval\nterraform apply -refresh-only\n\n# Check state after refresh\nterraform state list</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Confusing -refresh-only with a normal apply: <code>terraform apply -refresh-only</code> updates <em>only the state file</em> to match the cloud. It will NEVER modify, create, or delete physical cloud resources.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Production drift reconciliation: 'When an engineer manually updates an EC2 tag or RDS parameter in the AWS console, run <code>terraform plan -refresh-only</code>. If the change is approved, apply refresh-only, then commit the updated attribute into your HCL code to bring code and state into 100% alignment.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf200_40","level":"mid","question":"How do you generate and view a resource graph in Terraform to analyze circular dependencies?","tags":["TERRAFORM","Graph","Architecture","Dependencies"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Terraform's core engine evaluates infrastructure as a <strong>Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG)</strong>. The <code>terraform graph</code> command outputs this internal dependency graph in the standard Graphviz DOT format, allowing engineers to visualize resource relationships and pinpoint cyclic dependency deadlocks.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Graph Execution Engine:</strong> Nodes in the graph represent resources, providers, and modules; edges represent dependencies. Independent nodes are provisioned concurrently.</li>\n  <li><strong>Graph Cycles (Cycle Errors):</strong> Occur when Resource A depends on B, and B depends on A (e.g. a Security Group referencing itself, or a circular module output). Terraform detects cycles during graph construction and halts.</li>\n  <li><strong>Visualizing the Graph:</strong> Pipe the output of <code>terraform graph</code> into the <code>dot</code> utility (from Graphviz) to generate visual SVG or PNG diagrams.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Generate visual dependency graph in PNG format (requires graphviz installed)\nterraform graph | dot -Tpng &gt; dependency_graph.png\n\n# Generate SVG for interactive zoomable browser inspection\nterraform graph | dot -Tsvg &gt; dependency_graph.svg\n\n# Target graph generation for a specific plan\nterraform graph -plan=tfplan | dot -Tpng &gt; plan_graph.png</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Graph explosion in large codebases: Running <code>terraform graph</code> on a monolithic repository with 500 resources generates an unreadable spiderweb diagram of thousands of nodes. Scope graph analysis to specific child modules or sub-directories.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Breaking circular security group cycles: 'If Security Group A needs to allow traffic from SG B, and SG B needs to allow traffic from SG A, do not embed inline ingress blocks. Instead, use standalone <code>aws_security_group_rule</code> resources. This breaks the resource-level dependency cycle in the DAG.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf200_41","level":"senior","question":"What are lifecycle blocks in Terraform (create_before_destroy, prevent_destroy, ignore_changes)?","tags":["TERRAFORM","Lifecycle","ZeroDowntime","Operations"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">The <code>lifecycle</code> meta-argument customizes the fundamental CRUD behavior of individual managed resources. It allows engineers to enforce zero-downtime rollouts, protect mission-critical databases from accidental deletion, and ignore dynamic runtime attribute changes.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>1. create_before_destroy = true:</strong> By default, Terraform destroys the old resource before creating the new one. This flag flips the sequence: provisions the new resource first, verifies creation, re-points references, and then deletes the old resource, eliminating downtime for web servers and TLS certificates.</li>\n  <li><strong>2. prevent_destroy = true:</strong> A client-side guard. If any plan or command attempts to destroy the resource, Terraform halts immediately with an error.</li>\n  <li><strong>3. ignore_changes:</strong> Specifies a list of resource attributes that Terraform will ignore during plan diff calculations, preventing drift conflicts with external systems (e.g. Auto Scaling groups, dynamic tags).</li>\n  <li><strong>4. replace_triggered_by:</strong> Replaces a resource whenever another referenced resource or attribute changes.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>resource \"aws_instance\" \"web\" {\n  ami           = var.ami_id\n  instance_type = \"t3.medium\"\n\n  lifecycle {\n    # Zero-downtime replacement\n    create_before_destroy = true\n\n    # Prevent accidental destruction in production\n    prevent_destroy = false\n\n    # Ignore changes to tags managed out-of-band by AWS Cost Optimizer\n    ignore_changes = [\n      tags[\"LastScanned\"],\n      user_data\n    ]\n  }\n}</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Dependency cycles with create_before_destroy: If Resource A has <code>create_before_destroy = true</code> and depends on Resource B that does NOT have it, Terraform can encounter dependency graph deadlocks. Propagate the flag across dependent resources.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Staff pattern: 'Always pair <code>create_before_destroy = true</code> on Auto Scaling Launch Templates and Security Groups to ensure new infrastructure is healthy and traffic-ready before tearing down the old fleet.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf200_42","level":"mid","question":"How do you ignore changes to specific resource attributes in Terraform to prevent drift conflicts with external systems?","tags":["TERRAFORM","Lifecycle","IgnoreChanges","Drift"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">In modern cloud environments, external automation tools (AWS Auto Scaling, Kubernetes controllers, cost allocators) frequently update cloud resource attributes at runtime. To prevent Terraform from constantly attempting to revert these intentional external modifications on every apply, engineers declare <code>ignore_changes</code> in the resource's <code>lifecycle</code> block.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Targeted Attribute Scoping:</strong> Accepts a list of relative attribute names: <code>ignore_changes = [tags, desired_capacity]</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Map Key Granularity:</strong> Can ignore specific map keys while managing others: <code>ignore_changes = [tags[\"CostCenter\"], tags[\"AutoStop\"]]</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Ignore All Changes:</strong> <code>ignore_changes = all</code> instructs Terraform to create the resource once and never update or modify it again regardless of code or cloud drift.</li>\n  <li><strong>Evaluation:</strong> During <code>terraform plan</code>, Terraform still queries the cloud API, but suppresses any diff calculation for the ignored attributes.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>resource \"aws_autoscaling_group\" \"app_asg\" {\n  name                = \"app-asg\"\n  min_size            = 2\n  max_size            = 10\n  desired_capacity    = 2\n  vpc_zone_identifier = var.subnet_ids\n\n  lifecycle {\n    # Let AWS Target Tracking Auto Scaling adjust desired_capacity dynamically\n    ignore_changes = [\n      desired_capacity,\n      target_group_arns\n    ]\n  }\n}</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Ignoring attributes that should be strictly version-controlled: Using <code>ignore_changes = all</code> indiscriminately masks real security drift (e.g. someone opening security group ports in the AWS console). Use ignore_changes with surgical precision.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Production use case: 'Always apply <code>ignore_changes = [desired_capacity]</code> to Auto Scaling Groups and ECS Services. Otherwise, every CI/CD deployment will reset auto-scaled production fleets back down to initial minimum capacity, causing immediate capacity outages.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf200_43","level":"junior","question":"What is the terraform import command used for and what are its operational limitations?","tags":["TERRAFORM","Import","CLI","Adoption"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\"><code>terraform import</code> is the CLI command used to bind existing, unmanaged cloud resources to an address in your Terraform state file. It enables teams to bring legacy click-ops infrastructure under Infrastructure as Code without recreating running resources.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Syntax:</strong> <code>terraform import <RESOURCE_TYPE>.<RESOURCE_NAME> <CLOUD_RESOURCE_ID></code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Operation:</strong> Calls the provider's <code>Read</code> API method using the provided cloud ID, populating the local/remote state file with real-world attributes.</li>\n  <li><strong>Limitations of Legacy CLI Import:</strong></li>\n  <li>• Does NOT generate HCL code: You must manually write the matching HCL block in your <code>.tf</code> files beforehand.</li>\n  <li>• Immediate State Mutation: Modifies the state file on disk immediately without a plan or confirmation prompt.</li>\n  <li>• Replaced in Terraform 1.5+ by declarative <code>import { ... }</code> blocks.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Step 1: Manually define the shell resource in main.tf\n# resource \"aws_s3_bucket\" \"legacy_bucket\" {\n#   bucket = \"corp-legacy-data-2022\"\n# }\n\n# Step 2: Import the physical AWS bucket into state\nterraform import aws_s3_bucket.legacy_bucket corp-legacy-data-2022\n\n# Step 3: Run plan to verify zero diff between code and state\nterraform plan</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Attribute mismatch after import: If the manually written HCL block differs from the imported cloud state, the next <code>terraform plan</code> will attempt to update or replace the resource. Always run <code>terraform plan</code> immediately after import to reconcile attributes.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Modern alternative: 'Avoid <code>terraform import</code> on the CLI in modern projects. Use Terraform 1.5+ declarative <code>import</code> blocks with <code>terraform plan -generate-config-out=generated.tf</code> to have Terraform write the HCL for you.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf200_44","level":"mid","question":"How do you use output values across different modules in Terraform architectures?","tags":["TERRAFORM","Outputs","Modules","Architecture"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Cross-module data sharing is essential for modular infrastructure. Child modules cannot directly access each other's resources. Instead, child modules expose data via <strong>output values</strong>, which are referenced by the parent module and passed as input variables into downstream sibling modules.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>The Pipeline Flow:</strong></li>\n  <li>1. <em>Child Module A (VPC):</em> Creates subnets and declares: <code>output \"private_subnets\" { value = aws_subnet.private[*].id }</code>.</li>\n  <li>2. <em>Parent Module (Root):</em> References the output using the module syntax: <code>module.vpc.private_subnets</code>.</li>\n  <li>3. <em>Child Module B (EKS):</em> Declares an input variable: <code>variable \"subnet_ids\" {}</code>.</li>\n  <li>4. <em>Parent Module:</em> Passes the reference: <code>module \"eks\" { subnet_ids = module.vpc.private_subnets }</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Implicit Dependency Linking:</strong> Referencing <code>module.vpc.<output></code> automatically establishes an implicit dependency, guaranteeing Terraform builds the VPC before starting the EKS cluster.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># In Root Module (main.tf):\nmodule \"vpc\" {\n  source     = \"./modules/vpc\"\n  cidr_block = \"10.0.0.0/16\"\n}\n\nmodule \"database\" {\n  source         = \"./modules/rds\"\n  vpc_id         = module.vpc.vpc_id\n  db_subnet_ids  = module.vpc.private_subnets\n  db_name        = \"production_db\"\n}</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Exposing whole resource blocks in outputs: Avoid <code>output \"vpc\" { value = aws_vpc.main }</code>. Exporting the entire raw resource leaks internal provider schema details and can expose sensitive tokens. Export only the specific attributes consumers need.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Clean architecture: 'Always write clear <code>description</code> attributes on module outputs. This automatically generates self-documenting code when using <code>terraform-docs</code>.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf200_45","level":"junior","question":"What is the difference between terraform output CLI commands and module output values in Terraform configurations?","tags":["TERRAFORM","Outputs","CLI","Integration"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">While both revolve around the <code>output</code> HCL construct, <strong>module output values</strong> are internal HCL references used within code for cross-module communication, whereas <strong>terraform output CLI commands</strong> are shell-level tools used by operators and CI/CD pipelines to query state.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Module Outputs (HCL Level):</strong> Declared inside child modules to export values up to parent callers. Evaluated during graph processing; not accessible on the CLI unless re-exported by the root module.</li>\n  <li><strong>Root Outputs (CLI Level):</strong> Declared in the root working directory. Displayed on the console upon apply completion and stored in the root state file.</li>\n  <li><strong>CLI Subcommand Queries:</strong></li>\n  <li>• <code>terraform output</code>: Prints all root outputs in formatted text.</li>\n  <li>• <code>terraform output -json</code>: Generates structured JSON for CI scripts and automation.</li>\n  <li>• <code>terraform output -raw <name></code>: Returns unquoted raw string (ideal for piping into environment variables).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Extract raw database endpoint for Ansible deployment script\nDB_HOST=$(terraform output -raw rds_endpoint)\n\n# Parse JSON outputs in bash pipeline using jq\nEKS_CLUSTER_NAME=$(terraform output -json | jq -r .cluster_name.value)\n\n# Query sensitive output explicitly (bypasses redact mask)\nterraform output -raw db_master_password</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Attempting to query child module outputs directly via CLI: Running <code>terraform output module.vpc.vpc_id</code> will fail. The CLI can ONLY query outputs defined in the root module. You must create a pass-through root output if you need CLI access.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">CI/CD pattern: 'In continuous delivery pipelines, export outputs directly to environment files: <code>terraform output -json > tf_env.json</code>. Downstream deployment stages can consume infrastructure endpoints without re-running Terraform.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf200_46","level":"mid","question":"What are dynamic blocks in Terraform and how do you cleanly generate repeated nested structures?","tags":["TERRAFORM","DynamicBlocks","HCL","Iteration"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">A <strong>Dynamic Block</strong> constructs repeated nested configuration blocks inside top-level resources or data sources based on a collection (list or map). It acts like a <code>for_each</code> loop scoped specifically to nested blocks (such as <code>ingress</code> rules in Security Groups or <code>setting</code> blocks).</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Syntax Anatomy:</strong></li>\n  <li>• <code>dynamic \"<BLOCK_TYPE>\" { ... }</code>: Declares the nested block name to generate.</li>\n  <li>• <code>for_each = <COLLECTION></code>: The list or map to iterate over.</li>\n  <li>• <code>iterator = <CUSTOM_NAME></code>: Optional custom iterator name (defaults to block name).</li>\n  <li>• <code>content { ... }</code>: The body of the block, accessing attributes via <code><iterator>.value</code> and <code><iterator>.key</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>variable \"security_rules\" {\n  type = list(object({\n    port        = number\n    protocol    = string\n    cidr_blocks = list(string)\n    description = string\n  }))\n  default = [\n    { port = 443, protocol = \"tcp\", cidr_blocks = [\"0.0.0.0/0\"], description = \"HTTPS\" },\n    { port = 80,  protocol = \"tcp\", cidr_blocks = [\"0.0.0.0/0\"], description = \"HTTP\" }\n  ]\n}\n\nresource \"aws_security_group\" \"alb\" {\n  name   = \"alb-sg\"\n  vpc_id = aws_vpc.main.id\n\n  dynamic \"ingress\" {\n    for_each = var.security_rules\n    content {\n      from_port   = ingress.value.port\n      to_port     = ingress.value.port\n      protocol    = ingress.value.protocol\n      cidr_blocks = ingress.value.cidr_blocks\n      description = ingress.value.description\n    }\n  }\n}</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Dynamic blocks do not work on top-level resources: You cannot write <code>dynamic \"aws_instance\"</code>. Dynamic blocks only operate on nested sub-blocks within an existing resource. For top-level resources, use <code>for_each</code>.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Maintainability rule: 'Use dynamic blocks sparingly. While powerful, deeply nested dynamic blocks can make code hard to read and debug. If a resource requires 10 complex rules, consider standalone resources like <code>aws_security_group_rule</code>.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf200_47","level":"junior","question":"How do you define and use maps and objects in Terraform for structured variable inputs?","tags":["TERRAFORM","Types","HCL","Variables"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Terraform provides structured data types to group related attributes into cohesive units. <strong>Maps</strong> represent collections of key-value pairs of identical types, while <strong>Objects</strong> represent complex structured records where different keys can have distinct types (string, number, list, bool).</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Map Type (Homogeneous):</strong> <code>map(string)</code> or <code>map(number)</code>. All values must share the same type.</li>\n  <li><strong>Object Type (Heterogeneous):</strong> <code>object({ name = string, port = number, enabled = bool })</code>. Each key can have a distinct, enforced schema.</li>\n  <li><strong>Optional Attributes (Terraform 1.3+):</strong> Use <code>optional(<TYPE>, <DEFAULT>)</code> to allow callers to omit non-essential fields in an object schema.</li>\n  <li><strong>Lookup Operations:</strong> Accessed via dot notation (<code>var.config.port</code>) or the <code>lookup()</code> function with fallback defaults.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Modern structured object variable with optional attributes (Terraform 1.3+)\nvariable \"database_cluster\" {\n  type = object({\n    cluster_name      = string\n    instance_class    = string\n    node_count        = number\n    allocated_storage = optional(number, 50)\n    deletion_protect  = optional(bool, true)\n    backup_retention  = optional(number, 7)\n  })\n\n  default = {\n    cluster_name   = \"prod-aurora\"\n    instance_class = \"db.r6g.xlarge\"\n    node_count     = 3\n  }\n}\n\nresource \"aws_rds_cluster\" \"db\" {\n  cluster_identifier  = var.database_cluster.cluster_name\n  deletion_protection = var.database_cluster.deletion_protect\n  backup_retention_period = var.database_cluster.backup_retention\n}</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Type coercion failures in maps: In a <code>map(string)</code>, passing <code>{ port = 8080 }</code> will attempt type conversion, but passing a nested list will fail immediately. Use <code>object</code> whenever fields have diverse types.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">API design standard: 'Always use <code>object()</code> with <code>optional()</code> defaults for complex module configuration variables. It provides full schema validation, autocompletion in IDEs, and clean default fallbacks.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf200_48","level":"junior","question":"What is the count parameter in Terraform and what are its key limitations compared to for_each?","tags":["TERRAFORM","Iteration","Count","HCL"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\"><code>count</code> is a resource meta-argument that instructs Terraform to create a specified whole number of identical resource instances. While simple to implement, <code>count</code> uses integer array indexing, making it dangerous when resources in the middle of a list are modified or deleted.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Index-Based Addressing:</strong> Resources are recorded in state as an ordered array: <code>aws_instance.server[0]</code>, <code>aws_instance.server[1]</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Current Index Access:</strong> The current integer index is accessible inside the block via <code>count.index</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Index-Shifting Hazard:</strong> If you use count to iterate over a list of names, removing an element from the middle of the list causes all subsequent array indices to shift by -1. Terraform interprets this as attribute changes across every shifted resource, destroying and recreating innocent production systems.</li>\n  <li><strong>Valid Use Cases for count:</strong></li>\n  <li>• Boolean conditional creation: <code>count = var.create_resources ? 1 : 0</code>.</li>\n  <li>• Truly homogeneous, interchangeable workers (e.g. 5 identical stateless web runners).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Safe usage of count: Conditional Feature Toggle\nvariable \"enable_bastion\" {\n  type    = bool\n  default = false\n}\n\nresource \"aws_instance\" \"bastion\" {\n  count         = var.enable_bastion ? 1 : 0\n  ami           = \"ami-0c55b159cbfafe1f0\"\n  instance_type = \"t3.micro\"\n\n  tags = {\n    Name = \"bastion-host\"\n  }\n}</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Using count for named resources: Never do <code>count = length(var.users)</code> for IAM users or subnets. Deleting the first user in the list will cause Terraform to destroy and recreate every single user in the organization. Always use <code>for_each</code>.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Best practice rule: 'Use <code>count</code> ONLY for boolean feature toggles (0 or 1). For everything else that represents a list or map of distinct items, use <code>for_each</code>.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf200_49","level":"senior","question":"What are Terraform Cloud and Terraform Enterprise, and what capabilities do they add over open-source CLI?","tags":["TERRAFORM","TerraformCloud","Enterprise","Governance"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">While the open-source Terraform CLI provides core provisioning mechanics, <strong>HCP Terraform (formerly Terraform Cloud)</strong> and self-hosted <strong>Terraform Enterprise (TFE)</strong> provide enterprise collaboration, policy enforcement, security compliance, and automated orchestration platforms for managing IaC at scale.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Key Capabilities Beyond Open Source:</strong></li>\n  <li>1. <strong>Remote State & Concurrency Management:</strong> Managed state storage with automatic locking, versioning, and zero infrastructure maintenance.</li>\n  <li>2. <strong>Remote Execution Runners:</strong> Executes plan and apply in isolated Linux containers triggered via Git webhooks, PR comments, or CLI.</li>\n  <li>3. <strong>Policy as Code (Sentinel / OPA):</strong> Automated compliance guardrails that evaluate plans before apply (e.g. blocking unencrypted S3 buckets or enforcing budget caps).</li>\n  <li>4. <strong>Private Module Registry (PMR):</strong> Internal catalog for sharing verified, versioned modules across corporate business units.</li>\n  <li>5. <strong>Cost Estimation & Run Tasks:</strong> Analyzes resource diffs to display projected monthly cloud cost changes and integrates with tools like Infracost and Snyk.</li>\n  <li>6. <strong>Drift Detection:</strong> Continuous background health checks that detect out-of-band cloud changes automatically.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Connecting open-source CLI to Terraform Cloud\nterraform {\n  cloud {\n    organization = \"enterprise-fintech\"\n\n    workspaces {\n      tags = [\"production\", \"aws\"]\n    }\n  }\n}</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Cost explosion of per-managed-resource pricing: HCP Terraform pricing is based on Managed Resources (RUM). Unmonitored ephemeral test clusters creating thousands of short-lived resources can trigger unexpected licensing bills.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Executive evaluation: 'If your engineering organization has >20 engineers writing Terraform, HCP Terraform, Spacelift, or Atlantis is essential to prevent conflicting local CLI applies, enforce policy guardrails, and provide audit logging for SOC2 compliance.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf200_50","level":"mid","question":"How do you configure and secure an S3 remote backend with DynamoDB locking and KMS encryption?","tags":["TERRAFORM","Backends","S3","Security","DynamoDB"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">The combination of <strong>Amazon S3</strong> and <strong>Amazon DynamoDB</strong> is the gold standard for managing self-hosted remote state in AWS. S3 provides scalable, durable storage for the <code>.tfstate</code> JSON file, while DynamoDB provides distributed mutex locking to prevent concurrent apply collisions.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>S3 Requirements:</strong></li>\n  <li>• Server-Side Encryption (SSE-KMS) with Customer Managed Keys.</li>\n  <li>• S3 Object Versioning enabled for disaster recovery.</li>\n  <li>• S3 Block Public Access enabled at bucket and account level.</li>\n  <li>• Bucket policy enforcing TLS 1.2+ (<code>aws:SecureTransport = true</code>).</li>\n  <li><strong>DynamoDB Table Requirements:</strong></li>\n  <li>• Primary Partition Key MUST be named exactly <code>LockID</code> of type <code>String</code>.</li>\n  <li>• Pay-Per-Request (On-Demand) billing to eliminate idle capacity costs.</li>\n  <li><strong>Terraform S3 Backend Parameters:</strong> <code>bucket</code>, <code>key</code>, <code>region</code>, <code>encrypt = true</code>, and <code>dynamodb_table</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># 1. Backend declaration in HCL configuration\nterraform {\n  backend \"s3\" {\n    bucket         = \"corp-tfstate-production-us-east-1\"\n    key            = \"networking/terraform.tfstate\"\n    region         = \"us-east-1\"\n    encrypt        = true\n    dynamodb_table = \"terraform-state-lock\"\n  }\n}\n\n# 2. Dedicated DynamoDB Table for Distributed Locking\nresource \"aws_dynamodb_table\" \"tf_lock\" {\n  name         = \"terraform-state-lock\"\n  billing_mode = \"PAY_PER_REQUEST\"\n  hash_key     = \"LockID\"\n\n  attribute {\n    name = \"LockID\"\n    type = \"S\"\n  }\n\n  tags = {\n    Name = \"Terraform State Lock Table\"\n  }\n}</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Typo in DynamoDB Partition Key: Naming the primary hash key <code>id</code> or <code>lock_id</code> instead of <code>LockID</code> will cause <code>terraform init</code> to fail with: <code>ValidationException: The provided key element does not match the schema</code>.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Bootstrap chicken-and-egg solution: 'How do you create the S3 state bucket and DynamoDB lock table? Use a standalone 'bootstrap' Terraform project that uses a local backend to provision the S3 bucket and DynamoDB table once. Then migrate the bootstrap project itself to the remote S3 backend.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf200_51","level":"junior","question":"What is the terraform fmt command used for and how do you enforce formatting in CI/CD pipelines?","tags":["TERRAFORM","CLI","Formatting","CI/CD"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\"><code>terraform fmt</code> is the official code formatting tool built directly into the Terraform CLI. It scans <code>.tf</code> and <code>.tfvars</code> files and rewrites them into canonical HashiCorp style, standardizing indentation, spacing, and alignment across team codebases.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>In-Place Formatting:</strong> Running <code>terraform fmt</code> updates files on disk in-place.</li>\n  <li><strong>Recursive Scanning:</strong> The <code>-recursive</code> flag formats files in all subdirectories and child modules.</li>\n  <li><strong>CI Verification (-check):</strong> The <code>-check</code> flag runs in non-mutating mode. It returns exit code <strong>0</strong> if all files conform, or exit code <strong>3</strong> if any file needs formatting (printing the list of offending files).</li>\n  <li><strong>Diff Inspection (-diff):</strong> Displays the exact line-by-line whitespace diff.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Format all files across entire repository recursively\nterraform fmt -recursive\n\n# CI/CD Pipeline Step: Block PR if unformatted files exist\nterraform fmt -check -recursive -diff\nif [ $? -ne 0 ]; then\n  echo \"Error: Unformatted Terraform files detected. Run 'terraform fmt -recursive' locally!\"\n  exit 1\nfi</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Forgetting the -recursive flag: Running plain <code>terraform fmt</code> only formats the current working directory, leaving child modules in <code>./modules/</code> unformatted.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Developer workflow: 'Configure your IDE (VS Code, IntelliJ) to run <code>terraform fmt</code> automatically on file save. This ensures you never fail CI formatting checks.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf200_52","level":"mid","question":"How does the dependency lock file (.terraform.lock.hcl) work and why should it be checked into Git?","tags":["TERRAFORM","Security","LockFile","SupplyChain"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Introduced in Terraform 0.14, the <strong>Dependency Lock File</strong> (<code>.terraform.lock.hcl</code>) records the exact provider versions and cryptographic checksum hashes (hashes for macOS, Linux, Windows) used by your configuration. It is a critical defense against supply chain attacks and guarantees reproducible builds across machines.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Creation & Updates:</strong> Generated automatically during <code>terraform init</code>. When a provider is installed, Terraform calculates and records its cryptographic SHA256 checksum.</li>\n  <li><strong>Cryptographic Checksum Verification:</strong> On subsequent <code>terraform init</code> runs (e.g. on CI runners), Terraform verifies that the downloaded provider binary matches the exact hash recorded in <code>.terraform.lock.hcl</code>. If a binary is altered upstream, initialization fails with a security alert.</li>\n  <li><strong>Cross-Platform Pre-population:</strong> Running <code>terraform providers lock -platform=linux_amd64 -platform=darwin_arm64</code> pre-computes hashes for all developer and CI architectures.</li>\n  <li><strong>Source Control Requirement:</strong> Must be committed to Git alongside <code>.tf</code> files.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Commit lock file to Git\ngit add .terraform.lock.hcl\ngit commit -m \"chore: lock provider dependencies\"\n\n# Pre-populate lock file checksums for multiple OS architectures\nterraform providers lock \\\n    -platform=linux_amd64 \\\n    -platform=linux_arm64 \\\n    -platform=darwin_arm64 \\\n    -platform=windows_amd64</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Adding .terraform.lock.hcl to .gitignore: Ignoring the lock file strips your CI/CD pipelines of cryptographic supply chain verification, allowing compromised or drifted provider binaries to execute in production.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">CI/CD speed & security: 'Always check in <code>.terraform.lock.hcl</code>. When running <code>terraform init</code> on CI runners, use <code>terraform init -input=false</code> to ensure it runs strictly against verified locked provider checksums.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf200_54","level":"senior","question":"How do you manage secrets in Terraform without exposing credentials in plaintext?","tags":["TERRAFORM","Security","Secrets","Vault"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Terraform configurations frequently need to configure passwords, API keys, and TLS certificates. Securing secrets requires a defense-in-depth approach to prevent credentials from leaking into Git repositories, terminal logs, or unauthorized state file reads.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>1. Mark Input Variables Sensitive:</strong> <code>variable \"token\" { sensitive = true }</code> redacts values from CLI logs and plans (displaying <code>(sensitive value)</code>).</li>\n  <li><strong>2. Dynamic Secrets Retrieval:</strong> Query secrets dynamically from external secret managers (HashiCorp Vault, AWS Secrets Manager, Azure Key Vault) at runtime rather than storing them in variables.</li>\n  <li><strong>3. Short-Lived Ephemeral Credentials:</strong> Use HashiCorp Vault dynamic database secrets that generate temporary credentials with a 1-hour TTL, or use IAM database authentication.</li>\n  <li><strong>4. State File Lockdown:</strong> Store state in an encrypted S3 bucket with KMS CMK and restrict IAM permissions.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Read dynamic short-lived database credentials from HashiCorp Vault\nprovider \"vault\" {\n  address = \"https://vault.corp.internal:8200\"\n}\n\ndata \"vault_generic_secret\" \"db_creds\" {\n  path = \"secret/data/production/database\"\n}\n\nresource \"aws_db_instance\" \"app_db\" {\n  allocated_storage = 50\n  engine            = \"postgres\"\n  username          = data.vault_generic_secret.db_creds.data[\"username\"]\n  password          = data.vault_generic_secret.db_creds.data[\"password\"]\n  instance_class    = \"db.r6g.large\"\n}</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Hardcoding secrets in local .tfvars files: Even if <code>terraform.tfvars</code> is gitignored, developers can easily push it by accident or share it insecurely over Slack. Inject values via environment variables or secret managers.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Architectural standard: 'Whenever possible, eliminate static passwords completely. Use IAM Roles for Service Accounts (IRSA) in EKS, RDS IAM Authentication, and instance profile roles so applications and Terraform authenticate via IAM tokens rather than passwords.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf200_55","level":"junior","question":"What is the terraform console command used for and how do you test HCL functions interactively?","tags":["TERRAFORM","CLI","Console","Debugging"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\"><code>terraform console</code> provides an interactive Read-Eval-Print Loop (REPL) shell for experimenting with HCL expressions, testing built-in functions, inspecting current state values, and validating interpolation syntax before writing them into code.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Interactive REPL:</strong> Launches a command prompt that loads the current state and variable definitions in read-only mode.</li>\n  <li><strong>Function Testing:</strong> Test complex string manipulations (<code>split</code>, <code>join</code>, <code>replace</code>), collection transforms (<code>flatten</code>, <code>merge</code>, <code>slice</code>), and IP math (<code>cidrsubnet</code>).</li>\n  <li><strong>State Inspection:</strong> Query real attributes of managed resources in memory (e.g. typing <code>aws_vpc.main.id</code> prints the VPC ID).</li>\n  <li><strong>Safe Read-Only Environment:</strong> Does not mutate state or execute cloud API calls.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Launch the interactive Terraform REPL shell\nterraform console\n\n# Inside the console: Test IP subnet CIDR calculations\n&gt; cidrsubnet(\"10.0.0.0/16\", 8, 1)\n\"10.0.1.0/24\"\n\n# Test JSON encoding and string transformations\n&gt; upper(trimspace(\"   production-api  \"))\n\"PRODUCTION-API\"\n\n# Query resources in the active state\n&gt; aws_subnet.public[*].id\n[\n  \"subnet-01234567\",\n  \"subnet-89abcdef\",\n]</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Console requires state locking: When running in a directory connected to a remote backend with DynamoDB locking, launching <code>terraform console</code> acquires a state lock, blocking other users or CI/CD pipelines from applying until you exit the console.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">SRE debugging trick: 'Use <code>terraform console</code> to troubleshoot complex <code>for</code> expressions and <code>flatten()</code> operations before embedding them into <code>for_each</code> loops. It saves dozens of failed <code>terraform plan</code> iterations.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf200_56","level":"junior","question":"How do you reference data sources in Terraform to query dynamic cloud metadata?","tags":["TERRAFORM","DataSources","HCL","Metadata"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Data sources allow Terraform configurations to query and consume metadata from external cloud platforms. They are referenced using the <code>data.<TYPE>.<NAME>.<ATTRIBUTE></code> syntax, enabling dynamic discovery of AMIs, VPCs, subnets, and IAM roles without hardcoding static IDs.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Definition Syntax:</strong> <code>data \"<PROVIDER_TYPE>\" \"<NAME>\" { ... }</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Reference Syntax:</strong> <code>data.<TYPE>.<NAME>.<ATTRIBUTE></code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Execution Timing:</strong> Evaluated during the <code>terraform plan</code> phase. If all input arguments to the data source are known, it queries the cloud API immediately. If arguments depend on uncreated resources, query execution is deferred until <code>apply</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Filtering:</strong> Most data sources support <code>filter</code> blocks to match cloud tags or attributes.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Query available AWS Availability Zones in the current region\ndata \"aws_availability_zones\" \"available\" {\n  state = \"available\"\n}\n\n# Query existing shared VPC managed by the Network Team\ndata \"aws_vpc\" \"shared_network\" {\n  tags = {\n    Environment = \"production\"\n    ManagedBy   = \"platform-team\"\n  }\n}\n\n# Reference discovered attributes in a new resource\nresource \"aws_subnet\" \"app\" {\n  vpc_id            = data.aws_vpc.shared_network.id\n  cidr_block        = \"10.0.50.0/24\"\n  availability_zone = data.aws_availability_zones.available.names[0]\n}</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Data sources that return 0 or multiple results: If a data query matches zero resources or returns multiple resources when only one was expected, Terraform halts with an error during planning. Ensure filter tags are uniquely identifying.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Enterprise pattern: 'Always discover Availability Zones using <code>data \"aws_availability_zones\"</code> rather than hardcoding <code>us-east-1a</code>. Some AWS accounts have AZ naming randomization where your <code>us-east-1a</code> is physically a different datacenter than another account's <code>us-east-1a</code>.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf200_57","level":"senior","question":"What is the purpose of the terraform state mv command and how does it prevent resource recreation during refactoring?","tags":["TERRAFORM","State","Refactoring","ZeroDowntime"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">When refactoring Terraform code (renaming resources, moving resources into child modules, or changing module namespace), Terraform matches resources strictly by their <strong>resource address string</strong>. If the address changes, Terraform treats the old resource as deleted and the new one as created. <code>terraform state mv</code> updates the resource's address inside the state file, preventing destructive teardown.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>State Address Mapping:</strong> Moves the state record from <code><source_address></code> to <code><destination_address></code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Zero Cloud Impact:</strong> Makes zero cloud API calls; does not touch physical cloud infrastructure.</li>\n  <li><strong>Cross-Module Moving:</strong> Moves top-level resources into child modules: <code>terraform state mv aws_security_group.app module.network.aws_security_group.app</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Modern Alternative:</strong> Superseded in Terraform 1.1+ by declarative <code>moved</code> blocks in HCL code.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Safe procedure: Backup state first\nterraform state pull &gt; state_pre_refactor.json\n\n# Move standalone resource into a newly created child module\nterraform state mv aws_instance.web module.compute.aws_instance.web\n\n# Move a resource from an array index to a for_each map key\nterraform state mv 'aws_subnet.public[0]' 'aws_subnet.public[\"us-east-1a\"]'\n\n# Verify plan shows ZERO changes to infrastructure\nterraform plan</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Moving resources without updating HCL code: If you run <code>terraform state mv</code> but forget to update the corresponding resource declaration in your <code>.tf</code> files, the next plan will attempt to destroy the newly moved resource! State and code must match.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Refactoring rule of thumb: 'After running state mv, <code>terraform plan</code> must output: <code>No changes. Your infrastructure matches the configuration.</code> If it shows any creates or destroys, your address mapping was incorrect.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf200_59","level":"mid","question":"How do you diagnose and untaint a resource using terraform untaint or -replace?","tags":["TERRAFORM","Taint","Untaint","Operations"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">When a resource is marked as <strong>tainted</strong> (due to a failed provisioner or an explicit <code>terraform taint</code> command), Terraform flags it in the state file to be destroyed and recreated on the next apply. If the failure was transient or you manually fixed the resource, you can clear the taint using <code>terraform untaint</code>.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Taint Status in State:</strong> In <code>terraform.tfstate</code>, tainted resources have <code>\"status\": \"tainted\"</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>terraform untaint:</strong> Clears the tainted flag, restoring the resource to normal managed status so it will not be destroyed.</li>\n  <li><strong>Diagnosis:</strong> Inspect the state with <code>terraform state show <address></code> or run <code>terraform plan</code> to see if a resource has the <code>-/+ (replace)</code> action due to taint.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Inspect resource in state to check for tainted status\nterraform state show aws_instance.worker\n\n# Untaint the resource to prevent recreation\nterraform untaint aws_instance.worker\n\n# Verify plan no longer schedules resource for destruction\nterraform plan</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Untainting a broken VM: If a VM was tainted because an essential configuration script failed halfway through, untainting it leaves an incomplete, non-functional node running in production. Only untaint if you manually remediated the issue.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Modern replacement: 'In modern Terraform (>= 0.15), avoid using taint altogether. Use <code>terraform plan -replace=\"aws_instance.worker\"</code> for controlled, previewable replacements.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf200_75","level":"senior","question":"How do you secure state files in Terraform against unauthorized access and data loss?","tags":["TERRAFORM","Security","State","DisasterRecovery"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Terraform state files represent the crown jewels of cloud infrastructure: they contain full architectural schematics and sensitive unencrypted credentials. Securing state requires implementing <strong>encryption, immutable versioning, least-privilege IAM policies, and audit logging</strong>.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>1. Server-Side Encryption (KMS CMK):</strong> Enforce AWS KMS customer managed key encryption on the S3 bucket. Keys should have independent rotation policies.</li>\n  <li><strong>2. S3 Object Versioning:</strong> Retains all historical revisions of state files, protecting against accidental deletion, corrupted applies, or malicious tampering.</li>\n  <li><strong>3. S3 Bucket Policies & TLS:</strong> Deny unencrypted HTTP requests (<code>aws:SecureTransport = false</code>) and enforce strict bucket ACL blocks.</li>\n  <li><strong>4. IAM RBAC & OIDC:</strong> Restrict S3 read/write access to automated CI/CD execution service principals (e.g. GitHub Actions OIDC roles). Block human developer IAM users from downloading state files.</li>\n  <li><strong>5. S3 Server Access Logging:</strong> Log all S3 GET/PUT requests to a dedicated security audit bucket monitored by SIEM tools.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># S3 Bucket resource with mandatory security configurations\nresource \"aws_s3_bucket\" \"state\" {\n  bucket = \"enterprise-tf-state-locked\"\n\n  lifecycle {\n    prevent_destroy = true\n  }\n}\n\nresource \"aws_s3_bucket_versioning\" \"versioning\" {\n  bucket = aws_s3_bucket.state.id\n  versioning_configuration {\n    status = \"Enabled\"\n  }\n}\n\nresource \"aws_s3_bucket_server_side_encryption_configuration\" \"kms_enc\" {\n  bucket = aws_s3_bucket.state.id\n  rule {\n    apply_server_side_encryption_by_default {\n      kms_master_key_id = aws_kms_key.state_key.arn\n      sse_algorithm     = \"aws:kms\"\n    }\n  }\n}</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Forgetting to protect the DynamoDB Lock Table: While S3 contains the data, DynamoDB controls write locks. If an attacker deletes the DynamoDB table, concurrent pipelines can overwrite each other's state files simultaneously.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Enterprise governance: 'Place Terraform state buckets in a dedicated, isolated AWS Core/Security account separate from development and production application accounts. This prevents compromised production EC2 instances or developers from accessing state files.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf200_83","level":"senior","question":"How do you handle provider dependencies and multi-region provider configurations in Terraform?","tags":["TERRAFORM","Providers","MultiRegion","Aliases"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Production architectures often span multiple geographic cloud regions (e.g., primary in <code>us-east-1</code>, disaster recovery in <code>us-west-2</code>, CloudFront ACM certificates in <code>us-east-1</code>). Terraform handles this using <strong>Provider Aliases</strong> and explicit provider passing to child modules.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Default vs. Aliased Providers:</strong></li>\n  <li>• <em>Default Provider:</em> The unaliased provider block used by all resources unless specified otherwise.</li>\n  <li>• <em>Aliased Provider:</em> Declares an <code>alias</code> label (e.g. <code>alias = \"west\"</code>).</li>\n  <li><strong>Resource Targeting:</strong> Resources specify non-default providers using the <code>provider</code> meta-argument: <code>provider = aws.west</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Passing Providers to Child Modules:</strong> Inside the <code>module</code> block, map parent providers to child provider requirements using the <code>providers = { aws = aws.west }</code> configuration map.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Default Primary Region Provider\nprovider \"aws\" {\n  region = \"us-east-1\"\n}\n\n# Alternate Region Provider via Alias\nprovider \"aws\" {\n  alias  = \"west\"\n  region = \"us-west-2\"\n}\n\n# Deploying a primary VPC in us-east-1\nresource \"aws_vpc\" \"primary\" {\n  cidr_block = \"10.0.0.0/16\"\n}\n\n# Deploying a DR VPC in us-west-2 using the aliased provider\nresource \"aws_vpc\" \"disaster_recovery\" {\n  provider   = aws.west\n  cidr_block = \"10.1.0.0/16\"\n}</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">CloudFront ACM Certificates Must Be in us-east-1: A classic gotcha in AWS is creating an AWS CloudFront distribution in any region, but forgetting that the SSL/TLS certificate in AWS Certificate Manager (ACM) MUST reside in <code>us-east-1</code>. You must use a provider alias targeting us-east-1 for the ACM certificate.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Module provider passing best practice: 'In reusable child modules, never hardcode provider blocks. Always declare provider requirements in <code>terraform { required_providers { ... } }</code> and let the parent root module pass aliased providers via <code>providers = { aws = aws.west }</code>.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf200_85","level":"mid","question":"How do you safely execute terraform destroy with target flags and protection guards?","tags":["TERRAFORM","CLI","Destroy","Targeting","Safeguards"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Running an unconstrained <code>terraform destroy</code> in production is the ultimate catastrophic mistake. Safe operational practices require using <strong>targeted destruction (<code>-target</code>)</strong> for isolated decommissioning, combined with HCL <code>prevent_destroy</code> lifecycle guards and IAM role restrictions.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Targeted Destruction:</strong> <code>terraform destroy -target=<RESOURCE_ADDRESS></code> computes a destruction plan strictly for that resource and any downstream resources that depend on it.</li>\n  <li><strong>Planning a Destruction First:</strong> Always generate a destruction plan file: <code>terraform plan -destroy -out=destroy.tfplan</code>. Review the diff thoroughly before executing.</li>\n  <li><strong>Lifecycle Protection Guard:</strong> Resources with <code>prevent_destroy = true</code> cause Terraform to immediately reject the destroy command with an error.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Step 1: Preview targeted destruction in a reviewable plan file\nterraform plan -destroy -target=\"aws_instance.legacy_test\" -out=destroy.tfplan\n\n# Step 2: Review destruction plan\nterraform show destroy.tfplan\n\n# Step 3: Apply the approved targeted destruction\nterraform apply destroy.tfplan</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Cascading destruction with -target: If you run <code>terraform destroy -target=aws_vpc.main</code>, Terraform will also destroy all subnets, route tables, internet gateways, NAT gateways, and EC2 instances inside that VPC! Review the plan carefully to inspect downstream cascading deletions.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">CI/CD safeguard: 'Completely disable the <code>terraform destroy</code> command in automated CI/CD runners. Decommissioning production environments should require an explicit, multi-person approved manual pipeline with separate IAM credentials.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf200_96","level":"senior","question":"How do you manage secrets in Terraform configurations without exposing credentials in plaintext?","tags":["TERRAFORM","Security","Secrets","Vault","IAM"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Securing secrets in enterprise Terraform configurations requires eliminating static plaintext credentials across the entire lifecycle: code, CI/CD runners, and state storage. Modern architectures combine <strong>ambient identity (OIDC), runtime secrets retrieval, sensitive variable masking, and KMS envelope encryption</strong>.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>1. Ambient Authentication (No Static Keys):</strong> CI/CD runners (GitHub Actions, GitLab CI) authenticate to cloud providers via OIDC federated IAM roles, eliminating long-lived API keys.</li>\n  <li><strong>2. Sensitive Attribute Redaction:</strong> Declaring <code>sensitive = true</code> on input variables and outputs prevents values from being printed in logs.</li>\n  <li><strong>3. KMS Encrypted Remote State:</strong> The remote backend bucket is encrypted with an AWS KMS Customer Managed Key.</li>\n  <li><strong>4. Native State Encryption (Terraform 1.7+):</strong> Terraform 1.7 introduced native client-side encryption blocks that encrypt state data before transmission to backends.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Modern Terraform 1.7+ Native State Encryption Configuration\nterraform {\n  encryption {\n    key_provider \"aws_kms\" \"state_key\" {\n      kms_key_id = \"arn:aws:kms:us-east-1:123456789012:key/12345678-abcd\"\n      region     = \"us-east-1\"\n    }\n\n    method \"aes_gcm\" \"method\" {\n      keys = key_provider.aws_kms.state_key\n    }\n\n    state {\n      method = method.aes_gcm.method\n      enforced = true\n    }\n  }\n}</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Accidental secret exposure via local-exec: Passing sensitive variables to <code>local-exec</code> shell commands prints the secret in command line execution logs (visible in <code>ps aux</code> on the runner). Avoid passing secrets to local-exec.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Production standard: 'Use IAM Database Authentication for RDS PostgreSQL and MySQL: applications connect using temporary 15-minute IAM auth tokens generated at runtime, completely eliminating database passwords from Terraform code and state.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf_extra_1","level":"senior","question":"How do you manage Terragrunt DRY remote state and multi-account orchestration?","tags":["TERRAFORM","Terragrunt","DRY","MultiAccount","Enterprise"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\"><strong>Terragrunt</strong> is a thin open-source wrapper for Terraform that provides extra tools for keeping configurations DRY (Don't Repeat Yourself), managing remote state configurations automatically, and orchestrating deployments across multiple AWS accounts and environments.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>1. DRY Remote State (generate / remote_state):</strong> A root <code>terragrunt.hcl</code> defines the S3 backend and DynamoDB lock table once. Child modules inherit the backend configuration automatically, interpolating the state key based on directory path.</li>\n  <li><strong>2. DRY Provider Configurations:</strong> Generates standardized <code>provider.tf</code> files with account-specific IAM assume-role blocks automatically.</li>\n  <li><strong>3. Inter-Module Dependency Orchestration:</strong> The <code>dependency</code> block allows one module to read outputs from another (e.g. EKS reading VPC output) and enforces correct execution order across directories during <code>terragrunt run-all apply</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Root terragrunt.hcl (defines remote state once for all child modules)\nremote_state {\n  backend = \"s3\"\n  generate = {\n    path      = \"backend.tf\"\n    if_exists = \"overwrite\"\n  }\n  config = {\n    bucket         = \"corp-terragrunt-state-\\${get_aws_account_id()}\"\n    key            = \"\\${path_relative_to_include()}/terraform.tfstate\"\n    region         = \"us-east-1\"\n    encrypt        = true\n    dynamodb_table = \"terraform-locks\"\n  }\n}\n\n# Child app/terragrunt.hcl referencing VPC dependency:\n# dependency \"vpc\" {\n#   config_path = \"../vpc\"\n# }\n# inputs = {\n#   subnet_ids = dependency.vpc.outputs.private_subnet_ids\n# }</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Running terragrunt run-all apply in massive codebases: Executing <code>terragrunt run-all apply</code> across hundreds of directories concurrently can trigger severe AWS API rate-limiting (429 Throttling) and make debugging plan failures difficult. Limit concurrency with <code>--terragrunt-parallelism</code>.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Architecture decision: 'Terragrunt is the premier solution for multi-account AWS topologies. It enforces complete state separation per environment while eliminating 90% of copy-paste backend and provider boilerplate.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf_extra_2","level":"senior","question":"How do you manage Sentinel Policy-as-Code to mandate encryption on S3 and EBS?","tags":["TERRAFORM","Sentinel","PolicyAsCode","Compliance","Security"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\"><strong>HashiCorp Sentinel</strong> is an embedded Policy-as-Code framework built into HCP Terraform and Terraform Enterprise. It evaluates fine-grained logic against planned infrastructure changes <strong>after terraform plan but before terraform apply</strong>, automatically blocking non-compliant deployments (e.g. unencrypted S3 buckets, open security groups, or unauthorized instance sizes).</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Three Enforcement Levels:</strong></li>\n  <li>• <code>advisory</code>: Prints a warning message on policy violation, but allows apply to proceed.</li>\n  <li>• <code>soft-mandatory</code>: Blocks the apply, but permits an authorized administrator to manually override.</li>\n  <li>• <code>hard-mandatory</code>: Absolute blocking. The apply fails immediately; no override is possible.</li>\n  <li><strong>Evaluation Mechanics:</strong> Sentinel inspects the planned configuration (<code>tfplan</code>), current state (<code>tfstate</code>), and configuration code (<code>tfconfig</code>) using rules written in Sentinel's procedural policy language.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Sentinel Policy: Mandate EBS Volume Encryption across all instances\nimport \"tfplan/v2\" as tfplan\n\n# Filter all aws_ebs_volume resources in the plan\nebs_volumes = filter tfplan.resource_changes as _, rc {\n  rc.type is \"aws_ebs_volume\" and\n  (rc.change.actions contains \"create\" or rc.change.actions contains \"update\")\n}\n\n# Rule: Verify that encrypted attribute is strictly true\nall_ebs_encrypted = rule {\n  all ebs_volumes as _, volume {\n    volume.change.after.encrypted is true\n  }\n}\n\nmain = rule {\n  all_ebs_encrypted\n}</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Ignoring 'update' actions in Sentinel rules: Writing Sentinel policies that only check <code>create</code> actions allows developers to update existing resources and disable encryption later. Always check both <code>create</code> and <code>update</code> actions.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Open-Source alternative: 'If you are using open-source Terraform CLI rather than HCP Terraform, use <strong>Open Policy Agent (OPA)</strong> with <strong>Rego</strong> or <strong>Trivy</strong> to enforce equivalent Policy-as-Code guardrails in your CI/CD pipelines.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf_extra_3","level":"senior","question":"How do you manage custom provider development using HashiCorp Terraform Plugin Framework?","tags":["TERRAFORM","PluginFramework","Go","CustomProvider"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">When an organization needs to manage internal proprietary APIs, bare-metal hardware controllers, or bespoke SaaS platforms using Terraform, engineers develop a <strong>Custom Terraform Provider</strong> in Go using HashiCorp's modern <strong>Terraform Plugin Framework</strong> (replacing the legacy SDKv2).</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Architecture Components:</strong></li>\n  <li>1. <strong>Provider Struct:</strong> Implements <code>provider.Provider</code>, handles authentication credentials, and configures API clients.</li>\n  <li>2. <strong>Resource Struct:</strong> Implements <code>resource.Resource</code> with CRUD lifecycles: <code>Create()</code>, <code>Read()</code>, <code>Update()</code>, <code>Delete()</code>, and <code>ImportState()</code>.</li>\n  <li>3. <strong>Schema Definition:</strong> Strongly typed attribute definitions (String, Int64, Bool, Object) with automatic validation and null handling.</li>\n  <li>4. <strong>gRPC Protocol:</strong> The plugin compiles into a standalone binary that communicates with Terraform Core over local gRPC RPCs.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> GO</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>// Go: Example Custom Resource Create method in Terraform Plugin Framework\nfunc (r *customResource) Create(ctx context.Context, req resource.CreateRequest, resp *resource.CreateResponse) {\n    var plan customResourceModel\n    diags := req.Plan.Get(ctx, &amp;plan)\n    resp.Diagnostics.Append(diags...)\n    if resp.Diagnostics.HasError() {\n        return\n    }\n\n    // Call proprietary internal API client\n    item, err := r.client.CreateItem(ctx, plan.Name.ValueString())\n    if err != nil {\n        resp.Diagnostics.AddError(\"API Client Error\", err.Error())\n        return\n    }\n\n    // Set state with returned ID\n    plan.ID = types.StringValue(item.ID)\n    diags = resp.State.Set(ctx, plan)\n    resp.Diagnostics.Append(diags...)\n}</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Failing to implement the Read method correctly: If the <code>Read()</code> method does not accurately refresh state from the external API or does not handle 404 Not Found by calling <code>resp.State.RemoveResource(ctx)</code>, Terraform will fail to detect external drift or deletion.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Testing requirement: 'Always write unit and acceptance tests using <code>resource.Test()</code> from <code>terraform-plugin-testing</code>. Acceptance tests spin up real test resources against your API and verify state lifecycle handling automatically.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf_extra_4","level":"senior","question":"How do you manage dynamic module iteration using for_each across AWS accounts?","tags":["TERRAFORM","Modules","Iteration","MultiAccount","for_each"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Modern Terraform (v0.13+) supports the <strong>for_each meta-argument on module blocks</strong>. This allows a single module declaration to dynamically stamp out multiple isolated infrastructure stacks (e.g. provisioning 10 VPCs across 10 regions, or provisioning identical baseline security modules across multiple configurations) from a structured map variable.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Map-Driven Module Expansion:</strong> The module block iterates over a map of objects: <code>for_each = var.environments</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Scoping:</strong> Inside the module block, individual attributes are accessed via <code>each.key</code> (the map key) and <code>each.value</code> (the object payload).</li>\n  <li><strong>Output Referencing:</strong> Outputs from iterated modules become a map of outputs indexed by the same keys: <code>module.vpc[\"us-east-1\"].vpc_id</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Safe Add/Remove:</strong> Adding or removing a key in the map creates or destroys strictly that specific module instance without touching sibling instances.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>variable \"vpc_configs\" {\n  type = map(object({\n    cidr_block = string\n    azs        = list(string)\n  }))\n  default = {\n    \"dev\"  = { cidr_block = \"10.0.0.0/16\", azs = [\"us-east-1a\", \"us-east-1b\"] }\n    \"qa\"   = { cidr_block = \"10.1.0.0/16\", azs = [\"us-east-1a\", \"us-east-1b\"] }\n    \"prod\" = { cidr_block = \"10.2.0.0/16\", azs = [\"us-east-1a\", \"us-east-1b\", \"us-east-1c\"] }\n  }\n}\n\n# Dynamically instantiate VPC module for each environment\nmodule \"vpc\" {\n  source   = \"./modules/vpc\"\n  for_each = var.vpc_configs\n\n  environment = each.key\n  cidr_block  = each.value.cidr_block\n  azs         = each.value.azs\n}</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Provider mapping limitations with for_each: You CANNOT dynamically iterate providers inside a module with <code>for_each</code> if each instance requires a different AWS provider alias (e.g. deploying into 5 different AWS accounts dynamically). Providers must be statically declared.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Architecture insight: 'To deploy modules dynamically across multiple AWS accounts, use Terragrunt or directory-based pipelines with dynamic IAM assume-role blocks rather than attempting to pass aliased providers into for_each modules.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf_extra_5","level":"senior","question":"How do you manage safe state migration between S3 backends?","tags":["TERRAFORM","State","Migration","Operations"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">During enterprise cloud migrations, account consolidations, or infrastructure re-architecting, teams must migrate Terraform state files from one remote S3 bucket (or account) to another. Executing state migration safely requires using <strong><code>terraform init -migrate-state</code></strong> with zero downtime and verified cryptographic integrity.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Migration Workflow:</strong></li>\n  <li>1. Pull an offline backup of current state: <code>terraform state pull > state_pre_migration.json</code>.</li>\n  <li>2. Ensure the destination S3 bucket and DynamoDB lock table exist with proper KMS permissions.</li>\n  <li>3. Update the <code>backend \"s3\"</code> block in your HCL code with the new bucket name, key, and region.</li>\n  <li>4. Run <code>terraform init -migrate-state</code>. Terraform automatically detects that the backend configuration changed.</li>\n  <li>5. Terraform prompts: <em>'Do you wish to copy existing state to the new backend?'</em> Answer <strong>yes</strong>.</li>\n  <li>6. Terraform writes state to the new S3 bucket, acquires a lock on the new DynamoDB table, and leaves the old bucket untouched as an emergency rollback fallback.</li>\n  <li>7. Run <code>terraform plan</code> to verify zero diff.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Step 1: Emergency backup of current state\nterraform state pull &gt; backup_state.json\n\n# Step 2: Update backend block in backend.tf to point to new bucket/key\n\n# Step 3: Execute safe automated state migration\nterraform init -migrate-state\n\n# Step 4: Validate that infrastructure diff is completely clean\nterraform plan</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Using -reconfigure instead of -migrate-state: Running <code>terraform init -reconfigure</code> ignores the existing backend state and initializes the new backend as completely empty! If you apply afterward, Terraform will attempt to recreate all resources. Always use <code>-migrate-state</code>.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Rollback strategy: 'The old S3 state bucket is never deleted during migration. If the new backend experiences IAM or region issues, revert the <code>backend.tf</code> configuration to the old bucket and re-run <code>terraform init -migrate-state</code> to instantly restore operations.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf_extra_6","level":"senior","question":"How do you manage preventing drift using continuous scheduled terraform plan pipelines?","tags":["TERRAFORM","Drift","CI/CD","Automation","Observability"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Infrastructure drift is inevitable when team members make out-of-band changes or cloud providers modify default behaviors. Rather than discovering drift during an emergency production deployment, high-performing SRE teams implement <strong>Continuous Drift Detection Pipelines</strong> that run scheduled, non-mutating plans to alert on unmanaged modifications.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Detailed Exit Code Flag:</strong> Running <code>terraform plan -detailed-exitcode -no-color</code> returns exit code <strong>0</strong> (no changes/no drift), <strong>1</strong> (error), or <strong>2</strong> (changes/drift present).</li>\n  <li><strong>Scheduled CI Cron:</strong> A GitHub Actions or GitLab CI cron job executes hourly or daily across all environment directories.</li>\n  <li><strong>Slack/PagerDuty Notification:</strong> If exit code 2 is returned, the pipeline converts the diff into JSON via <code>terraform show -json</code> and posts a structured alert with resource diffs to the on-call channel.</li>\n  <li><strong>Reconciliation Action:</strong> Teams either submit a PR to adopt the change or trigger an automated apply to overwrite the out-of-band change.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Continuous Drift Detection Shell Script for CI/CD Cron\nset +e\nterraform plan -detailed-exitcode -no-color -out=drift.tfplan\nEXIT_CODE=$?\nset -e\n\nif [ $EXIT_CODE -eq 2 ]; then\n  echo \"CRITICAL: Cloud infrastructure drift detected!\"\n  terraform show -no-color drift.tfplan &gt; drift_summary.txt\n  # Post alert to Slack Webhook\n  curl -X POST -H 'Content-type: application/json' \\\n       --data \"{\\\"text\\\":\\\"⚠️ *Terraform Drift Alert in Production*\\n\\`\\`\\`$(head -n 20 drift_summary.txt)\\`\\`\\`\\\"}\" \\\n       \"$SLACK_DRIFT_WEBHOOK\"\n  exit 2\nelif [ $EXIT_CODE -eq 1 ]; then\n  echo \"Terraform plan encountered an execution error.\"\n  exit 1\nfi\necho \"Clean: Infrastructure matches code perfectly.\"</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Triggering automated apply without human review: Automatically running <code>terraform apply</code> when drift is detected can destroy emergency manual hotfixes applied during an active outage. Always notify humans first unless the drift is pre-approved for auto-remediation.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Production cadence: 'Run drift detection on a scheduled nightly cron job (e.g. 2:00 AM). Morning standup begins with reviewing any drift tickets, ensuring 100% fidelity between Git and cloud reality.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf_extra_7","level":"senior","question":"How do you manage Terraform Cloud Run Tasks with Infracost for cloud spend estimation?","tags":["TERRAFORM","FinOps","Infracost","TerraformCloud","CostOptimization"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Cloud cost overruns frequently happen when developers accidentally deploy oversized compute instances or multi-AZ databases. <strong>Infracost</strong> integrated into <strong>Terraform Cloud Run Tasks</strong> (or GitHub Actions PR checks) evaluates the financial cost diff of every proposed plan, posting a detailed monthly cost estimate directly onto pull requests before code is merged.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Run Task Lifecycle Hook:</strong> In HCP Terraform, a Run Task executes in the <strong>Post-Plan</strong> phase, intercepting the generated JSON plan before the apply confirmation step.</li>\n  <li><strong>Pricing Engine:</strong> Infracost parses the plan JSON, queries Cloud Pricing APIs (AWS, Azure, GCP), and calculates monthly baseline and usage costs.</li>\n  <li><strong>PR Comment Decoration:</strong> Automatically comments on pull requests showing: Previous Monthly Spend, New Monthly Spend, and Net Difference (+/- $USD).</li>\n  <li><strong>FinOps Budget Guardrails:</strong> Can be configured to block PR merges if a proposed change increases monthly spend by more than a specified threshold (e.g. +$500/month) without VP approval.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># GitHub Actions Step: Infracost PR Cost Estimation\n- name: Generate Infracost Cost Estimate Baseline\n  run: |\n    infracost breakdown --path=./production \\\n                        --format=json \\\n                        --out-file=/tmp/infracost.json\n\n- name: Post Infracost Comment to GitHub PR\n  run: |\n    infracost comment github --path=/tmp/infracost.json \\\n                             --repo=$GITHUB_REPOSITORY \\\n                             --pull-request=${{ github.event.pull_request.number }} \\\n                             --token=${{ secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN }} \\\n                             --behavior=update</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Usage-based resource blindness: Infracost accurately estimates fixed-cost resources (EC2, RDS, EBS), but usage-based resources (S3 egress, Lambda invocations, CloudWatch logs) require specifying an <code>infracost-usage.yml</code> file with projected traffic numbers.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">FinOps cultural impact: 'Showing engineers the exact dollar cost of their architectural choices inside pull requests reduces unnecessary cloud spend by 25-30% before a single dollar is ever spent in production.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf_extra_8","level":"mid","question":"How do you manage handling null_resource and terraform_data triggers in modern Terraform?","tags":["TERRAFORM","terraform_data","null_resource","Triggers","HCL"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Historically, engineers used the external <strong>null_resource</strong> from <code>hashicorp/null</code> to run arbitrary provisioners or trigger downstream actions when values changed. In Terraform 1.4+, HashiCorp introduced the native built-in <strong>terraform_data</strong> resource, eliminating the need for the external null provider and adding state-tracking capabilities.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>1. Built-in Nature:</strong> <code>terraform_data</code> is built directly into Terraform Core; it requires zero provider downloads during <code>terraform init</code>, making it ideal for air-gapped environments.</li>\n  <li><strong>2. Triggers (triggers_replace):</strong> Replaces the resource whenever the hashed input value changes, triggering dependent lifecycle events.</li>\n  <li><strong>3. State Value Persistence (input):</strong> Stores an arbitrary value in state and provides an <code>output</code> attribute that records the <em>previous</em> value before modification, enabling safe state transitions.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Modern replacement for null_resource: terraform_data (Terraform 1.4+)\nresource \"terraform_data\" \"trigger_cluster_upgrade\" {\n  # Trigger replacement whenever the AMI ID changes\n  triggers_replace = [\n    var.eks_node_ami_id,\n    var.kubernetes_version\n  ]\n\n  input = var.eks_node_ami_id\n}\n\n# Resource that replaces triggered by the terraform_data lifecycle\nresource \"aws_autoscaling_group\" \"workers\" {\n  name = \"worker-asg\"\n\n  lifecycle {\n    replace_triggered_by = [\n      terraform_data.trigger_cluster_upgrade\n    ]\n  }\n}</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Using triggers to run arbitrary scripts on every apply: Using non-deterministic triggers (like <code>timestamp()</code>) causes <code>terraform_data</code> or <code>null_resource</code> to trigger recreation on <em>every single plan and apply</em>, breaking idempotency.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Migration standard: 'Refactor all legacy <code>null_resource</code> instances to built-in <code>terraform_data</code>. It removes external provider dependencies and is natively supported across all Terraform 1.4+ and OpenTofu versions.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf_extra_9","level":"senior","question":"How do you manage sensitive variable masking and secret redaction in state files?","tags":["TERRAFORM","Security","Secrets","Masking","State"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">In Terraform, sensitive values must be protected from accidental leakage in terminal consoles, CI/CD build logs, and unauthorized state access. Modern Terraform combines the <code>sensitive = true</code> HCL attribute with <strong>native state encryption</strong> to ensure confidentiality across both execution and storage.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Terminal & Plan Redaction:</strong> When a variable, local, or output is marked <code>sensitive = true</code>, Terraform's plan formatter replaces the value with <code>(sensitive value)</code> in all CLI outputs, plan files, and error messages.</li>\n  <li><strong>Propagation Rules:</strong> Any expression that derives from a sensitive value automatically inherits the sensitive flag (e.g. <code>local.connection_string = \"db:\\${var.sensitive_pass}\"</code> is marked sensitive).</li>\n  <li><strong>Native State Encryption (Terraform 1.7+):</strong> Configured via an <code>encryption</code> block, allowing state files to be encrypted using AES-GCM with keys managed by AWS KMS, HashiCorp Vault, or GCP KMS before being written to the remote backend.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>variable \"database_password\" {\n  type        = string\n  sensitive   = true\n  description = \"Master DB password\"\n}\n\n# The derived local automatically becomes sensitive\nlocals {\n  db_dsn = \"postgres://admin:\\${var.database_password}@db.corp.internal:5432/main\"\n}\n\n# The output must also declare sensitive = true to prevent compilation error\noutput \"dsn\" {\n  value     = local.db_dsn\n  sensitive = true\n}</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Using sensitive values in resource tags: If you assign a sensitive variable to a resource tag (e.g. <code>tags = { Token = var.secret_token }</code>), some cloud providers do not treat tags as sensitive in their APIs, inadvertently exposing the secret in cloud console GUIs.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Security audit trick: 'To query a sensitive output during emergency operations without exposing it in terminal logs, pipe directly into clipboard: <code>terraform output -raw db_password | pbcopy</code>.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf_extra_10","level":"senior","question":"How do you manage moved blocks for zero-downtime resource refactoring in Terraform 1.1+?","tags":["TERRAFORM","Refactoring","MovedBlocks","ZeroDowntime"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Refactoring large monolithic Terraform codebases into reusable child modules or renaming resources historically required dangerous manual <code>terraform state mv</code> CLI commands. <strong>Moved blocks</strong> (introduced in Terraform 1.1) provide a <strong>declarative, version-controlled</strong> mechanism to record renames directly in HCL code.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Syntax:</strong> <code>moved { from = <OLD_ADDRESS> to = <NEW_ADDRESS> }</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Automatic Reconciliation:</strong> When any developer or CI runner runs <code>terraform plan</code>, Terraform automatically migrates the resource in state without destroying or recreating the physical cloud object.</li>\n  <li><strong>Module Splitting:</strong> Supports moving standalone resources into child modules: <code>moved { from = aws_vpc.main to = module.vpc.aws_vpc.main }</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Array to Map Migration:</strong> Can migrate resources created with <code>count</code> to <code>for_each</code> map keys.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Refactoring: Migrating count-based subnets to for_each map\n# Old code was: resource \"aws_subnet\" \"public\" { count = 2 ... }\n# New code is: resource \"aws_subnet\" \"public\" { for_each = toset([\"us-east-1a\", \"us-east-1b\"]) ... }\n\nmoved {\n  from = aws_subnet.public[0]\n  to   = aws_subnet.public[\"us-east-1a\"]\n}\n\nmoved {\n  from = aws_subnet.public[1]\n  to   = aws_subnet.public[\"us-east-1b\"]\n}</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Prematurely deleting moved blocks: If you merge a moved block and delete it from Git after 2 days, any developer branches or staging environments that haven't applied yet will attempt to destroy the old resource. Retain moved blocks for multiple releases.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">PR review guideline: 'Whenever an engineer submits a PR refactoring modules, review the plan carefully. If the plan shows <code>+ create</code> and <code>- destroy</code>, reject the PR and request a <code>moved</code> block so the plan displays clean state address updates with zero infrastructure changes.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf_extra_16","level":"senior","question":"Explain OpenTofu vs Terraform: the BSL license change, the Linux Foundation fork, and migration compatibility.","tags":["TERRAFORM","OpenTofu","OpenSource","Licensing","Architecture"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">In August 2023, HashiCorp changed the license of Terraform from open-source MPL-2.0 to the proprietary <strong>Business Source License (BSL 1.1)</strong>, restricting competitive commercial usage. In response, the open-source community created <strong>OpenTofu</strong>, a truly open-source fork backed by the Linux Foundation.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>The Fork Point:</strong> OpenTofu forked from Terraform 1.5.7 (the last MPL-licensed release).</li>\n  <li><strong>Compatibility:</strong> OpenTofu is a drop-in replacement for Terraform CLI (supporting identical HCL syntax, provider registries, and S3 backends).</li>\n  <li><strong>OpenTofu Enhancements:</strong> Native state encryption, client-side evaluation improvements, and enhanced provider registries.</li>\n  <li><strong>Migration:</strong> Replacing the <code>terraform</code> binary with <code>tofu</code> works seamlessly for configurations up to 1.5.x.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Drop-in migration to OpenTofu\n# Install OpenTofu\nbrew install opentofu\n\n# Run identical workflow with tofu binary\ntofu init\ntofu plan -out=tfplan\ntofu apply tfplan</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Divergence in Terraform 1.6+: Terraform 1.6+ introduced proprietary features (like HCP-specific testing and cloud integration) that may not be supported identically in OpenTofu. Verify feature compatibility before upgrading.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Industry guidance: 'For enterprises seeking strictly open-source tooling without commercial licensing liabilities, OpenTofu is the Linux Foundation standard.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf_extra_17","level":"senior","question":"How do declarative import blocks in Terraform 1.5+ automate HCL code generation using -generate-config-out?","tags":["TERRAFORM","Import","Automation","Refactoring"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Terraform 1.5 introduced <strong>declarative import blocks</strong> and automated HCL code generation. Instead of manually writing HCL code before running imperative CLI import commands, Terraform can inspect existing cloud resources and write the HCL configuration automatically.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Import Block Syntax:</strong> <code>import { to = <RESOURCE_ADDRESS> id = \"<CLOUD_ID>\" }</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Automated Code Generation:</strong> Running <code>terraform plan -generate-config-out=generated.tf</code> connects to the cloud API, reads resource attributes, and generates the exact HCL resource block.</li>\n  <li><strong>Review & Apply:</strong> The generated file can be reviewed, edited for variable interpolation, and applied cleanly in CI/CD.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Declarative import block in imports.tf\nimport {\n  to = aws_security_group.web_sg\n  id = \"sg-0123456789abcdef0\"\n}\n\n# Run plan to generate matching HCL code automatically:\n# terraform plan -generate-config-out=generated_sg.tf\n\n# Generated generated_sg.tf:\n# resource \"aws_security_group\" \"web_sg\" {\n#   name        = \"web-security-group\"\n#   description = \"Managed by Terraform\"\n#   vpc_id      = \"vpc-12345\"\n# }</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Blindly accepting generated code: Automated HCL generation copies all cloud attributes statically, including default arguments. Clean up the generated code to use variables and locals before committing to Git.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Enterprise adoption: 'Use <code>import</code> blocks with <code>-generate-config-out</code> for brownfield cloud migrations. It slashes the time required to bring hundreds of legacy AWS resources into IaC from weeks to hours.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf_extra_18","level":"senior","question":"Explain custom preconditions and postconditions lifecycle blocks for automated state validation.","tags":["TERRAFORM","Lifecycle","Preconditions","Validation"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Introduced in Terraform 1.2, <strong>preconditions</strong> and <strong>postconditions</strong> allow engineers to declare automated assertions and sanity checks inside resource and data source <code>lifecycle</code> blocks. They prevent bad configurations from executing and catch broken assumptions early.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Precondition:</strong> Evaluated <em>before</em> the resource is created or updated. Validates that prerequisite assumptions hold true (e.g. verifying an imported AMI has required tags).</li>\n  <li><strong>Postcondition:</strong> Evaluated <em>after</em> the resource is created or updated. Inspects the resulting cloud attributes (e.g. verifying that an allocated EBS volume has encryption enabled).</li>\n  <li><strong>Failure Handling:</strong> If the condition expression evaluates to false, Terraform halts immediately and prints a custom <code>error_message</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>resource \"aws_ebs_volume\" \"data\" {\n  availability_zone = \"us-east-1a\"\n  size              = 100\n\n  lifecycle {\n    # Postcondition: Guarantee volume is encrypted before downstream use\n    postcondition {\n      condition     = self.encrypted == true\n      error_message = \"CRITICAL: The EBS volume must be encrypted!\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n\ndata \"aws_ami\" \"base\" {\n  most_recent = true\n  owners      = [\"self\"]\n\n  lifecycle {\n    # Precondition: Ensure selected AMI is tagged with security approval\n    precondition {\n      condition     = self.tags[\"SecApproval\"] == \"Passed\"\n      error_message = \"Selected AMI has not passed security audit!\"\n    }\n  }\n}</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Referencing unknown attributes in preconditions: Preconditions cannot evaluate attributes that will only be known after apply (e.g. referencing an IP address of a server that hasn't been created yet). Use postconditions for computed attributes.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Staff pattern: 'Use postconditions on database clusters and storage volumes to verify that backup retention periods and encryption are active before application pods attempt to connect.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf_extra_19","level":"senior","question":"What are check blocks (Terraform 1.5+) and how do they provide continuous infrastructure assertion monitoring?","tags":["TERRAFORM","CheckBlocks","Validation","Monitoring"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Introduced in Terraform 1.5, <strong>check blocks</strong> allow operators to validate the ongoing operational health and compliance of infrastructure without blocking or failing <code>terraform plan</code> or <code>apply</code> executions. They act as automated continuous assertions.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Non-Blocking Evaluation:</strong> Unlike preconditions (which abort execution), check block failures issue <strong>warnings</strong>, allowing plans and applies to complete.</li>\n  <li><strong>Scoped Data Sources:</strong> Check blocks can contain nested scoped data sources (e.g. <code>http</code> data source) that query external endpoints to verify uptime or SSL certificate validity.</li>\n  <li><strong>Continuous Validation:</strong> When running in HCP Terraform or continuous drift pipelines, check blocks continuously monitor live infrastructure status.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Check block: Verify that production web URL returns HTTP 200\ncheck \"web_health_check\" {\n  data \"http\" \"endpoint\" {\n    url = \"https://\\${aws_lb.web.dns_name}/healthz\"\n  }\n\n  assert {\n    condition     = data.http.endpoint.status_code == 200\n    error_message = \"Health check failed: Endpoint returned \\${data.http.endpoint.status_code}\"\n  }\n}\n\n# Check block: Verify SSL certificate expiration window\ncheck \"tls_cert_expiry\" {\n  assert {\n    condition     = aws_acm_certificate.cert.status == \"ISSUED\"\n    error_message = \"ACM Certificate is not in ISSUED state!\"\n  }\n}</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Using check blocks for critical security blocking: Check blocks only produce non-blocking warnings. If a check must absolutely prevent infrastructure deployment (e.g. unencrypted S3 bucket), use <code>postcondition</code> or Sentinel instead.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Modern SRE workflow: 'Use check blocks to run post-deployment smoke tests (e.g. querying health endpoints) directly inside Terraform. It eliminates the need for separate external bash curl verification scripts in CI/CD.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf_extra_20","level":"senior","question":"Explain ephemeral resources and write-only attributes in modern Terraform for secret lifecycle management.","tags":["TERRAFORM","Ephemeral","Secrets","Security","State"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">A long-standing security vulnerability in Terraform was that any credential generated or queried (e.g. database passwords, temporary vault tokens) was permanently stored in plaintext inside <code>terraform.tfstate</code>. Modern Terraform introduces <strong>Ephemeral Resources</strong> and <strong>Write-Only Attributes</strong> that exist strictly during planning and applying, but are <strong>never written to the state file</strong>.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Ephemeral Lifecycle:</strong> Ephemeral resources are created in memory, passed to target resources during apply, and discarded immediately after the operation completes.</li>\n  <li><strong>Zero State Footprint:</strong> Leaves no trace in <code>terraform.tfstate</code>, preventing credential leakage even if state files are compromised.</li>\n  <li><strong>Common Use Cases:</strong> Generating short-lived Vault tokens, temporary IAM session credentials, and dynamic TLS certificates.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Conceptual Ephemeral Secret Configuration in modern Terraform\n# Ephemeral resource generates short-lived DB credential\nephemeral \"vault_database_secret_backend_role\" \"temp_creds\" {\n  backend = \"database\"\n  name    = \"app-role\"\n}\n\n# Consuming ephemeral secret without writing it to state\nresource \"aws_db_instance\" \"app\" {\n  allocated_storage = 20\n  engine            = \"postgres\"\n  username          = ephemeral.vault_database_secret_backend_role.temp_creds.username\n  password          = ephemeral.vault_database_secret_backend_role.temp_creds.password\n}</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Ephemeral resources cannot be referenced by persistent attributes: If a persistent resource requires reading an ephemeral attribute on subsequent plans, Terraform cannot refresh it because the value was never saved in state.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Security architecture: 'Ephemeral resources eliminate the largest security flaw in Terraform. Pair them with HashiCorp Vault dynamic secrets to achieve zero-secret-in-state compliance.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf_extra_21","level":"senior","question":"How does AWS S3 backend state locking work under the hood using DynamoDB conditional writes?","tags":["TERRAFORM","DynamoDB","Locking","Internals","Concurrency"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">When using the AWS S3 remote backend, Amazon S3 alone cannot provide distributed locking because object storage lacks atomic test-and-set locking primitives. Terraform uses <strong>Amazon DynamoDB</strong> to implement distributed mutex locking via <strong>Conditional Writes (PutItem with attribute_not_exists)</strong>.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>1. Lock Acquisition (PutItem with Condition):</strong></li>\n  <li>• When an apply begins, Terraform executes a DynamoDB <code>PutItem</code> for the primary key <code>LockID = \"<BUCKET>/<KEY>-md5\"</code>.</li>\n  <li>• The request includes a condition expression: <code>attribute_not_exists(LockID)</code>.</li>\n  <li>• If no lock exists, DynamoDB atomically inserts the lock record containing JSON metadata (PID, hostname, who, timestamp, operation).</li>\n  <li>• If a lock already exists, the conditional write fails with a <code>ConditionalCheckFailedException</code>, and Terraform halts with the lock error.</li>\n  <li><strong>2. Lock Release (DeleteItem):</strong></li>\n  <li>• Upon operation completion, Terraform executes a <code>DeleteItem</code> for that <code>LockID</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Inspect active Terraform lock item directly in DynamoDB using AWS CLI\naws dynamodb get-item \\\n    --table-name terraform-locks \\\n    --key '{\"LockID\": {\"S\": \"corp-tf-state-prod/vpc/terraform.tfstate-md5\"}}'\n\n# Manually delete stale lock item if process crashed and force-unlock fails\naws dynamodb delete-item \\\n    --table-name terraform-locks \\\n    --key '{\"LockID\": {\"S\": \"corp-tf-state-prod/vpc/terraform.tfstate-md5\"}}'</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">DynamoDB read/write throttling: In large organizations running hundreds of concurrent CI pipelines, using DynamoDB Provisioned capacity with low limits (e.g. 5 RCU/WCU) can cause pipeline failures due to DynamoDB throttling. Always set billing mode to <code>PAY_PER_REQUEST</code> (On-Demand).</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Deep internal insight: 'The <code>-md5</code> suffix on the LockID in DynamoDB is the MD5 hash of the state path. This ensures that even if state keys are long, the lock key remains unique and bounded.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf_extra_22","level":"senior","question":"How do you structure multi-region AWS infrastructure using provider aliases and cross-region module inheritance?","tags":["TERRAFORM","MultiRegion","Architecture","Providers"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Deploying multi-region cloud infrastructure (e.g. active-active VPCs in <code>us-east-1</code> and <code>eu-west-1</code>, or cross-region S3 replication) requires configuring <strong>Provider Aliases</strong> in the root module and explicitly passing them to child modules via the <code>providers</code> configuration map.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Provider Alias Declaration:</strong> Declare multiple <code>provider \"aws\"</code> blocks with distinct <code>alias</code> labels.</li>\n  <li><strong>Child Module Independence:</strong> Child modules declare required provider types in <code>terraform { required_providers { aws = { ... } } }</code> without hardcoding aliases.</li>\n  <li><strong>Explicit Provider Passing:</strong> The root module invokes the child module multiple times, passing the appropriate regional provider alias via <code>providers = { aws = aws.europe }</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Root Module: Multi-Region Setup\nprovider \"aws\" {\n  alias  = \"us_east\"\n  region = \"us-east-1\"\n}\n\nprovider \"aws\" {\n  alias  = \"eu_west\"\n  region = \"eu-west-1\"\n}\n\n# Deploy VPC in US East\nmodule \"vpc_us\" {\n  source    = \"./modules/vpc\"\n  providers = { aws = aws.us_east }\n  cidr      = \"10.0.0.0/16\"\n}\n\n# Deploy identical VPC in EU West using the same reusable module\nmodule \"vpc_eu\" {\n  source    = \"./modules/vpc\"\n  providers = { aws = aws.eu_west }\n  cidr      = \"10.1.0.0/16\"\n}</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Forgetting provider mappings in child modules: If you call a module without the <code>providers = { ... }</code> map, Terraform falls back to the default unaliased provider, accidentally deploying resources intended for Europe into US East.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Design rule: 'Child modules must NEVER contain provider blocks with hardcoded regions. Reusable child modules should be completely agnostic of region; region assignment is strictly the responsibility of the root module.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf_extra_23","level":"senior","question":"How do you configure cross-account AWS deployments using assume_role blocks in provider definitions?","tags":["TERRAFORM","AWS","IAM","MultiAccount","Security"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">In enterprise multi-account AWS topologies (AWS Organizations), CI/CD runners reside in a central Tools/Deployment account. To provision resources into target accounts (Dev, Staging, Prod), the runner assumes target IAM execution roles using the <strong>assume_role</strong> block inside provider declarations.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Central Runner Identity:</strong> The runner authenticates as a base identity in Account A (e.g. via GitHub Actions OIDC).</li>\n  <li><strong>Target Account Trust:</strong> Target Account B contains an IAM Role (e.g. <code>TerraformExecutionRole</code>) that trusts Account A's IAM principal.</li>\n  <li><strong>assume_role Provider Block:</strong> Configures <code>role_arn</code>, <code>session_name</code>, and optional <code>external_id</code>. Terraform calls AWS STS <code>AssumeRole</code> to generate temporary 1-hour credentials for all API operations.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Provider assuming cross-account IAM role in Production Account\nprovider \"aws\" {\n  region = \"us-east-1\"\n\n  assume_role {\n    role_arn     = \"arn:aws:iam::111122223333:role/TerraformDeploymentRole\"\n    session_name = \"GitHubActions-CI-Deploy\"\n    external_id  = \"CorpSecurityToken-987\"\n  }\n\n  default_tags {\n    tags = { DeployedBy = \"Central-CI\" }\n  }\n}</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">STS Session Expiration during long applies: AWS STS AssumeRole credentials default to a 1-hour session duration. If an apply provisioning 50 resources takes longer than 60 minutes, subsequent API calls fail with <code>ExpiredToken</code>. Increase <code>max_session_duration</code> on the IAM role to 2 or 4 hours.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Security best practice: 'Always configure an <code>external_id</code> in cross-account assume-role relationships. It prevents the 'Confused Deputy' problem in multi-tenant environments.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf_extra_24","level":"senior","question":"What causes dependency cycle errors (Cycle in graph) in Terraform and what architectural patterns break them?","tags":["TERRAFORM","Graph","Cycles","Troubleshooting"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Terraform calculates infrastructure execution order using a Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG). A <strong>Cycle Error</strong> occurs when two or more resources directly or indirectly depend on each other, making it mathematically impossible to determine which resource must be created first.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Common Cycle Scenario:</strong></li>\n  <li>• Security Group A allows ingress from Security Group B.</li>\n  <li>• Security Group B allows ingress from Security Group A.</li>\n  <li>• Declaring ingress inline creates: SG A -> depends on SG B -> depends on SG A (Deadlock!).</li>\n  <li><strong>Breaking Cycles Pattern 1: Standalone Resources:</strong> Decouple the bidirectional relationship using independent sub-resources (e.g. <code>aws_security_group_rule</code>).</li>\n  <li><strong>Breaking Cycles Pattern 2: Decoupled Intermediate Identifiers:</strong> Pass static CIDR blocks or discover resources via data sources rather than circular module outputs.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># SOLUTION: Breaking Security Group cycle using standalone rule resources\nresource \"aws_security_group\" \"frontend\" {\n  name   = \"frontend-sg\"\n  vpc_id = aws_vpc.main.id\n}\n\nresource \"aws_security_group\" \"backend\" {\n  name   = \"backend-sg\"\n  vpc_id = aws_vpc.main.id\n}\n\n# Standalone rule breaks the cycle in the dependency graph\nresource \"aws_security_group_rule\" \"frontend_to_backend\" {\n  type                     = \"ingress\"\n  from_port                = 8080\n  to_port                  = 8080\n  protocol                 = \"tcp\"\n  security_group_id        = aws_security_group.backend.id\n  source_security_group_id = aws_security_group.frontend.id\n}</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Embedding inline ingress rules while also using standalone rule resources: Mixing inline <code>ingress { ... }</code> inside <code>aws_security_group</code> with separate <code>aws_security_group_rule</code> causes continuous flapping drift where the two rule definitions fight each other.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Graph visualization: 'When encountering a cycle error, run <code>terraform graph | dot -Tpng > cycle.png</code>. The visual diagram highlights the cyclic loop in red, showing the exact attribute reference causing the loop.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf_extra_25","level":"senior","question":"Why is using terraform apply -target considered a dangerous anti-pattern in production environments?","tags":["TERRAFORM","Targeting","AntiPatterns","Production"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">The <code>-target</code> flag scopes Terraform execution to a specific resource address. While tempting for quick emergency hotfixes, using <code>-target</code> in production is a severe anti-pattern that causes <strong>state divergence, partial configurations, and broken dependencies</strong>.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Why -target is Dangerous:</strong></li>\n  <li>1. <strong>Bypasses Graph Calculations:</strong> Ignores resources outside the targeted subgraph, leaving upstream or downstream configurations in an unapplied, partial state.</li>\n  <li>2. <strong>Masks State Drift:</strong> Only targeted resources are refreshed; out-of-band drift across the rest of the infrastructure goes undetected.</li>\n  <li>3. <strong>Breaks Shared State:</strong> Teammates running normal plans afterward encounter unexpected diffs and conflicts.</li>\n  <li><strong>Valid Exceptions:</strong> Recovering from broken circular dependencies during initial bootstrapping, or importing legacy resources.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Anti-pattern: Applying only one resource to bypass a failure elsewhere\n# terraform apply -target=aws_instance.app\n\n# Proper SRE Fix: Clean up or isolate the failing resource in code\n# Then run full plan and apply to guarantee global consistency\nterraform plan -out=tfplan\nterraform apply tfplan</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Applying a targeted plan in CI/CD: Never script <code>-target</code> in automated pipelines. It guarantees your production state will drift from declared HCL code.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Staff guidance: 'If your codebase is so large that you feel compelled to use <code>-target</code> to avoid 30-minute plans, the real problem is architectural: split your monolithic state file into smaller, modular state partitions.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf_extra_26","level":"senior","question":"How do you write automated integration tests using the native Terraform Test Framework (.tftest.hcl)?","tags":["TERRAFORM","Testing","TerraformTest","QualityAssurance"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Introduced in Terraform 1.6, the native <strong>Terraform Test Framework</strong> enables automated unit and integration testing of Terraform modules using declarative <code>.tftest.hcl</code> files, replacing third-party Go testing tools like Terratest for standard validation.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Test File Convention:</strong> Placed in the <code>tests/</code> directory with the extension <code>.tftest.hcl</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Command Types:</strong></li>\n  <li>• <code>command = plan</code>: Fast unit testing (verifies variable validations and HCL logic without provisioning real cloud resources).</li>\n  <li>• <code>command = apply</code>: Full integration testing (provisions real cloud resources, verifies attributes with assertions, and destroys resources upon test completion).</li>\n  <li><strong>Assertions:</strong> Uses <code>assert { condition = ... error_message = ... }</code> to validate outputs and resource states.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># File: tests/vpc_test.tftest.hcl\n# Unit Test: Verify that subnet count matches input\nrun \"verify_subnet_allocation\" {\n  command = plan\n\n  variables {\n    cidr_block = \"10.0.0.0/16\"\n    subnets    = [\"10.0.1.0/24\", \"10.0.2.0/24\"]\n  }\n\n  assert {\n    condition     = length(aws_subnet.private) == 2\n    error_message = \"Expected exactly 2 private subnets to be planned\"\n  }\n\n  assert {\n    condition     = aws_vpc.main.cidr_block == \"10.0.0.0/16\"\n    error_message = \"VPC CIDR block does not match input\"\n  }\n}</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Cost of apply tests: Running <code>command = apply</code> in CI runs creates real cloud resources. If test teardown fails due to timeouts, orphaned cloud resources can run up bills. Set tight timeout limits.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">CI/CD testing pyramid: 'Run <code>tofu test</code> or <code>terraform test</code> with <code>command = plan</code> on every pull request for instant unit testing. Run full <code>apply</code> integration tests on nightly schedule against a dedicated sandbox account.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf_extra_27","level":"mid","question":"How do you implement custom validation rules and error messages on input variables using HCL regex and logic?","tags":["TERRAFORM","Variables","Validation","Regex"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Custom validation rules inside <code>variable</code> blocks allow module authors to enforce architectural standards, naming conventions, and security policies early in the <strong>plan phase</strong>, preventing invalid configurations from reaching cloud APIs.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Syntax:</strong> <code>validation { condition = <BOOLEAN_EXPR> error_message = \"...\" }</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Multiple Validation Blocks:</strong> A single variable can have multiple <code>validation</code> blocks testing distinct rules.</li>\n  <li><strong>Regex Matching:</strong> Use <code>can(regex(\"^...\", var.name))</code> for pattern enforcement.</li>\n  <li><strong>HCL Functions:</strong> Combine with <code>contains()</code>, <code>startswith()</code>, <code>endswith()</code>, and <code>length()</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code>variable \"environment\" {\n  type        = string\n  description = \"Target deployment environment\"\n\n  validation {\n    condition     = contains([\"dev\", \"stage\", \"prod\"], var.environment)\n    error_message = \"Environment must be one of: 'dev', 'stage', or 'prod'.\"\n  }\n}\n\nvariable \"project_name\" {\n  type        = string\n  description = \"Resource naming convention prefix\"\n\n  validation {\n    condition     = can(regex(\"^[a-z0-9-]+$\", var.project_name)) &amp;&amp; length(var.project_name) &lt;= 24\n    error_message = \"Project name must be lowercase alphanumeric with hyphens, max 24 chars.\"\n  }\n}</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Omitting the can() wrapper with regex: If <code>regex()</code> does not find a match, it raises an error rather than returning false! Always wrap regex calls in <code>can(regex(...))</code> to safely return boolean values.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">API design: 'Provide precise, actionable <code>error_message</code> strings explaining exactly what values are permitted. A clear error saves developers hours of debugging.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf_extra_28","level":"mid","question":"How do you use the templatefile() function to render dynamic EC2 cloud-init user_data scripts and Kubernetes YAML?","tags":["TERRAFORM","HCL","Templates","CloudInit"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">The <strong>templatefile()</strong> built-in function reads a file from disk and renders its contents as a template, interpolating HCL variables dynamically. It replaced the deprecated <code>template_file</code> data source, providing cleaner syntax and zero state bloat.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Syntax:</strong> <code>templatefile(path, vars_map)</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>File System Path:</strong> Resolved relative to the current module using <code>path.module</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Template Syntax:</strong> Supports <code>${variable}</code> interpolation, <code>%{ for x in list } ... %{ endfor }</code> loops, and <code>%{ if bool } ... %{ endif }</code> conditionals.</li>\n  <li><strong>No Provider Dependencies:</strong> Built directly into Terraform Core.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Rendering an EC2 user_data script dynamically\nresource \"aws_instance\" \"app\" {\n  ami           = \"ami-0c55b159cbfafe1f0\"\n  instance_type = \"t3.medium\"\n\n  user_data = templatefile(\"\\${path.module}/templates/bootstrap.sh.tftpl\", {\n    cluster_name  = var.cluster_name\n    admin_email   = var.admin_email\n    enable_docker = true\n  })\n}\n\n# In templates/bootstrap.sh.tftpl:\n# #!/bin/bash\n# echo \"Bootstrapping \\${cluster_name}...\"\n# %{ if enable_docker }\n# systemctl enable --now docker\n# %{ endif }</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Indentation issues in rendered YAML: When rendering nested Kubernetes YAML or JSON with <code>templatefile()</code>, whitespace errors can invalidate the target schema. Use the <code>indent()</code> function to preserve formatting.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Extension convention: 'Name template files with the <code>.tftpl</code> extension (e.g. <code>bootstrap.sh.tftpl</code>). Modern IDEs recognize this extension and provide syntax highlighting for both the underlying language and HCL template tags.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf_extra_29","level":"senior","question":"Compare terraform_remote_state data source vs AWS SSM Parameter Store for cross-stack output sharing.","tags":["TERRAFORM","Architecture","RemoteState","SSM","Decoupling"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">When decomposing monolithic Terraform repositories into modular stacks (e.g. Foundation Network stack vs. EKS Compute stack), downstream stacks need access to upstream outputs (like VPC ID). The two primary integration patterns are <strong>terraform_remote_state</strong> and <strong>Cloud Parameter Stores (AWS SSM)</strong>.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>1. terraform_remote_state Data Source:</strong></li>\n  <li>• Directly reads the raw <code>.tfstate</code> JSON file of another stack from S3.</li>\n  <li>• <em>Pros:</em> Strongly typed, built-in, easy to wire up.</li>\n  <li>• <em>Cons:</em> High coupling. Downstream stacks require S3 read access and KMS decrypt permissions to the entire upstream state file, exposing upstream database passwords and secrets.</li>\n  <li><strong>2. AWS SSM Parameter Store / Secrets Manager (Recommended):</strong></li>\n  <li>• Upstream stack writes specific outputs as SSM parameters (e.g. <code>/platform/network/vpc_id</code>).</li>\n  <li>• Downstream stack reads the parameter using <code>data \"aws_ssm_parameter\"</code>.</li>\n  <li>• <em>Pros:</em> Loose coupling, granular IAM permissions per parameter, zero state file cross-access.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># In Upstream Network Stack: Publish output to SSM Parameter Store\nresource \"aws_ssm_parameter\" \"vpc_id\" {\n  name  = \"/infrastructure/production/vpc_id\"\n  type  = \"String\"\n  value = aws_vpc.main.id\n}\n\n# In Downstream EKS Stack: Consume VPC ID dynamically without touching network state\ndata \"aws_ssm_parameter\" \"vpc_id\" {\n  name = \"/infrastructure/production/vpc_id\"\n}\n\nresource \"aws_eks_cluster\" \"main\" {\n  name     = \"prod-eks\"\n  vpc_config {\n    subnet_ids = var.subnet_ids\n  }\n}</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Security exposure with terraform_remote_state: Granting application developers IAM read access to the network or core state file allows them to read unencrypted database master passwords stored in that state. Avoid <code>terraform_remote_state</code> across organizational boundaries.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Staff SRE doctrine: 'Use AWS SSM Parameter Store or Consul as the contract interface between infrastructure layers. It enforces clean abstraction boundaries and prevents security state leakage between teams.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf_extra_30","level":"senior","question":"How do you build immutable infrastructure pipelines combining HashiCorp Packer AMIs and Terraform Auto Scaling Launch Templates?","tags":["TERRAFORM","Packer","ImmutableInfrastructure","ASG","AMI"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\"><strong>Immutable Infrastructure</strong> mandates that servers are never modified, patched, or updated in-place. When software updates or OS patches are required, a new golden machine image (AMI) is baked using <strong>HashiCorp Packer</strong> and deployed by updating <strong>Terraform Launch Templates</strong> and initiating an <strong>Auto Scaling Group Instance Refresh</strong>.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>1. Image Baking (Packer):</strong> Packer builds an AMI containing the OS, security patches, runtime binaries, and monitoring agents. Tags the AMI with Git commit SHA and version.</li>\n  <li><strong>2. Terraform Discovery:</strong> Terraform queries the golden AMI via <code>data \"aws_ami\"</code> filter.</li>\n  <li><strong>3. Launch Template Update:</strong> Updates <code>aws_launch_template</code> with the new AMI ID.</li>\n  <li><strong>4. Instance Refresh (Zero-Downtime Rollout):</strong> The <code>instance_refresh</code> block in <code>aws_autoscaling_group</code> performs a rolling replacement of EC2 instances, honoring health checks.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Discover latest golden AMI baked by Packer pipeline\ndata \"aws_ami\" \"golden_image\" {\n  most_recent = true\n  owners      = [\"self\"]\n  filter {\n    name   = \"name\"\n    values = [\"corp-golden-linux-2026-*\"]\n  }\n}\n\nresource \"aws_launch_template\" \"app\" {\n  name_prefix   = \"app-lt-\"\n  image_id      = data.aws_ami.golden_image.id\n  instance_type = \"m6i.large\"\n}\n\nresource \"aws_autoscaling_group\" \"app_asg\" {\n  name                = \"app-asg\"\n  vpc_zone_identifier = var.subnet_ids\n\n  launch_template {\n    id      = aws_launch_template.app.id\n    version = \"$Latest\"\n  }\n\n  # Automated zero-downtime rolling replacement of instances\n  instance_refresh {\n    strategy = \"Rolling\"\n    preferences {\n      min_healthy_percentage = 50\n      instance_warmup        = 300\n    }\n  }\n}</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Forgetting instance_refresh preferences: If <code>min_healthy_percentage</code> is omitted, an instance refresh can terminate instances faster than new instances can pass target group health checks, causing intermittent 502 Bad Gateway outages.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Disaster recovery benefit: 'Immutable infrastructure eliminates configuration drift permanently. If a node exhibits degraded performance, never SSH in to debug—terminate it and let the ASG spin up an identical clean instance from the golden image.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf_extra_31","level":"senior","question":"How do you manage Kubernetes resources and Helm releases natively using Terraform Helm and Kubernetes providers?","tags":["TERRAFORM","Kubernetes","Helm","GitOps","EKS"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Terraform can provision not only the cloud Kubernetes cluster (EKS, GKE, AKS) but also the internal cluster software (ingress controllers, cert-manager, Datadog agents) using the <strong>Helm Provider</strong> and <strong>Kubernetes Provider</strong>.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Authentication Handshake:</strong> The provider authenticates dynamically via AWS IAM authentication token (<code>aws_eks_cluster_auth</code>) or client certificates.</li>\n  <li><strong>helm_release Resource:</strong> Deploys and manages Helm charts with values injection, version constraints, and rollback automation.</li>\n  <li><strong>Wait & Health Verification:</strong> <code>wait = true</code> ensures Terraform pauses until all pods and services are healthy before proceeding.</li>\n  <li><strong>Ordering:</strong> Explicitly depends on the EKS cluster and node group resources.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Dynamic Kubernetes/Helm provider configuration for EKS\ndata \"aws_eks_cluster_auth\" \"cluster\" {\n  name = aws_eks_cluster.main.name\n}\n\nprovider \"helm\" {\n  kubernetes {\n    host                   = aws_eks_cluster.main.endpoint\n    cluster_ca_certificate = base64decode(aws_eks_cluster.main.certificate_authority[0].data)\n    token                  = data.aws_eks_cluster_auth.cluster.token\n  }\n}\n\n# Deploy Ingress NGINX via Helm Release\nresource \"helm_release\" \"ingress_nginx\" {\n  name             = \"ingress-nginx\"\n  repository       = \"https://kubernetes.github.io/ingress-nginx\"\n  chart            = \"ingress-nginx\"\n  version          = \"4.9.0\"\n  namespace        = \"ingress-nginx\"\n  create_namespace = true\n  wait             = true\n\n  set {\n    name  = \"controller.service.annotations.service\\.beta\\.kubernetes\\.io/aws-load-balancer-type\"\n    value = \"nlb\"\n  }\n}</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Single-plan cluster and Helm chicken-and-egg bug: Attempting to create an EKS cluster AND deploy Helm charts in the exact same Terraform apply run can fail because provider authentication requires cluster endpoints that do not exist during initial planning. Best practice is to separate cluster creation from add-on deployment.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Architecture boundary: 'Use Terraform to install platform foundational components (Cilium CNI, AWS Load Balancer Controller, ArgoCD). Hand off business application microservices to ArgoCD or Flux for GitOps application delivery.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf_extra_32","level":"senior","question":"How do you orchestrate Blue/Green and Canary infrastructure deployments using Route 53 weighted records in Terraform?","tags":["TERRAFORM","BlueGreen","Route53","Deployment","ZeroDowntime"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Blue/Green deployments mitigate deployment risk by running two identical infrastructure environments (Blue = active, Green = idle). Terraform orchestrates traffic shifting between environments using <strong>AWS Route 53 Weighted Routing Policies</strong>, enabling incremental Canary rollouts (e.g. 10% to Green, 90% to Blue) and instant zero-downtime rollbacks.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Two Independent Stacks:</strong> Deploy Blue and Green clusters with distinct load balancer endpoints.</li>\n  <li><strong>Weighted Routing Records:</strong> Create two Route 53 DNS records sharing the identical domain name (<code>api.company.com</code>) with weighted routing policies.</li>\n  <li><strong>Traffic Dialing:</strong> Adjust the <code>weight</code> parameter via variable inputs: start with Blue=100/Green=0, shift to Blue=90/Green=10, and finally Blue=0/Green=100.</li>\n  <li><strong>Instant Rollback:</strong> Setting Green weight back to 0 immediately redirects 100% of user traffic to Blue.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Route 53 Weighted Record: Blue (Current Active)\nresource \"aws_route53_record\" \"blue\" {\n  zone_id = var.dns_zone_id\n  name    = \"api.enterprise.com\"\n  type    = \"A\"\n\n  weighted_routing_policy {\n    weight = var.blue_weight # e.g. 90\n  }\n  set_identifier = \"blue-environment\"\n\n  alias {\n    name                   = aws_lb.blue_alb.dns_name\n    zone_id                = aws_lb.blue_alb.zone_id\n    evaluate_target_health = true\n  }\n}\n\n# Route 53 Weighted Record: Green (New Canary)\nresource \"aws_route53_record\" \"green\" {\n  zone_id = var.dns_zone_id\n  name    = \"api.enterprise.com\"\n  type    = \"A\"\n\n  weighted_routing_policy {\n    weight = var.green_weight # e.g. 10\n  }\n  set_identifier = \"green-environment\"\n\n  alias {\n    name                   = aws_lb.green_alb.dns_name\n    zone_id                = aws_lb.green_alb.zone_id\n    evaluate_target_health = true\n  }\n}</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Client DNS caching delays: Even with weighted records, client operating systems and ISP recursive resolvers cache DNS records for the duration of the TTL. Keep DNS TTL low (60 seconds) prior to canary deployments.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Modern alternative: 'For microservices running on EKS, handle Canary traffic shifting at Layer 7 using Envoy, Istio, or AWS App Mesh rather than DNS. L7 proxies shift traffic per-request with zero DNS cache delays.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf_extra_33","level":"senior","question":"How do you coordinate relational database schema migrations alongside Terraform infrastructure provisioning?","tags":["TERRAFORM","Database","Migrations","Flyway","Liquibase"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Managing relational database infrastructure (AWS RDS, Aurora) with Terraform is declarative, but executing database schema migrations (DDL/DML like adding columns or creating tables) is stateful and transactional. SREs establish a strict boundary between <strong>infrastructure provisioning</strong> (Terraform) and <strong>schema migrations</strong> (Flyway, Liquibase, or ORM migration runners).</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>The Separation of Concerns:</strong></li>\n  <li>• <em>Terraform Scope:</em> Provisions the RDS cluster, subnet groups, KMS keys, security groups, and outputs the connection endpoint.</li>\n  <li>• <em>Application Pipeline Scope:</em> A separate pipeline runner executes schema migration tools (e.g. Flyway) inside the VPC.</li>\n  <li><strong>Why NOT to Run Migrations in Terraform local-exec:</strong></li>\n  <li>• Breaks idempotency: Terraform plans cannot preview DDL schema changes.</li>\n  <li>• Security flaw: Requires the Terraform runner to have network access to private database subnets and plaintext database master credentials.</li>\n  <li>• Rollback failures: If an apply fails, Terraform cannot roll back database DDL changes.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Recommended Pipeline Sequence:\n# Step 1: Terraform provisions/updates RDS Aurora infrastructure\nterraform apply -input=false tfplan\n\n# Step 2: Extract endpoint safely\nDB_HOST=$(terraform output -raw rds_endpoint)\n\n# Step 3: Run Flyway schema migration container inside VPC via ECS task\naws ecs run-task \\\n    --cluster cicd-tasks \\\n    --overrides '{\"containerOverrides\": [{\"name\": \"flyway\", \"environment\": [{\"name\": \"DB_URL\", \"value\": \"jdbc:postgresql://db/main\"}]}]}'</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Triggering migrations on every Terraform run: Never use <code>local-exec</code> to trigger database migrations on RDS resource blocks. Any non-destructive update (e.g. tag change) will re-run migrations, causing locking or migration failures.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Expand-Contract Pattern: 'Always design database schema migrations using the Expand-Contract pattern: 1) Add new nullable column in migration; 2) Deploy new app version that writes to both columns; 3) Backfill old data; 4) Remove old column. This guarantees zero-downtime rollouts.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf_extra_34","level":"senior","question":"What are the blast radius trade-offs between Monorepo vs Multi-repo state partitioning in enterprise Terraform?","tags":["TERRAFORM","Architecture","Monorepo","BlastRadius","Scale"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">As cloud infrastructure scales from 10 to 1,000 resources, organizations must partition their Terraform code and state files to minimize the <strong>Blast Radius</strong> (the potential damage a single misconfiguration can inflict) and optimize execution performance.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>1. Monolithic State (Anti-Pattern):</strong></li>\n  <li>• Single state file containing networking, databases, IAM, and applications.</li>\n  <li>• <em>Flaws:</em> Plan times exceed 30 minutes; concurrent lock contention halts team velocity; a typo in an app resource can destroy the primary VPC.</li>\n  <li><strong>2. Partitioned State by Layer (Best Practice):</strong></li>\n  <li>• Partitioned into discrete state boundaries: <code>01-network</code>, <code>02-security</code>, <code>03-data</code>, <code>04-apps</code>.</li>\n  <li>• <em>Benefits:</em> Network state changes once a quarter; app state changes 20 times a day. App deploys cannot touch the VPC.</li>\n  <li><strong>3. Monorepo vs. Multi-Repo Code Organization:</strong></li>\n  <li>• <em>Monorepo (Terragrunt / Atlantis):</em> Single Git repository containing isolated directories with independent state keys. Easy code discovery and atomic refactoring.</li>\n  <li>• <em>Multi-Repo:</em> Distinct Git repos per team. Enforces strict RBAC and isolated CI pipelines.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Recommended Enterprise Layered State Architecture\n# repo-root/\n# ├── live/\n# │   ├── prod/\n# │   │   ├── 01-vpc/           # State: prod-vpc.tfstate (Infrequent changes)\n# │   │   ├── 02-security/      # State: prod-security.tfstate\n# │   │   ├── 03-databases/     # State: prod-databases.tfstate (Critical)\n# │   │   └── 04-workloads/     # State: prod-apps.tfstate (Frequent changes)</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Over-partitioning into micro-states: Creating a separate state file for every single EC2 instance introduces excessive cross-stack parameter lookups and orchestration complexity. Balance blast radius by logical service domain.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">SRE golden rule: 'State boundaries should mirror organizational team boundaries and change frequencies: Network and Security teams own foundation layers; Application teams own workload layers.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf_extra_35","level":"senior","question":"Compare Atlantis, Spacelift, and HCP Terraform for Pull Request-driven GitOps infrastructure automation.","tags":["TERRAFORM","GitOps","Atlantis","Spacelift","CI/CD"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Executing Terraform from local developer laptops is an operational hazard. Organizations enforce <strong>PR-driven GitOps workflows</strong> using platforms like <strong>Atlantis</strong> (open-source), <strong>Spacelift</strong> (enterprise orchestration), and <strong>HCP Terraform</strong> (HashiCorp managed).</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>1. Atlantis (Open-Source Self-Hosted):</strong></li>\n  <li>• Operates via GitHub/GitLab PR comments (e.g. <code>atlantis plan</code>, <code>atlantis apply</code>).</li>\n  <li>• Automatically locks the PR branch to prevent conflicting runs.</li>\n  <li>• Free, self-hosted, lightweight; lacks native RBAC, compliance policies, or audit reporting.</li>\n  <li><strong>2. Spacelift (Enterprise Multi-IaC):</strong></li>\n  <li>• Sophisticated orchestration supporting Terraform, OpenTofu, Pulumi, CloudFormation, and Ansible.</li>\n  <li>• Native Open Policy Agent (OPA) policy gates for plan evaluation, approvals, and run dependencies.</li>\n  <li>• Self-hosted private workers and audit logging.</li>\n  <li><strong>3. HCP Terraform (HashiCorp Managed):</strong></li>\n  <li>• Deep native Terraform integration, Sentinel policy enforcement, private module registry, and cost estimation.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Atlantis Configuration File: atlantis.yaml\nversion: 3\nprojects:\n- name: production-vpc\n  dir: live/prod/vpc\n  workflow: default\n  autoplan:\n    when_modified: [\"*.tf\", \"*.tfvars\"]\n    enabled: true\n  apply_requirements: [approved, mergeable]\n\nworkflows:\n  default:\n    plan:\n      steps:\n      - run: terraform fmt -check\n      - init\n      - plan:\n          extra_args: [\"-no-color\"]</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Security risks of PR comment triggers: In public or multi-tenant repositories, allowing untrusted PRs to trigger <code>atlantis apply</code> can execute malicious <code>local-exec</code> code on your internal build runners. Restrict plan/apply triggers to authorized team members.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Evaluation metric: 'Choose Atlantis for a lightweight, free, self-hosted developer experience. Choose Spacelift or HCP Terraform for enterprise governance, SOC2 compliance audit trails, and automated Policy-as-Code gates.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf_extra_36","level":"senior","question":"How do you manage air-gapped and offline enterprise environments using terraform providers mirror?","tags":["TERRAFORM","AirGapped","Security","Mirrors","Enterprise"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">In highly regulated environments (defense, banking, healthcare), production build networks are completely isolated from the public Internet (air-gapped). Because <code>terraform init</code> cannot reach <code>registry.terraform.io</code> to download plugins, teams build a <strong>Local Provider Mirror</strong> using <strong>terraform providers mirror</strong>.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>1. Mirror Construction (Connected Machine):</strong></li>\n  <li>• Run <code>terraform providers mirror <TARGET_DIRECTORY></code> on an internet-connected workstation.</li>\n  <li>• Terraform downloads all required provider binaries, archives, and cryptographic checksums for specified OS architectures (e.g. <code>linux_amd64</code>).</li>\n  <li><strong>2. Secure Ingestion:</strong> The mirrored directory is scanned for malware and transferred to the air-gapped network.</li>\n  <li><strong>3. CLI Configuration (Network Mirroring):</strong></li>\n  <li>• Configure <code>~/.terraformrc</code> or <code>/etc/terraform.rc</code> with a <code>provider_installation</code> block.</li>\n  <li>• Directs Terraform to source providers strictly from an internal filesystem directory or private Artifactory/Nexus mirror, completely disabling public registry lookups.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Step 1: Pre-download providers on connected machine\n# terraform providers mirror -platform=linux_amd64 /opt/terraform-plugins/\n\n# Step 2: Configure /etc/terraform.rc in the air-gapped environment\nprovider_installation {\n  filesystem_mirror {\n    path    = \"/opt/terraform-plugins\"\n    include = [\"registry.terraform.io/*/*\"]\n  }\n  direct {\n    exclude = [\"*/*\"] # Block all public internet registry lookups\n  }\n}</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Missing platform architecture binaries: If you run <code>terraform providers mirror</code> on a macOS developer machine (darwin_arm64) without passing <code>-platform=linux_amd64</code>, the downloaded mirror will lack Linux binaries, causing CI runners in the air-gapped network to fail during <code>init</code>.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Enterprise deployment: 'Host an internal provider mirror using JFrog Artifactory or Sonatype Nexus. Configure <code>network_mirror</code> in <code>.terraformrc</code> to allow internal developers and CI agents to download verified providers over HTTPS.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf_extra_37","level":"senior","question":"How do you safely rotate AWS KMS Customer Managed Keys (CMKs) without breaking Terraform-managed encrypted resources?","tags":["TERRAFORM","AWS","KMS","Encryption","KeyRotation"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Security compliance standards (PCI-DSS, SOC2, HIPAA) mandate periodic cryptographic key rotation. In AWS KMS, rotating keys incorrectly can render encrypted EBS volumes, S3 buckets, and RDS databases completely inaccessible. SREs utilize <strong>Automated KMS Key Rotation</strong> in Terraform to rotate key material safely with zero resource downtime.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Automated KMS Key Rotation (Recommended):</strong></li>\n  <li>• Setting <code>enable_key_rotation = true</code> instructs AWS KMS to generate new backing cryptographic key material automatically every year (or 90-360 days in modern AWS).</li>\n  <li>• The key ARN and Key ID remain identical.</li>\n  <li>• Old key material is preserved indefinitely by AWS KMS to decrypt historical data.</li>\n  <li>• Zero changes in Terraform state; zero resource recreation.</li>\n  <li><strong>Manual Key Rotation (Creating a New Key):</strong></li>\n  <li>• Required when changing key algorithms or importing external keys.</li>\n  <li>• Requires creating Key B, updating resource <code>kms_key_id</code> attributes, and re-encrypting data before decommissioning Key A.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Automated annual KMS key rotation configuration in Terraform\nresource \"aws_kms_key\" \"storage_key\" {\n  description             = \"KMS Key for RDS &amp; EBS storage encryption\"\n  deletion_window_in_days = 30\n  enable_key_rotation     = true # Automatically rotates backing key material\n\n  policy = jsonencode({\n    Version = \"2012-10-17\"\n    Statement = [\n      {\n        Sid    = \"Enable IAM User Permissions\"\n        Effect = \"Allow\"\n        Principal = {\n          AWS = \"arn:aws:iam::\\${data.aws_caller_identity.current.account_id}:root\"\n        }\n        Action   = \"kms:*\"\n        Resource = \"*\"\n      }\n    ]\n  })\n}</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Deleting old KMS keys after manual rotation: If you manually rotate by creating Key B and delete Key A, any existing S3 object versions, EBS snapshots, or RDS database backups encrypted with Key A will become permanently undecryptable. Retain historical keys in disabled state rather than deleting them.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Compliance simplicity: 'Always set <code>enable_key_rotation = true</code> on <code>aws_kms_key</code> resources. It satisfies SOC2 compliance audits automatically while eliminating 100% of the manual operational overhead of re-encrypting datasets.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf_extra_38","level":"mid","question":"How do you use dynamic blocks and for_each to construct complex AWS IAM policies and Security Groups cleanly?","tags":["TERRAFORM","DynamicBlocks","IAM","SecurityGroups","HCL"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Writing repetitive static blocks inside resources like <code>aws_iam_policy_document</code> and <code>aws_security_group</code> leads to code bloat and maintenance headaches. Combining <strong>dynamic blocks</strong> with structured maps enables clean, dynamic policy construction based on input variables.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Dynamic Statements in IAM:</strong> The <code>aws_iam_policy_document</code> data source supports <code>dynamic \"statement\"</code>, allowing engineers to generate least-privilege IAM statements dynamically from a list of authorized actions and ARNs.</li>\n  <li><strong>Dynamic Ingress in Security Groups:</strong> Iterates over a map of port definitions, generating matching ingress rules with custom descriptions and CIDRs.</li>\n  <li><strong>Iterator Scoping:</strong> Accesses current attributes via <code><iterator_name>.value</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Dynamic IAM Policy Document generation\nvariable \"bucket_permissions\" {\n  type = list(object({\n    actions   = list(string)\n    resources = list(string)\n  }))\n  default = [\n    { actions = [\"s3:GetObject\", \"s3:ListBucket\"], resources = [\"arn:aws:s3:::app-data\", \"arn:aws:s3:::app-data/*\"] },\n    { actions = [\"s3:PutObject\"], resources = [\"arn:aws:s3:::app-uploads/*\"] }\n  ]\n}\n\ndata \"aws_iam_policy_document\" \"dynamic_s3_policy\" {\n  dynamic \"statement\" {\n    for_each = var.bucket_permissions\n    content {\n      effect    = \"Allow\"\n      actions   = statement.value.actions\n      resources = statement.value.resources\n    }\n  }\n}</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Generating wildcard '*' statements accidentally: Ensure default variable values in dynamic blocks do not permit wildcard actions (<code>*</code>), which could grant unintentional administrator privileges.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Readability balance: 'Use dynamic blocks when configuration is legitimately variable per environment. If a policy has fixed, unchanging rules, write them statically—explicit code is easier to audit than overly dynamic code.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf_extra_39","level":"mid","question":"How do you tune Terraform concurrency using the -parallelism flag to prevent cloud API rate-limiting (429 Too Many Requests)?","tags":["TERRAFORM","Performance","Parallelism","RateLimiting"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">By default, Terraform walks the dependency graph with a concurrency limit of <strong>10 concurrent operations</strong> (<code>-parallelism=10</code>). In massive configurations or environments with strict API rate limits (e.g. AWS IAM, Cloudflare, GitHub APIs), 10 parallel API threads can trigger <strong>HTTP 429 Too Many Requests (Throttling)</strong>, causing applies to fail.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>The -parallelism Flag:</strong> Limits the number of concurrent resource operations executed during graph walking.</li>\n  <li><strong>Lowering Parallelism:</strong> Setting <code>-parallelism=3</code> or <code>-parallelism=5</code> reduces concurrent API requests, allowing cloud API token buckets to refill and eliminating throttling errors.</li>\n  <li><strong>Increasing Parallelism:</strong> Setting <code>-parallelism=30</code> on high-bandwidth, independent resources (e.g. provisioning 100 DNS records across Cloudflare) can slash apply runtimes from 10 minutes to 45 seconds.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Reduce concurrency to avoid AWS IAM API throttling\nterraform apply -parallelism=3 tfplan\n\n# Increase concurrency for rapid provisioning of independent resources\nterraform apply -parallelism=25 tfplan\n\n# Configure provider-level retry backoff (AWS Provider)\n# provider \"aws\" {\n#   max_retries = 10\n# }</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Setting parallelism too high on AWS IAM: AWS IAM has notoriously strict global account-level API rate limits. Running <code>-parallelism=20</code> on IAM role attachments will trigger immediate throttling and failed deployments. Keep IAM parallelism low.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Production tuning: 'If you see <code>Throttling: Rate exceeded</code> or <code>RequestLimitExceeded</code> in your Terraform logs, do not immediately assume cloud outage. Reduce parallelism to <code>-parallelism=3</code> and set <code>max_retries = 10</code> in the provider block.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf_extra_40","level":"mid","question":"How do you troubleshoot and recover from state lock acquisition errors (Error acquiring the state lock) safely?","tags":["TERRAFORM","Troubleshooting","Locking","Operations"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">When executing a command against a remote backend, Terraform halts if another process holds the state lock, printing: <code>Error: Error acquiring the state lock</code>. If a CI pipeline crashes unexpectedly or an engineer kills a local terminal process abruptly, the lock can remain orphaned in the backend.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Lock Error Output:</strong> Displays the Lock ID (UUID), timestamp, who acquired it, and operation type.</li>\n  <li><strong>Triage Procedure:</strong></li>\n  <li>1. Check the hostname and PID listed in the lock error. Verify whether the process is genuinely running (e.g. active CI build).</li>\n  <li>2. If the process is confirmed dead or crashed, release the lock using <code>terraform force-unlock <LOCK_ID></code>.</li>\n  <li>3. Terraform contacts DynamoDB or remote backend API and forcefully removes the lock item.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Example Error Output:\n# Error: Error acquiring the state lock\n# Lock Info:\n#   ID:        e0123456-789a-bcde-f012-3456789abcde\n#   Path:      corp-tf-state/vpc/terraform.tfstate\n#   Who:       runner@github-actions-01\n\n# Force-unlock the orphaned lock using the UUID from the error\nterraform force-unlock e0123456-789a-bcde-f012-3456789abcde</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Force-unlocking while a job is actively applying: Force-unlocking a running job allows a second job to start applying, guaranteeing race-condition state file corruption. Always verify the runner is stopped before unlocking.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Communication standard: 'In team environments, always post in the engineering channel before running <code>force-unlock</code>: *'Force-unlocking VPC state lock e0123456... caused by crashed build #142.'* This prevents accidental collision with teammates.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf_extra_41","level":"senior","question":"How do you restore a corrupted remote state file from Amazon S3 object versioning history in disaster recovery?","tags":["TERRAFORM","DisasterRecovery","State","S3","Operations"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">A corrupted or accidentally truncated Terraform state file can halt all company cloud operations. With <strong>S3 Object Versioning</strong> enabled on the remote state bucket, SREs can roll back the state file to any prior point in time within minutes.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Disaster Recovery Procedure:</strong></li>\n  <li>1. <strong>List Historical Versions:</strong> Query S3 object version history using AWS CLI to locate the last known healthy state version ID.</li>\n  <li>2. <strong>Download Healthy Version:</strong> Download that specific version ID locally.</li>\n  <li>3. <strong>Validate JSON:</strong> Verify JSON formatting with <code>jq</code> and inspect the serial number.</li>\n  <li>4. <strong>Push Restored State:</strong> Use <code>terraform state push</code> to overwrite the corrupted remote state.</li>\n  <li>5. <strong>Verify with Plan:</strong> Run <code>terraform plan</code> to confirm zero unexpected diffs.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> BASH</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Step 1: List all historical versions of the state file in S3\naws s3api list-object-versions \\\n    --bucket corp-tf-state-prod \\\n    --prefix vpc/terraform.tfstate \\\n    --query 'Versions[0:5].[VersionId, LastModified, Size]'\n\n# Step 2: Download the previous healthy version\naws s3api get-object \\\n    --bucket corp-tf-state-prod \\\n    --key vpc/terraform.tfstate \\\n    --version-id \"q1w2e3r4t5y6u7i8VersionID\" \\\n    healthy_state.json\n\n# Step 3: Verify JSON integrity\njq .serial healthy_state.json\n\n# Step 4: Force-push healthy state back to remote backend\nterraform state push -force healthy_state.json</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Forgetting the -force flag on state push: Because the restored state file has a lower serial number than the corrupted state, <code>terraform state push</code> will reject it by default. The <code>-force</code> flag is required to override the serial check during DR recovery.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">DR preparation: 'Automate state backups: set up an AWS Backup vault or cross-region S3 replication bucket that automatically replicates state versions to a secondary AWS account with Object Lock enabled for ransomware protection.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf_extra_42","level":"senior","question":"How do you manage zero-downtime major version database upgrades for AWS RDS PostgreSQL using Terraform read replicas?","tags":["TERRAFORM","RDS","ZeroDowntime","Database","Upgrades"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Performing a major version upgrade on AWS RDS PostgreSQL (e.g. from v13 to v15) in-place requires taking the primary database offline, inducing 15 to 45 minutes of downtime. SREs execute <strong>Near-Zero-Downtime Major Upgrades</strong> using Terraform by provisioning an RDS Read Replica, upgrading the replica, and promoting it to primary with minimal cutover time.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>The 4-Step Cutover Sequence:</strong></li>\n  <li>1. <strong>Create Replica in Terraform:</strong> Provision an <code>aws_db_instance</code> with <code>replicate_source_db = aws_db_instance.primary.identifier</code>.</li>\n  <li>2. <strong>Upgrade Replica:</strong> Upgrade the read replica's <code>engine_version</code> to PostgreSQL 15 while the primary database continues serving live traffic.</li>\n  <li>3. <strong>Promote Replica:</strong> During a brief maintenance window (1-2 minutes), pause writes on the app, wait for replication lag to hit 0, promote the replica to standalone primary, and re-point application DNS/SSM endpoints.</li>\n  <li>4. <strong>Update Terraform State:</strong> Remove <code>replicate_source_db</code> in code and update state so the promoted instance becomes the primary.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># Step 1: Provision Read Replica in Terraform\nresource \"aws_db_instance\" \"replica\" {\n  identifier          = \"prod-db-v15-replica\"\n  replicate_source_db = aws_db_instance.primary.identifier\n  instance_class      = \"db.r6g.xlarge\"\n  auto_minor_version_upgrade = false\n\n  # Once promoted during maintenance cutover:\n  # Remove replicate_source_db argument and adopt as primary!\n}</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Replication Lag during promotion: Promoting a read replica before replication lag reaches 0 seconds causes data loss (un-replicated transactions are discarded). Always verify <code>ReplicaLag == 0</code> in CloudWatch before promoting.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Blue/Green RDS Deployment: 'In modern AWS (RDS PostgreSQL >= 11), use <strong>AWS RDS Blue/Green Deployments</strong> (supported via <code>aws_rds_cluster</code> and native AWS features). It creates a synchronized staging environment and switches over in under 1 minute with zero data loss.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf_extra_43","level":"senior","question":"How do you orchestrate multi-cloud resources (AWS VPC + Cloudflare CDN + Datadog monitors) within unified modules?","tags":["TERRAFORM","MultiCloud","Cloudflare","Datadog","Architecture"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Modern enterprise applications do not live in AWS alone: traffic enters through Cloudflare CDN, runs on AWS ECS/EKS compute, and is monitored by Datadog. Terraform's provider model allows engineers to stitch together resources across diverse cloud and SaaS APIs within a single unified module.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Multi-Provider Architecture:</strong> Declare required providers in <code>terraform { required_providers { ... } }</code> for AWS, Cloudflare, and Datadog.</li>\n  <li><strong>Cross-Provider Chaining:</strong></li>\n  <li>1. Provision AWS Application Load Balancer.</li>\n  <li>2. Pass ALB DNS hostname directly to Cloudflare DNS CNAME record (<code>cloudflare_record</code>).</li>\n  <li>3. Pass ALB ARN directly to Datadog monitor resource (<code>datadog_monitor</code>) to alert on 5xx error rates.</li>\n  <li><strong>Unified Destruction:</strong> When decommissioning a service, <code>terraform destroy</code> cleanly deletes the Datadog alert, Cloudflare DNS record, and AWS infrastructure in proper reverse order.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> HCL</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># 1. Provision AWS ALB\nresource \"aws_lb\" \"web\" {\n  name               = \"prod-alb\"\n  internal           = false\n  load_balancer_type = \"application\"\n  subnets            = var.public_subnets\n}\n\n# 2. Automatically point Cloudflare DNS to AWS ALB\nresource \"cloudflare_record\" \"api\" {\n  zone_id = var.cloudflare_zone_id\n  name    = \"api\"\n  value   = aws_lb.web.dns_name\n  type    = \"CNAME\"\n  proxied = true\n}\n\n# 3. Automatically create Datadog Monitor for ALB 5xx Spikes\nresource \"datadog_monitor\" \"alb_5xx\" {\n  name    = \"High 5xx errors on \\${aws_lb.web.name}\"\n  type    = \"metric alert\"\n  query   = \"avg(last_5m):sum:aws.applicationelb.httpcode_elb_5xx{loadbalancer:\\${aws_lb.web.arn_suffix}} &gt; 10\"\n  message = \"ALB 5xx error rate exceeded threshold! @pagerduty-platform\"\n}</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Provider credential coupling: If Datadog API credentials fail or expire, <code>terraform plan</code> will fail completely, even if you only intended to update an AWS tag. Use granular provider alias passing to minimize credential coupling.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Staff pattern: 'Co-locating monitoring (Datadog/PagerDuty) alongside infrastructure code ensures monitoring is NEVER an afterthought. A service cannot be provisioned without its alert rules and dashboards automatically existing.'</p>\n</div>"},{"id":"tf_extra_44","level":"senior","question":"How do you build an end-to-end GitOps CI/CD pipeline for Terraform with automated security scanning, cost estimation, and approval gates?","tags":["TERRAFORM","GitOps","CI/CD","Security","FinOps","Enterprise"],"source":"DevOps Terraform Vault","answer":"<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-cube\"></i> 1. Executive Concept & \"The Why\"</h4>\n<p class=\"answer-p\">Enterprise Terraform requires a robust, automated GitOps delivery pipeline that eliminates manual laptop applies, guarantees reproducible plans, validates security compliance, estimates costs, and enforces dual-control approval gates before touching production cloud infrastructure.</p>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-gears\"></i> 2. Deep-Dive Internal Mechanics</h4>\n<ul class=\"answer-ul\">\n  <li><strong>Stage 1: Lint & Static Validation (PR Open):</strong></li>\n  <li>• <code>terraform fmt -check</code> (formatting).</li>\n  <li>• <code>tflint</code> (cloud provider syntax and deprecated attributes).</li>\n  <li>• <code>trivy config .</code> (security misconfigurations).</li>\n  <li><strong>Stage 2: Speculative Dry Run & FinOps (PR Open):</strong></li>\n  <li>• Authenticate via GitHub Actions OIDC (short-lived IAM role).</li>\n  <li>• <code>terraform plan -out=tfplan</code>.</li>\n  <li>• Run Infracost to post cost estimate comment on PR.</li>\n  <li>• Run Conftest (OPA) / Sentinel to enforce compliance guardrails.</li>\n  <li><strong>Stage 3: Peer Review & Approval Gate:</strong> Minimum 2 senior SRE approvals required; PR merged to <code>main</code>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Stage 4: Automated Deployment (On Merge):</strong></li>\n  <li>• CI runner downloads approved <code>tfplan</code>.</li>\n  <li>• Executes <code>terraform apply tfplan</code> with exclusive DynamoDB state lock.</li>\n  <li>• Posts deployment telemetry to Slack/Teams.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4 class=\"answer-h4\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-code\"></i> 3. Production Manifest / Diagnostic Commands</h4>\n<div class=\"code-block-wrapper\">\n  <div class=\"code-block-header\">\n    <span class=\"code-lang\"><i class=\"fa-brands fa-linux\"></i> YAML</span>\n    <button class=\"btn-copy-code\" type=\"button\"><i class=\"fa-regular fa-copy\"></i> Copy</button>\n  </div>\n  <pre><code># GitHub Actions Production Terraform GitOps Pipeline\nname: \"Terraform Production Deployment\"\n\non:\n  push:\n    branches: [ \"main\" ]\n  pull_request:\n    branches: [ \"main\" ]\n\npermissions:\n  id-token: write  # Mandatory for AWS OIDC authentication\n  contents: read\n  pull-requests: write\n\njobs:\n  validate_and_plan:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n    - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n\n    - name: Configure AWS Credentials via OIDC\n      uses: aws-actions/configure-aws-credentials@v4\n      with:\n        role-to-assume: \"arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/GitHubActionsTerraform\"\n        aws-region: \"us-east-1\"\n\n    - uses: hashicorp/setup-terraform@v3\n\n    - run: terraform fmt -check -recursive\n    - run: terraform init -input=false\n    - run: terraform validate\n\n    - name: Run Terraform Plan\n      run: terraform plan -input=false -out=tfplan\n\n    - name: Run Infracost\n      if: github.event_name == 'pull_request'\n      run: |\n        infracost breakdown --path=./ --format=json --out-file=/tmp/infracost.json\n        infracost comment github --path=/tmp/infracost.json --repo=$GITHUB_REPOSITORY --pull-request=${{ github.event.pull_request.number }} --token=${{ secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN }}\n\n    - name: Terraform Apply (Main Branch Only)\n      if: github.ref == 'refs/heads/main' &amp;&amp; github.event_name == 'push'\n      run: terraform apply -input=false tfplan</code></pre>\n</div><div class=\"gotcha-box\">\n  <div class=\"gotcha-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation\"></i> Production Pitfalls & Failure Modes</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Running plan on merge rather than reusing the pre-approved PR plan: If cloud drift occurs between PR merge and deploy, running a new plan on main might execute unreviewed changes. Apply the saved plan file or require branches to be up to date before merge.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"interview-tip-box\">\n  <div class=\"tip-title\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-lightbulb\"></i> Staff SRE Golden Answer / Interview Delivery Tip</div>\n  <p class=\"answer-p\">Executive takeaway: 'The golden rule of enterprise Infrastructure as Code: No human has write access to the cloud production console. All changes flow through Git, pass security and cost gates, and are deployed exclusively by automated OIDC CI/CD runners.'</p>\n</div>"}];
